This '''glossary of nautical terms''' is an alphabetical listing of terms and expressions connected with [[ships]], [[shipping]], [[seamanship]] and [[navigation]] on water (mostly though not necessarily on the sea). Some remain current, while many date from the 17th to 19th centuries. The word '''nautical''' derives from the Latin ''nauticus'', from Greek ''nautikos'', from ''nautēs'': "sailor", from ''naus'': "ship".

Further information on nautical terminology may also be found at [[Nautical metaphors in English]], and additional military terms are listed in the [[Multiservice tactical brevity code]] article. Terms used in other fields associated with bodies of water can be found at [[Glossary of fishery terms]], [[Glossary of underwater diving terminology]], [[Glossary of rowing terms]], and [[Glossary of meteorology]].

{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

== A ==
{{glossary}}
{{term|AAW}}
{{defn|An acronym for [[anti-aircraft warfare]].}}

{{term|aback}}
{{defn|(of a {{nautical term|sail}}) Filled by the wind on the opposite side to the one normally used to move the vessel forward. On a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ship, any of the square sails can be {{gli|brace|braced}} round to be aback, the purpose of which may be to reduce speed (such as when a {{nautical term|ship-of-the-line}} is keeping station with others), to {{gli|heave to}}, or to assist moving the ship's head through the eye of the wind when {{nautical term|tacking}}. A sudden shift in the wind can also cause a square-rigged vessel to be unintentionally "caught aback" with all sails aback. This is a dangerous situation that risks serious damage. In a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} vessel, a {{gli|headsail}} is backed either by hauling it across with the {{nautical term|weather sheet}} or by tacking without releasing the sheet. It is used to heave to or to assist with tacking.{{sfn|Harland|1984|pp=181–188, 222, 225–228}}<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/> See also ''{{gli|back and fill}}''.}}

{{term|abaft}}
{{defn|Toward the {{nautical term|stern}}, relative to some object (e.g. "abaft the cockpit").{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=3}} }}

{{term|abaft the beam}}
{{defn|Farther {{gli|aft}} than the {{gli|beam}}; a relative bearing of greater than 90 degrees from the {{gli|bow}}; e.g. "two points abaft the beam, starboard side" would describe "an object lying 22.5 degrees toward the rear of the ship, as measured clockwise from a perpendicular line from the right side, center, of the ship, toward the horizon".<ref name=navalslang>{{cite web |url=http://www.rtna.ac.th/article/Navy%20Slang%20Dictionary%20-%20pdf%20Version.pdf |title=Naval Slang Dictionary |access-date=February 19, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102005455/http://www.rtna.ac.th/article/Navy%20Slang%20Dictionary%20-%20pdf%20Version.pdf |archive-date=November 2, 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref>}}

{{term|abandon ship}}
{{defn|An imperative to leave the vessel immediately, usually in the face of some imminent overwhelming danger.<ref name=hope>{{cite web |url=http://www.splashmaritime.com.au/Marops/Dictionary.pdf |first1=Ranger |last1=Hope |title=A Seaman's Dictionary |year=2007 |publisher=Hope Ranger |access-date=February 15, 2014 }}</ref> It is an order issued by the {{nautical term|master}} or a delegated person in command, and must be a verbal order. It is usually the last resort after all other mitigating actions have failed or become impossible, when destruction or loss of the ship is imminent, and is customarily followed by a command to "man the [[Lifeboat (shipboard)|lifeboat]]s" or life rafts.<ref name=hope/><ref name=layton>{{cite web|url=http://www.seaspirit.ru/pdf/Dictionary%20of%20Nautical%20words%20and%20terms.pdf |title=Dictionary of Nautical Words and Terms: 8000 Definitions in Navigation, Seamanship, Rigging, Meteorology, Astronomy, Naval Architecture, Average, Ship Economics, Hydrography, Cargo Stowage, Marine Engineering, Ice Terminology, Buoyage, Yachting, etc. |last1=Layton |first1=C.W.T. |last2=Clissold |first2=Peter |last3=Miller |first3=A.G.W. |location=Glasgow |publisher=Brown, Son & Ferguson, Ltd., Nautical publishers |year=1994 |edition=Revised Fourth |access-date=February 23, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140227162441/http://www.seaspirit.ru/pdf/Dictionary%20of%20Nautical%20words%20and%20terms.pdf |archive-date=February 27, 2014 }}</ref>}}

{{term|abeam}}
{{defn|On the {{gli|beam}}; a relative bearing at right angles to the ship's {{gli|keel}}; e.g. describing an object located at a bearing of 90 degrees ([[port and starboard|starboard]]) or 270 degrees ([[port and starboard|port]]) as measured clockwise from the ship's {{gli|bow}}.<ref>{{citation |title=Hydrographic Dictionary |url=http://hd.iho.int/en/index.php/abeam |contribution=Abeam |publisher=International Hydrographic Organization |access-date=2016-10-17  |archive-date=2016-10-18  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018210430/http://hd.iho.int/en/index.php/abeam |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{term|[[able seaman]] (AB)}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''able-bodied seaman'''.}}
A merchant seaman qualified to perform all routine duties on a vessel, or a junior rank in some navies.}}

{{term|aboard}}
{{defn|On or in a vessel. Synonymous with "on board". See also ''{{gli|close aboard}}''.}}

{{term|about}}
{{defn|To change the {{gli|course}} of a ship by {{nautical term|tacking}}. "Ready about" is the order to prepare for tacking.{{sfnp|Hamersly|1884|p= [https://archive.org/details/navalencyclopedia00hamerich/page/13/ 13]}} }}

{{term|above board}}
{{defn|On or above the {{gli|deck}}; in plain view; not hiding anything. Pirates would often hide their crews below decks, thereby creating the false impression that an encounter with another ship was a casual matter of chance rather than a planned assault.}}

{{term|above-water hull}}
{{defn|The section of a vessel's {{gli|hull}} above the {{nautical term|waterline}}; the visible part of a ship. See also ''{{nautical term|topsides}}''.}}

{{term|absentee pennant}}
{{defn|A special pennant flown to indicate the absence of a ship's commanding officer, admiral, chief-of-staff, or an officer whose flag is nonetheless flying (a division, squadron, or [[flotilla]] commander).}}

{{term|term=absolute bearing|content=[[Bearing (angle)#Absolute|absolute bearing]]}}
{{defn|The bearing of an object in relation to north: either ''true bearing'', using the geographical or [[true north]], or ''magnetic bearing'', using [[magnetic declination|magnetic north]]. See also ''{{gli|bearing}}'' and [[Bearing (angle)#Relative|relative bearing]].}}

{{term|term=accommodation ladder|content=[[accommodation ladder]]}}
{{defn|A portable flight of steps down a ship's side.}}

{{term|term=accommodation ship|content=[[Barracks ship|accommodation ship]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''accommodation hull'''.}}
A ship or {{gli|hull}} used as housing, generally when there is a lack of quarters available ashore. An operational ship can be used, but more commonly a hull modified for accommodation is used.}}

{{term|Act of Grace}}
{{term|Act of Pardon|multi=y}}
{{defn|A letter from a state or power authorising action by a [[privateer]]. See also [[letter of marque]].}}

{{term|action stations}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|battle stations}}.}} }}

{{term|term=admiral|content=[[admiral]]}}
{{defn|A senior naval officer of flag rank. In ascending order of seniority in the [[Royal Navy]]: rear admiral, vice admiral, admiral, (until about 2001, when all British five-star ranks were discontinued) [[admiral of the fleet]], and the [[Lord High Admiral of the United Kingdom|Lord High Admiral]]. In the [[US Navy]]: rear admiral (lower half), rear admiral, vice admiral, admiral, and (unused since the [[Second World War]]) fleet admiral.}}

{{term|term=admiralty|content=[[Admiralty (navy)|admiralty]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A high naval authority in charge of a state's {{nautical term|navy}} or a major territorial component. In the [[Royal Navy]] (UK), the [[Admiralty Board (United Kingdom)|Board of Admiralty]], executing the office of the [[Lord High Admiral of the United Kingdom|Lord High Admiral]], promulgates naval law in the form of [[King's Regulations]] and admiralty instructions.}}
{{defn|no=2|Another name for [[admiralty law]].}}

{{term|term=admiralty law|content=[[admiralty law]]}}
{{defn|The body of law that deals with maritime cases. In the UK, it is administered by the [[Admiralty Court]], a special court within the [[King's Bench Division]] of the [[High Court of Justice]]. The Admiralty Court is now in the Rolls Building.}}

{{term|adrift}}
{{defn|no=1|Afloat and unattached in any way to the shore or seabed, but not {{nautical term|underway}}. When referring to a vessel, it implies that the vessel is not being or able to be controlled and therefore goes where the wind and current take her; a vessel in this condition may also be described as "loose from her moorings" or "out of place".{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=5}} }}
{{defn|no=2|Any gear not fastened down or stored properly.}}
{{defn|no=3|Any person or thing that is misplaced or missing. When applied to a member of the Navy or Marine Corps, such a person is said to be "absent without leave" (AWOL) or, in US Navy and US Marine Corps terminology, is guilty of an "unauthorized absence" (UA).<ref name=covey>{{cite web |url=http://www.hmsrichmond.org/348test.htm |title=1775 Naval Terms and Slang |publisher=H.M.S. Richmond |author=Commander A. Covey-Crump R.N. |year=2000 |access-date=February 19, 2014}}</ref>}}

{{term|advance note}}
{{defn|A note for one month's wages issued to a sailor on his signing a {{nautical term|ship's articles}}.}}

{{term|adviso}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|aviso}}.}} }}

{{term|afloat}}
{{defn|no=1|(of a vessel) Floating freely (not {{gli|aground}} or sunk). The term may also be used more generally of any floating object or person.}}
{{defn|no=2|In service, even if not currently {{nautical term|underway}}, but not stranded, crewless, in repair, or under construction (e.g. "the company has 10 ships afloat").}}

{{term|afore}}
{{defn|no=1|In, on, or toward the {{gli|fore}} or front of a vessel.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=5}}}}
{{defn|no=2|In front of a vessel.}}

{{term|term=aft|content=[[aft]]}}
{{defn|Toward the {{nautical term|stern}} or rear of a vessel. To the purist, this is an adverb (e.g. "he walked aft") with the corresponding adjective being {{nautical term|after}} (e.g. "the after mooring cleat"){{sfn|Mayne|2000|p=6}}}}

{{term|after}}
{{defn|Towards the stern. This is an adjective, unlike {{nautical term|aft}} which is an adverb.{{sfn|Mayne|2000|p=6}}}}

{{term|afterbrow}}
{{defn|On larger ships, a secondary {{gli|gangway}} rigged in the area {{gli|abaft}} of {{nautical term|midship}}. On some military vessels, such as US naval vessels, enlisted personnel below E-7 board the ship at the afterbrow; officers and [[Chief petty officer (United States)|CPO]]/[[Senior chief petty officer|SCPO]]/[[Master chief petty officer|MCPO]] board the ship at the brow.<ref>{{cite web|title=Module 1 – Basics of Shipboard life|url=http://fas.org/irp/doddir/navy/rfs/part01.htm#_Toc448392384|website=Ready-for-Sea Modular Course & Handbook|publisher=Federation of American Scientists|access-date=19 July 2015}}{{Dead link|date=July 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>}}

{{term|term=aftercastle|content=[[aftercastle]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''sterncastle'''.}}
A {{nautical term|stern}} structure behind the {{nautical term|mizzenmast}} and above the {{nautical term|transom}} on large sailing ships, much larger but less common than a {{gli|forecastle}}. The aftercastle houses the captain's cabin and sometimes other cabins and is topped by the {{nautical term|poop deck}}.}}

{{term|afterdeck}}
{{defn|The portion of the {{gli|deck}} that is {{gli|abaft}} of {{gli|amidships}}. }}

{{term|afternoon watch}}
{{defn|The 1200–1600 {{nautical term|watch}}.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=5}} }}

{{term|aground}}
{{defn|Resting on or touching the ground or land, or the bottom of a body of water (either unintentionally or deliberately, such as in a drying harbour), as opposed to {{gli|afloat}}.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=5}} }}

{{term|ahead}}
{{defn|Forward of the {{gli|bow}}.}}

{{term|ahoo}}
{{defn|An adjective indicating an un-seamanlike state of disarray.  Used to describe something awry, askew, or even round but out of true.<ref name="oed_ahoo">{{cite web |title=ahoo |url=https://www.oed.com/view/Entry/62892681?redirectedFrom=ahoo#eid |website=Oxford English Dictionary |publisher=Oxford University Press |access-date=7 June 2023}}</ref>  E.g. "What a sad lubberly display is that craft underway! They're still dragging their fenders in the surf, and their sails are all ahoo!".}}

{{term|ahoy}}
{{defn|A cry to draw attention. Used to hail a boat or a ship, e.g. "boat ahoy".{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=5}} }}

{{term|ahull}}
{{defn|no=1|Lying {{gli|broadside}} to the sea.}}
{{defn|no=2|To ride out a storm with no sails and {{gli|helm}} held to {{gli|leeward}}.}}

{{term|term=aid to navigation|content=[[navigational aid|aid to navigation]] (ATON) {{anchor|navigational aid}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''navigational aid'''.}}
1.&nbsp;Any device external to a vessel or aircraft specifically intended to assist navigators in determining their position or safe course, or to warn them of dangers or obstructions to navigation.}}
{{defn|no=2|Any sort of marker that aids a traveler in navigation, especially with regard to nautical or aviation travel. Such aids commonly include [[lighthouse]]s, [[buoy]]s, {{gli|fog signal|fog signals}}, and {{gli|day beacon|day beacons}}.}}

{{term|term=aircraft carrier|content=[[aircraft carrier]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also simply '''carrier'''.}}
A [[warship]] designed with a primary mission of deploying and recovering aircraft while at sea, thereby acting as a seagoing airbase. Since 1918, the term generally has been limited to a warship with an extensive [[flight deck]] designed to operate conventional fixed-wing aircraft. In US Navy slang, also called a "flat top" or a "bird farm".}}

{{term|air draft}}
{{term|air draught|multi=y}}
{{defn|maximum vertical extent of any part of the vessel above the water surface. Clearance required for passing under a bridge.<ref name="uscg">{{cite press release|url=http://www.uscg.mil/hq/cg5/cg545/alerts/0914.pdf| publisher=United States Coast Guard Inspections and Compliance Directorate|title=Marine Safety Alert 090-14: Air Draft is Critical!|date=2014-09-09|access-date=2015-02-15}}</ref> }}

{{term|term=aka|content=[[Polynesian multihull terminology|aka]]}}
{{defn|Structural section of a vessel that joins together the hulls of a [[multihull]]ed vessel.}}

{{term|alee}}
{{defn|no=1|On the {{gli|lee}} side of a ship.}}
{{defn|no=2|To {{gli|leeward}}.}}

{{term|all hands}}
{{defn|A ship's entire company, including both officers and enlisted personnel.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=6}} }}

{{term|all night in}}
{{defn|Having no night {{nautical term|watch|watches}}.}}

{{term|all standing}}
{{defn|Bringing a person or thing up short; i.e. an unforeseen and sudden stop.<ref name=covey/>{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=6}} }}

{{term|allision}}
{{defn|The impact of a moving vessel with a stationary object (not submerged), such as a bridge abutment or dolphin, pier or wharf, or another vessel made fast to a pier or wharf. More than incidental contact is required. The vessel is said to "allide" with the fixed object and is considered at fault. Contrast ''{{gli|collision}}''.}}

{{term|aloft}}
{{defn|no=1|In the {{nautical term|rigging}} of a sailing ship.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=6}}}}
{{defn|no=2|Above the ship's uppermost solid structure.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=6}}}}
{{defn|no=3|Overhead or high above.}}

{{term|alongside}}
{{defn|By the side of a ship or pier.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=6}} }}

{{term|term=ama|content=[[Polynesian multihull terminology|ama]]}}
{{defn|A secondary hull or float attached to the primary hull of a vessel for stability, or the hulls of a modern [[catamaran]].}}

{{term|amidships}}
{{defn|no=1|A position half way along the length of a ship or boat.<ref name="Read 2014a">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014a |c= A |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/a-16192}}</ref> }}
{{defn|no=2|A position half way between the port and starboard sides of a ship or boat, as in "helm amidships", when the rudder is in line with the keel.<ref name="Read 2014a"/> }}

{{term|term=ammunition ship|content=[[ammunition ship]]}}
{{defn|A naval {{gli|auxiliary ship}} specifically configured to carry ammunition, usually for combatant ships and aircraft.}}

{{term|term=amphibious warfare ship|content=[[amphibious warfare ship]]}}
{{defn|A wide variety of {{nautical term|warship}}s designed to land and support {{nautical term|marine}}s and ground forces in an [[Amphibious warfare|amphibious assault]]. Amphibious warfare ships range in size and capability from large oceangoing ships, some with full-length {{gli|flight deck}}s, to small vessels designed to land personnel and equipment directly onto a beach.}}

{{term|term=anchor|content=[[anchor]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Any object designed to prevent or slow the drift of a ship, attached to the ship by a {{gli|line}} or {{gli|anchor chain|chain}}; usually a metal, hook, or plough-like object designed to grip the solid seabed under the body of water. See also ''{{nautical term|sea anchor}}''.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=8}} }}
{{defn|no=2|To deploy an anchor (e.g. "she anchored offshore").{{sfn|Palmer|1975|pp=8–9}}}}

{{term|anchor ball}}
{{defn|A round, black shape hoisted in the forepart of a vessel to show that it is anchored.}}

{{term|1=anchor bolster}}
{{defn|1=A metal fabrication or casting on a vessel through which the anchor chain passes, and against which the anchor rests when fully housed. Also called bolster plate.}}

{{term|anchor buoy}}
{{defn|A small {{gli|buoy}} secured to a {{gli|line}} attached to the crown of an {{gli|anchor}}. The line allows the anchor to be unhooked from an obstruction, such as a rock or another vessel's anchor cable, so preventing raising the anchor in the normal way.<ref name="Read 2014a"/> }}

{{term|term=anchor chain|content= anchor chain {{anchor|anchor cable}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''anchor cable'''.}}
A chain connecting a ship to an {{gli|anchor}}.}}

{{term|anchor detail}}
{{defn|A group of men who handle ground tackle when the ship is anchoring or getting under way.}}

{{term|anchor home}}
{{defn|When the anchor is secured aboard the ship for sea; i.e. when it is not deployed. Typically rests just outside the {{gli|hawsepipe}} on the outer side of the {{gli|hull}}, at the {{gli|bow}} of a vessel.}}

{{term|anchor light}}
{{defn|A white light displayed by a ship to indicate that it is at anchor. Two such lights are displayed by a ship over {{convert|150|ft|m}} in length.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=9}} }}

{{term|anchor rode}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also simply '''rode'''.}}
The anchor line, rope, or cable connecting the {{gli|anchor chain}} to the vessel.}}

{{term|anchor scope}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also simply '''scope'''.}}
The ratio of the length of anchor rode to the water depth.}}

{{term|anchor sentinel}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''kellet'''.}}
A separate weight on a separate line that is loosely attached to the {{gli|anchor rode}} so that it can slide down it easily. It is made fast at a distance slightly longer than the {{gli|draft}} of the boat. It is used to prevent the anchor rode from becoming fouled on the {{gli|keel}} or other underwater structures when the boat is resting at anchor and moving randomly during {{nautical term|slack tide}}.}}

{{term|anchor watch}}
{{defn|The crewmen assigned to take care of a ship while it is anchored or moored, and charged with such duties as making sure that the anchor is holding and the vessel is not drifting. Most marine [[Global Positioning System|GPS]] units have an anchor watch alarm capability.}}

{{term|anchor winch}}
{{defn|A horizontal {{gli|capstan}} in the bow used for weighing anchor.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|anchorage}}
{{defn|Any place suitable for a ship to anchor, often an area of a port or harbor.}}

{{term|anchor's aweigh}}
{{defn|Said of an {{gli|anchor}} to indicate that it is just clear of the bottom and that the ship is therefore no longer anchored.}}

{{term|Andrew}}
{{defn|Traditional lower-deck slang term for the [[Royal Navy]].}}

{{term|term=anemometer|content=[[anemometer]]}}
{{defn|An instrument used to measure wind speed.}}

{{term|term=aneroid barometer|content=[[aneroid barometer]]}}
{{defn|An instrument used to measure air pressure, often with the aim of predicting changes in weather.}}

{{term|term=angle of attack|content=[[angle of attack]]}}
{{defn|The angle between the [[apparent wind]] and the chord line of the sail.}}

{{term|term=angle on the bow|content=[[angle on the bow]]}}
{{defn|A naval submariner's term for the angle between a target's course and the line of sight to the submarine. It is expressed as port or starboard, so never exceeds 180 degrees. This is one of the figures entered into the Torpedo Data Computer that makes all the calculations necessary for a [[torpedo]] attack on the target. Not to be confused with ''{{gli|doubling the angle on the bow}}''.}}

{{term|answer}}
{{defn|The expected response of a vessel to control mechanisms, such as a turn "answering" to the {{nautical term|wheel}} and {{nautical term|rudder}}. "She won't answer" might be the report from a helmsman when turning the wheel under a pilot's order fails to produce the expected change of direction.}}

{{term|anti-rolling tanks}}
{{defn|A pair of fluid-filled tanks mounted on opposite sides of a ship below the {{nautical term|waterline}}. The tanks are cross-linked by piping or ducts to allow water to flow between them and at the top by vents or air pipes. The piping is sized so that as the fluid flows from side to side it damps the amount of {{nautical term|roll}}.}}

{{term|term=anti-submarine net|content=[[anti-submarine net]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''anti-submarine boom'''.}}
A heavy underwater net attached to a boom and placed so as to protect a harbor, anchorage, or strait from penetration by submerged [[submarine]]s.}}

{{term|apeak}}
{{defn|More or less vertical. Having the {{gli|anchor rode}} or {{gli|anchor chain|chain}} as nearly vertical as possible without freeing the {{gli|anchor}}.{{citation needed|date=December 2018}}}}

{{term|aport}}
{{defn|Toward the {{nautical term|port}} side of a vessel.}}

{{term|apron}}
{{defn|A piece of wood fitted to the after side of the {{nautical term|stem}} post and the fore side of the {{nautical term|sternpost}} of a {{gli|clinker|clinker-built}} boat, where the planking is secured.{{sfn|Admiralty Manual of Seamanship |1972|p=242}}}}

{{term|term=apparent wind|content=[[apparent wind]]}}
{{defn|The combination of the true wind and the headwind caused by the boat's forward motion. For example, it causes a light side wind to appear to come from well ahead of the {{gli|beam}}.}}

{{term|arc of visibility}}
{{defn|The portion of the horizon over which a lighted {{gli|aid to navigation}} is visible from {{nautical term|seaward}}.}}

{{term|archboard}}
{{defn|A plank along the {{nautical term|stern}} where the name of a ship is commonly painted.<ref>{{cite web |title=How To Come Up With A Great Boat Name |url=https://boatplanet.com/the-captains-blog/how-to-come-up-with-a-great-boat-name |website=boatplanet.com |publisher=Boat Planet |access-date=28 October 2019}}</ref>}}

{{term|armada}}
{{defn|A large fleet of warships.}}

{{term|armament}}
{{defn|A ship's complement of weapons.}}

{{term|armor belt}}
{{defn|1=See ''{{glossary link|glossary=Glossary of nautical terms (A-L)|belt armor}}''.}}

{{term|armory}}
{{defn|Area on a warship for storage of [[small arms]] and ammunition.}}

{{term|term=Articles of War|content=[[Articles of War]]}}
{{defn|Regulations governing the military and [[navy|naval]] forces of the UK and US; read to every {{nautical term|ship's company}} on {{gli|commission|commissioning}} and at specified intervals during the commission.}}

{{term|as the crow flies}}
{{defn|As measured by a straight line between two points (which might cross land), in the way that a crow or other bird would be capable of traveling rather than a ship, which must go around land. See also ''{{gli|great circle}}''.}}

{{Term|[[ASDIC]]}}
{{defn|Purportedly an acronym for Allied Submarine Devices Investigation Committee, and a type of [[SONAR]] used by the Allies for detecting submarines during the First and Second World Wars. The term has been generically applied to equipment for "under-water supersonic echo-ranging equipment" of submarines and other vessels.<ref name=Abbot>{{cite web |website= U.S.S. Abbot DD-629 |url= http://abbot.us/dictionary/search=ASDIC |title= World War II Naval Dictionary |access-date= May 30, 2025 |archive-date= 2025-05-30 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250530194116/https://abbot.us/dictionary/display.shtml?search= }}</ref>}}

{{term|ashore}}
{{defn|no=1|On the beach, shore, or land (as opposed to {{gli|aboard}} or ''on board'' a vessel).}}
{{defn|no=2|Towards the shore.}}
{{defn|no=3|To "run ashore": to collide with the shore (as opposed to "run {{gli|aground}}", which is to strike a submerged feature such as a reef or sandbar).}}

{{term|assembly station}}
{{defn|See ''{{nautical term|muster station}}''.}}

{{term|astarboard}}
{{defn|Toward the {{nautical term|starboard}} side of a vessel.}}

{{term|astern}}
{{defn|no=1|Toward the {{nautical term|stern}} or rear of a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|Behind a vessel.}}

{{term|astern gear}}
{{defn|The gear or gears that, when engaged with an engine or motor, result in backwards movement or force. Equivalent to reverse in a manual-transmission automobile.}}

{{term|asylum harbour}}
{{defn|A harbour used to provide shelter from a storm. See ''{{gli|harbor of refuge}}''.}}

{{term|ASW}}
{{defn|An acronym for [[anti-submarine warfare]].}}

{{term|Atlantic bow}}
{{defn|A raised {{gli|bow}} with noticeable {{nautical term|sheer}} and {{gli|flare}} introduced in German {{nautical term|warship}}s in the late 1930s to improve {{nautical term|seakeeping}} by keeping the {{gli|forecastle}} drier and to allow easier operation of weapons.}}

{{term|athwart}}
{{term|athwartships|multi=y}}
{{defn|At right angles to the {{gli|fore}} and {{gli|aft}} or centerline of a ship.}}

{{term|auxiliary}}
{{defn|no=1|An engine installed on a sailing vessel to provide mechanical power when entering harbour or in light or contrary winds.}}
{{defn|no=2|A vessel in naval service but crewed mostly or entirely by civilians (as in Royal Fleet Auxiliary Service and Royal Naval Auxiliary Service){{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=11}}}}

{{term|auxiliary ship |multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Main article: [[auxiliary ship]]}}
A naval ship designed to operate in any number of roles supporting combatant ships and other naval operations, including a wide range of activities related to replenishment, transport, repair, harbor services and research.}}

{{term|avast}}
{{defn|Stop, cease or desist from whatever is being done. From the {{langx|nl|hou' vast|lit=hold on}}; the imperative form of {{langx|nl|vasthouden|lit=to hold on to|links=no}}, or the {{langx|it|basta}}.<ref name=covey/> Compare [[¡Ya basta!]]}}

{{term|aviso}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Formerly also '''adviso'''.}}
A kind of {{gli|dispatch boat}} or advice boat, surviving particularly in the French Navy. They are considered equivalent to modern {{nautical term|sloop|sloops}}.}}

{{term|awash}}
{{defn|So low in the water that the water is constantly washing across the surface.}}

{{term|aweigh}}
{{defn|The position of an {{gli|anchor}} that is just clear of making contact with the bottom.}}

{{term|axial fire}}
{{defn|Fire oriented towards the ends of the ship; the opposite of {{gli|broadside}} fire. In the Age of Sail, this was known as "raking fire".}}

{{Term|aye, aye}}
{{defn|({{IPAc-en|ˌ|aɪ|_|ˈ|aɪ}}) A reply to an order or command to indicate that it, firstly, is heard; and, secondly, is understood and will be carried out (e.g. "Aye, aye, sir" to officers). Also the proper reply from a hailed boat, to indicate that an officer is on board.}}

{{term|azimuth circle}}
{{defn|An instrument used to take the bearings of celestial objects.}}

{{term|term=azimuth compass|content=[[azimuth compass]]}}
{{defn|An instrument employed for ascertaining the position of the Sun with respect to [[magnetic north]]. The [[azimuth]] of an object is its {{gli|bearing}} from the observer measured as an angle clockwise from [[true north]].}}

{{term|term=azimuth thruster|content=[[azimuth thruster]]}}
{{defn|A steerable drive leg fitted through the bottom of a hull, carrying a propeller. Compare ''{{nautical term|stern drive}}'' and ''{{nautical term|sail drive}}''.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

== B ==
{{glossary}}
{{Term|term= B & R rig}}
{{defn|A style of standing rigging used on sailboats that lacks a {{gli|backstay}}. The mast is said to be supported like a "tripod", with swept-back spreaders and a {{gli|forestay}}. Used widely on Hunter brand sailboats, among others. Designed and named by Lars Bergstrom and Sven Ridder.{{citation needed|date=May 2020}}}}

{{term|back}}
{{defn|no=1|To make a sail fill with wind on the opposite side normally used for sailing forward. A fore and aft headsail is backed by either not moving the sail across when tacking, or by hauling it to windward with the weather sheet. A square sail is backed by hauling the yards round with the braces. The sail is then {{gli|aback}}.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=2|(With {{nautical term|oars}}) to push against the water with the oar in the opposite direction than normally used for moving the boat forward. This is used to slow the speed of the boat, or to move astern when manoeuvring.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=3|With regard to the wind, a counterclockwise shift relative to the vessel's current course.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} When this shift is clockwise, it is referred to as {{nautical term|veer|veer}}.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|back and fill}}
{{defn|A method of keeping a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} vessel under control while drifting with the tide along a narrow channel. The ship lies {{gli|broadside}} to the current, with the main {{nautical term|topsail}} backed and the fore and mizzen topsail full: essentially a {{gli|heave to|hove-to}} position. Selective backing and filling of these sails moves the ship {{gli|ahead}} or {{gli|astern}}, so allowing it to be kept in the best part of the channel. A {{gli|jib}} and the {{nautical term|spanker}} are used to help balance the sail plan. This method cannot be used if the wind is going in the same direction and at the same speed as the tide.{{sfn|Harland|1984|pp=199-202}}}}

{{term|term=backstay|content=[[backstay]]}}
{{defn|A stay or cable, reaching from the mast heads, of the [[topmast]], the {{nautical term|topgallant-mast}} the {{nautical term|royal-mast}}, the {{nautical term|skysail-mast}} to the ship's side abaft the lower rigging; used to support the mast.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}} }}

{{term|back wash}}
{{defn|Water forced {{gli|astern}} by the action of the {{nautical term|propeller}}. Also, the receding of waves.}}

{{term|term=baggywrinkle|content=[[baggywrinkle]]}}
{{defn|A soft covering for standing rigging (such as [[Shroud (sailing)|shrouds]] and [[Stays (nautical)|stays]]) that reduces sail chafing.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=bailer|content=[[hand bailer|bailer]]}}
{{defn|Any device for removing water that has entered a vessel.}}

{{term|bail out}}
{{defn|Tacking away from other boats to obtain ''clear air''. Often used for starting situations.}}

{{term|baldie}}
{{defn|A type of Scottish sailboat introduced in 1860, used for fishing. A baldie is [[Carvel (boat building)|carvel]]-built, with her mast far forward and rigged with a lug sail and sometimes a {{gli|jib}}. Some historians believe "Baldie" is a contraction of "Garibaldi", a reference to the Italian general and nationalist [[Giuseppe Garibaldi]], whose name was a household word at the time the baldie was introduced.}}

{{term|balance rudder}}
{{defn|Not usually a single rudder, but a set of three or four rudders operating together to maneuver a sternwheel steamboat. Placed just forward of the paddlewheels, the effectiveness of the balance rudder is increased by the flow of water generated by the paddles, giving such steamboats a high degree of maneuverability.<ref name=hunter1993_pp173-174>Hunter 1993. pp. 173–174.</ref>}}

{{term|balanced rudder}}
{{defn|A rudder with a significant amount of area ahead of the rorational axis, which moves the hydrodynamic centre of the rudder nearer to the rotation axis and reduces the torque required to steer.<ref name="PNA" /> }}

{{term|term=ballast|content=[[ballast]]}}
{{defn|Heavy material that is placed in a position low in the hull to provide stability. It can be moveable material, such as gravel or stones, permanently or semi-permanently installed, or integral to the hull, such as the (typically) lead or cast-iron ballast keel of a sailing yacht. See also ''{{gli|in ballast}}''.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=ballast tank|content=[[ballast tank]]}}
{{defn|A compartment which can be filled  or partly filled with water, used on ships, submarines and other submersibles to control buoyancy and stability.}}

{{term|term=Baltimore Clipper|content=[[Baltimore Clipper]]}}
{{defn|A fast sailing ship{{snd}}an early form of {{gli|clipper}}{{snd}}built on the [[Mid-Atlantic seaboard]] of the United States, especially at [[Baltimore, Maryland]]. Popular as {{nautical term|merchant ship|merchant ships}} in both the United States and the United Kingdom by the late 18th century, Baltimore Clippers usually were two-{{nautical term|mast|masted}} {{nautical term|schooner|schooners}} or {{gli|brigantine|brigantines}}. }}

{{term|balls to four watch}}
{{defn|US Navy slang for the 0000–0400 [[Watch system|watch]].}}

{{term|term=bank|content=[[bank (topography)|bank]]}}
{{defn|A large area of elevated sea floor, deep enough to allow navigation.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|banyan}}
{{defn|A traditional Royal Navy term for a day or less of rest and relaxation.}}

{{term|term=bar|content=[[Bar (river morphology)|bar]]}}
{{defn|Mass of sand or earth raised above the general seabed depth by the motion of water. Bars are often found at the mouth of rivers or entrances to harbours and can make navigation over them extremely dangerous at some states of tide and current flow, but can also confer tranquility in the inshore waters by acting as a barrier to large waves. See also ''{{nautical term|touch and go}}'' and ''{{gli|grounding}}''.}}

{{term|term=bar pilot|content=[[maritime pilot|bar pilot]]}}
{{defn|A navigator who guides a ship over dangerous sandbars at the mouths of rivers and bays.}}

{{term|term=barber hauler|content=[[Running rigging#Shaping|barber hauler]]}}
{{defn|A technique of temporarily rigging a sailboat lazy sheet so as to allow the boat to sail closer to the wind; i.e. using the lazy jib sheet to pull the {{gli|jib}} closer to the mid line, allowing a point of sail that would otherwise not be achievable.{{citation needed|date = July 2017}}}}

{{term|term=barbette|content=[[barbette]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A fixed armored enclosure protecting a ship's guns aboard warships without gun turrets, generally taking the form of a ring of armor over which guns mounted on an open-topped rotating turntable could fire, particularly on ships built during the second half of the 19th century.}}
{{defn|no=2|The inside fixed trunk of a warship's turreted gun-mounting, on which the turret revolves, containing the hoists for shells and cordite from the shell-room and magazine, particularly on ships built after the late 19th century.}}

{{term|term=barca-longa|content=[[barca-longa]]}}
{{defn|A two- or three-masted {{gli|lugger}} used for fishing on the coasts of Spain and Portugal and more widely in the Mediterranean Sea in the late 17th and 18th centuries. The British [[Royal Navy]] also used them for shore raids and as {{gli|dispatch boat|dispatch boats}} in the Mediterranean.}}

{{term|term=bareboat charter|content=[[bareboat charter]]}}
{{defn|An arrangement for the chartering or hiring of a vessel, whereby the vessel's owner provides no crew or provisions as part of the agreement; instead, the people who rent the vessel are responsible for crewing and provisioning her.}}

{{term|bare poles}}
{{defn|Sailing without any {{gli|canvas}} raised, usually in a strong wind.}}

{{term|term=barge|content=[[barge]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A towed or self-propelled flat-bottomed boat, built mainly for river, canal or coastal transport of heavy goods.}}
{{defn|no=2|''Admiral{{'}}s barge'': A boat (or aircraft) at the disposal of an {{gli|admiral}} (or other high ranking flag officer) for his or her use as transportation between a larger vessel and the shore or within a harbor. In Royal Navy service, the colour of the hull (or aircraft cheat-line) denotes the rank, green for commanders-in-chief and dark blue for other {{gli|flag officer}}s.<ref>{{cite book |title=Manual of Seamanship, Volume 1 |date=1937 |publisher=HMSO}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Photograph of de Havilland Sea Dove XJ319, 'The Green Parrot', Admiral's barge, C-in-C HMS Heron, 1973 |url=https://www.alamy.com/royal-navy-de-havilland-dh104-sea-devon-c20-xj319-at-the-1973-biggin-hill-international-air-fair-in-the-distinctive-green-and-white-livery-of-an-admirals-barge-from-hms-heron-rnas-yeovilton-image504871024.html |website=alamy.co.uk |access-date=25 October 2024}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=barge slip|content=[[Ferry slip|barge slip]]}}
{{defn|A specialized docking facility designed to receive a {{gli|barge}} or {{gli|car float}} that is used to carry wheeled vehicles across a body of water.}}

{{term|bark}}
{{defn|An alternate spelling of {{gli|barque}}.}}

{{term|barkentine}}
{{defn|An alternate spelling of {{gli|barquentine}}.}}

{{term|term=barque|content=[[barque]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also spelled '''bark'''.}}
A sailing vessel of three or more masts, with all masts {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} except the sternmost, which is {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}}.}}

{{term|term=barquentine|content=[[barquentine]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also spelled '''barkentine'''.}}
A sailing vessel with three or more masts, with all masts {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} except the foremast, which is {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}}.}}

{{term|barrack ship}}
{{defn|A ship or craft designed to function as a floating [[barracks]] for housing military personnel.}}

{{term|term=barratry|content=[[barratry (admiralty law)|barratry]]}}
{{defn|In {{gli|admiralty law}}, an act of gross misconduct against a [[shipowner]] or a ship's [[demise charter]]er by a ship's master or crew that damages the ship or its [[cargo]]. Acts of barratry can include [[desertion]], illegal {{nautical term|scuttling}}, theft of the ship or cargo and committing any actions that may not be in the shipowner's or demise charterer's best interests.}}

{{term|term=barometer|content=[[barometer]]}}
{{defn|An instrument for measuring air pressure. Used in weather forecasting.}}

{{term|term=barrelman|content=[[barrelman]]}}
{{defn|A sailor stationed in the {{gli|crow's nest}}.}}

{{term|term=batten|content=[[Sail batten|batten]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A stiff strip used to support the {{nautical term|roach}} of a {{nautical term|sail}}, increasing the sail area.}}
{{defn|no=2|Any thin strip of material (wood, plastic, etc.).}}

{{term|term=batten down the hatches|content=[[batten down the hatches]]}}
{{defn|To prepare for inclement weather by securing the closed [[cargo hatch]] covers with wooden {{gli|batten|battens}} so as to prevent water from entering from any angle.}}

{{term|term=battle stations|content=[[general quarters|battle stations]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''general quarters''' or '''action stations'''.}}
1.&nbsp;An announcement made aboard a naval warship to signal the crew to prepare for battle, imminent damage, or any other emergency (such as a fire).}}
{{defn|no=2|Specific positions in a naval warship to which one or more crew members are assigned when battle stations is called.}}

{{term|term=battlecruiser|content=[[battlecruiser]]}}
{{defn|A type of large {{gli|capital ship}} of the first half of the 20th century, similar in size, appearance, and cost to a {{gli|battleship}} and typically armed with the same kind of heavy guns, but much more lightly armored (on the scale of a {{gli|cruiser}}) and therefore faster than a battleship but more vulnerable to damage.}}

{{term|term=battleship|content=[[battleship]]}}
{{defn|A type of large, heavily armored warship of the second half of the 19th century and first half of the 20th century, armed with heavy-caliber guns and designed to fight other battleships in a {{gli|line of battle}}. It was the successor to the {{nautical term|ship-of-the-line}} used during the [[Age of Sail]].}}

{{term|beach}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Or '''the beach'''}}
A term used broadly to refer to land or the shore, and not necessarily literally to a [[beach]]. For example, a {{nautical term|ship}} which turns toward the shore can be said to have turned toward ''the beach'', and a person or object on land can be said to be on ''the beach''. See also ''{{nautical term|on the beach}}''.}}

{{term|term=beaching|content=[[beaching (nautical)|beaching]]}}
{{defn|Deliberately running a vessel {{gli|aground}} so as to load or unload it (as with [[landing craft]]), or sometimes to prevent a damaged vessel from sinking or to facilitate repairs below the {{nautical term|waterline}}.}}

{{term|term=beacon|content=[[beacon]]}}
{{defn|A lighted or unlighted fixed {{gli|aid to navigation}} attached directly to the Earth's surface. Examples include lighthouses and daybeacons.}}

{{term|term=beakhead|content=[[beakhead]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The ram on the prow of a fighting {{gli|galley}} of ancient and medieval times.}}
{{defn|no=2|The protruding part of the foremost section of a sailing ship of the 16th to the 18th centuries, usually ornate, which was used as a working platform by sailors handling the sails of the {{gli|bowsprit}}. It also housed the crew's heads (toilets).}}

{{term|term=beam|content=[[beam (nautical)|beam]]}}
{{defn|The width of a vessel at its widest point, or a point alongside the ship at the midpoint of its length.}}

{{term|beam ends}}
{{defn|The sides of a ship. To describe a ship as "on her beam ends" may mean the vessel is literally on her side and possibly about to capsize; more often, the phrase means the vessel is listing 45 degrees or more.}}

{{term|beam reach}}
{{defn|Sailing with the wind coming across the vessel's {{gli|beam}}. This is normally the fastest {{nautical term|point of sail}} for a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} vessel.}}

{{term|beam sea}}
{{defn|A sea in which waves are moving perpendicular to a vessel's course.<ref name="Saunders V3 1965" />}}

{{term|beam wind}}
{{defn|A wind blowing perpendicular to a vessel's course.}}

{{term|bear}}
{{defn|A large, squared-off stone used with sand for scraping wooden decks clean.}}

{{term|term=bear down|content= bear down {{anchor|bear away|bear off}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bear away''', '''bear off'''. See also {{gli|fall off}}.}}
To turn or steer a vessel away from the wind, often with reference to a transit.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|bear up}}
{{defn|To turn or steer a vessel into the wind.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=bearing|content=[[bearing (navigation)|bearing]]}}
{{defn|The horizontal direction of a line of sight between two objects on the surface of the Earth. See also ''{{gli|absolute bearing}}'' and ''{{nautical term|relative bearing}}''.}}

{{term|term=beat to quarters|content=[[beat to quarters]]}}
{{defn|Prepare for battle (in reference to beating a drum to signal the need for battle preparation).}}

{{term|term=beat to|content=[[Points of sail|beat to]]}}
{{term|beting|multi=y}}
{{defn|Sailing as close as possible towards the wind (perhaps only about 60°) in a zig-zag course so as to attain an upwind direction into which it is otherwise impossible to sail directly. See also ''{{nautical term|tacking}}''.}}

{{term|term=Beaufort scale|content=[[Beaufort scale]]}}
{{defn|A scale describing wind speed, devised by [[Francis Beaufort|Admiral Sir Francis Beaufort]] in 1808, in which winds are graded by the effects of their force on the surface of the sea or on a vessel (originally, the amount of sail that a fully rigged frigate could carry).}}

{{term|becalm}}
{{defn|To cut off the wind from a sailing vessel, either by the proximity of land or by another vessel.}}

{{term|becalmed}}
{{defn|Unable to move due to a lack of wind, said of a sailing vessel; resigned merely to drift with the current rather to move by controlled management of sails.}}

{{term|becket}}
{{defn|A short piece of {{gli|line}} usually {{nautical term|spliced}} into a circle or with an {{gli|eye splice|eye}} on either end.}}

{{term|before the mast}}
{{defn|Literally, the area of a ship before the {{gli|foremast}} (the {{gli|forecastle}}). Most often used to refer to men whose living quarters are located here: officers were typically quartered in the sternmost areas of the ship (near the {{nautical term|quarterdeck}}), while officer-trainees lived between the two ends of the ship and become known as "midshipmen". Crew members who started out as seamen and then became midshipmen, and later, officers, were said to have gone from "one end of the ship to the other". See also ''{{gli|hawsepiper}}''.}}

{{term|belay}}
{{defn|no=1|To make fast a line around a fitting, usually a cleat or {{gli|belaying pin}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|To secure a climbing person in a similar manner.}}
{{defn|no=3|An order to halt a current activity or countermand an order prior to execution.}}

{{term|term=belaying pin|content=[[belaying pin]]}}
{{defn|A short movable bar of iron or hard wood to which running rigging may be secured, or "belayed". Belaying pins are inserted in holes in a pin-rail.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}} }}

{{term|bell}}
{{defn|See {{nautical term|ship's bell}}.}}

{{term|bell rope}}
{{defn|A short length of {{gli|line}} made fast to the clapper of the {{nautical term|ship's bell}}.}}

{{term|bell buoy}}
{{defn|1=A type of {{gli|buoy}} with a large bell and hanging hammers that sound by wave action.<ref>{{cite journal|journal=[[Scientific American Supplement]]|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=j80wAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA280|access-date=2019-04-17|volume=86|issue=2235 |date=2 November 1918|publisher=Munn and Company|pages=280–281|title=Nature's Submarines: How we guard our commerce against them|doi = 10.1038/scientificamerican11021918-280supp|url-access=subscription}}</ref>}}

{{term|below}}
{{defn|1=On or into a lower deck.}}

{{term|below decks}}
{{defn|In or into any of the spaces below the main deck of a vessel.}}

{{term|term=belt armor|content=[[belt armor]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''armor belt'''.}}
A layer of heavy metal armor plated onto or within the outer {{gli|hull}} of a warship, typically on {{gli|battleship|battleships}}, {{gli|battlecruiser|battlecruisers}}, {{gli|cruiser|cruisers}} and {{gli|aircraft carrier|aircraft carriers}}, usually covering the warship from her main deck down to some distance below the {{nautical term|waterline}}. If built within the hull, rather than forming the outer hull, the belt would be installed at an inclined angle to improve the warship's protection from shells striking the hull.}}

{{term|term=bend|content=[[List of bend knots|bend]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A knot used to join two ropes or lines. See also ''{{gli|hitch}}''.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|To attach a rope to an object.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=3|To fasten a sail to a yard or stay so that it may be set.{{sfn|Biddlecombe|1990|p=2}}{{sfn|Mayne|2000}}}}

{{term|term=Bermuda rig|content=[[Bermuda rig]]}}
{{term|Bermudan rig|multi=y}}
{{defn|A triangular {{nautical term|mainsail}}, without any upper {{nautical term|spar}}, which is hoisted up the mast by a single {{gli|halyard}} attached to the head of the sail. This configuration, introduced to Europe about 1920, allows the use of a tall mast, enabling sails to be set higher where wind speed is greater.}}

{{term|term=Bermuda sloop|content=[[Bermuda sloop]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} sailing vessel with a single mast setting a {{gli|Bermuda rig}} mainsail and a single {{gli|headsail}}. The Bermuda sloop is a very common type of modern sailing yacht.}}

{{term|term=berth|content=[[berth (moorings)|berth]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A location in a port or harbor used specifically for mooring vessels while not at sea.}}
{{defn|no=2|A safe margin of distance to be kept by a vessel from another vessel or from an obstruction, hence the phrase "to give a wide berth".<ref name=Bathe>{{cite book |last1=Bathe |first1=Basil W. |last2=Villiers |first2=Alan |date=March 1978 |title=The Visual Encyclopedia of Nautical Terms Under Sail |location=New York Crown Publishers Inc |isbn=0-517-53317-0 |url=https://archive.org/details/visualencycloped00basi }} {{ISBN|978-0-517-53317-8}}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=3|A bed or sleeping accommodation on a boat or ship.}}
{{defn|no=4|A job or position of employment on a boat or ship.}}

{{term|best bower}}
{{defn|The larger of two {{gli|anchor|anchors}} carried in the {{gli|bow}}; so named as it was the last, "best" hope for anchoring a vessel.}}

{{term|between the devil and the deep blue sea}}
{{defn|See ''{{gli|devil seam}}''.}}

{{term|between wind and water}}
{{defn|The part of a ship's {{gli|hull}} that is sometimes submerged and sometimes brought above water by the rolling of the vessel.}}

{{term|bight}}
{{defn|no=1|A loop in a rope or line – a hitch or knot tied "on the bight" is one tied in the middle of a rope, without access to the ends.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|An indentation in a coastline.}}

{{term|term=bilander|content=[[bilander]] {{anchor|billander}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''billander''' or '''be{{'}}landre'''.}}
A small European merchant sailing ship with two masts, the {{nautical term|mainmast}} {{gli|lateen rigging|lateen-rigged}} with a trapezoidal {{nautical term|mainsail}}, and the {{gli|foremast}} carrying the conventional square course and square {{nautical term|topsail}}. Used in the Netherlands for coast and canal traffic and occasionally in the North Sea, but more frequently used in the Mediterranean Sea.}}

{{term|term=bilge|content=[[bilge]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The part of the {{gli|hull}} that the ship rests on if it takes the ground; the outer end of the {{gli|floor|floors}}. The "turn of the bilge" is the part of the hull that changes from the (approximately) vertical sides of the hull to the more horizontal bottom of the ship.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}
{{defn|no=2|(Usually in the plural: "bilges") The compartment at the bottom of the hull of a ship or boat where water collects and must be pumped out of the vessel; the space between the bottom hull planking and the ceiling of the hold.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=3|To damage the hull in the area of the bilge, usually by {{gli|grounding}} or hitting an obstruction.}}
{{defn|no=4|To fail an academic course ("bilge") or curriculum ("bilge out").}}

{{term|term=bilge keel|content=[[bilge keel]]}}
{{defn|One of a pair of {{gli|keel|keels}} on either side of the hull, usually slanted outwards. In [[yacht]]s, they allow the use of a drying mooring, the boat standing upright on the keels (and often a ''skeg'') when the tide is out.}}

{{term|bilged on her anchor}}
{{defn|A ship that has run upon her own {{gli|anchor}} such that the {{gli|anchor chain|anchor cable}} runs under the {{gli|hull}}.}}

{{term|bill}}
{{defn|The extremity of the arm of an {{gli|anchor}}; the point of or beyond the fluke.}}

{{term|term=billethead|content=[[billethead]]}}
{{defn|no=1|On smaller vessels, a non-figural carving, most often a curl of foliage, as a substitute for a {{gli|figurehead}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A round piece of timber at the {{gli|bow}} or {{nautical term|stern}} of a whaleboat, around which the harpoon line is run out when the whale darts off.}}

{{term|term=Bimini top|content=[[Bimini top]]}}
{{defn|An open-front canvas top for the cockpit of a boat, usually supported by a metal frame.}}

{{anchor|bimmy}}
{{term|term=bimmy|content=[[flagellation|bimmy]]}}
{{defn|A punitive instrument.}}

{{term|term=binnacle|content=[[binnacle]]}}
{{defn|The stand on which the ship's {{gli|compass}} is mounted, usually near the {{gli|helm}}, permitting ready reference by the {{gli|helmsman}}.}}

{{term|binnacle list}}
{{defn|A ship's sick list. The list of men unable to report for duty was given to the officer or mate of the watch by the ship's surgeon. The list was kept at the {{gli|binnacle}}.}}

{{term|bird farm}}
{{defn|United States Navy slang for an {{gli|aircraft carrier}}.}}

{{term|bite}}
{{defn|Verb used in reference to a {{nautical term|rudder}}, as in "the rudder begins to bite". When a vessel has steerageway the rudder will act to steer the vessel, i.e. it has enough water flow past it to steer with. Physically this is noticeable with tiller or unassisted wheel steering by the rudder exhibiting resistance to being turned from the straight ahead – this resistance is the rudder "biting" and is how a helmsman first senses that a vessel has acquired steerageway.}}

{{term|bitt}}
{{defn|no=1|A post or pair of posts mounted on the ship's {{gli|bow}} for fastening ropes or cables.}}
{{defn|no=2|A strong vertical timber or iron fastened through the deck beams that is used for securing ropes or {{gli|hawser|hawsers}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|bitt heads}}
{{defn|The tops of two massive timbers that support the {{nautical term|windlass}} on a sailing barge.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=bitter end|content=[[Knot#Bitter end|bitter end]]}}
{{defn|The last part or loose end of a rope or cable. The {{gli|anchor cable}} is tied to the {{gli|bitt|bitts}}; when the cable is fully paid out, the bitter end has been reached.}}

{{term|term=black gang|content=[[black gang (ship)|black gang]]}}
{{defn|The engineering crew of the vessel, i.e. crew members who work in the vessel's engine room, fire room and/or boiler room, so called because they would typically be covered in coal dust during the days of coal-fired steamships.}}

{{term|blinker}}
{{defn|A search light, used for signaling by code. Usually fitted with a spring controlled shutter.}}

{{term|term=block|content=[[block (sailing)|block]]}}
{{defn|A pulley with one or more {{nautical term|sheave|sheaves}} or grooves over which a {{gli|line}} is roved. It can be used to change the direction of the line, or in pairs used to form a tackle.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|block, fiddle}}
{{defn|A block with two {{nautical term|sheave|sheaves}} in the same plane, one being smaller than the other, giving the block a somewhat violin appearance.}}

{{term|block, snatch}}
{{defn|A single {{nautical term|sheave}} block with one end of the frame hinged and able to be opened, so as to admit a line other than by forcing an end through the opening.}}

{{term|term=blockship|content=[[blockship]]}}
{{defn|A vessel sunk deliberately to block a waterway to prevent the waterway's use by an enemy.}}

{{term|term=blower}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''blower engine''', '''blower fan'''.}}
A mechanically driven fan used to increase air supply to the boiler furnace of a steamboat or steamship, thus increasing combustion and steam pressure, and thereby engine power and speed.}}

{{Term|[[Blue Ensign]]}}
{{defn|A flag flown as an {{gli|ensign}} by certain British ships. Prior to 1864, ships of the [[Royal Navy]]'s [[Coloured squadrons of the Royal Navy|Blue Squadron]] flew it; since the reorganisation of the Royal Navy in 1864 eliminated its naval use, it has been flown instead by British merchant vessels whose officers and crew include a certain prescribed number (which has varied over the years) of retired Royal Navy or [[Royal Naval Reserve]] personnel or are commanded by an officer of the Royal Naval Reserve in possession of a government warrant; Royal Research Ships by warrant, regardless of their manning by naval, naval reserve and [[Merchant Navy]] personnel; or British-registered [[yacht]]s belonging to members of certain [[yacht club]]s, although yachts were prohibited from flying the Blue Ensign during World War I and World War II.}}

{{term|term=Blue Peter|content=[[International maritime signal flags|Blue Peter]]}}
{{defn|A blue and white flag (the flag for the letter ''P'') hoisted at the foretrucks of ships about to sail. Formerly a white ship on a blue ground, but later a white square on a blue ground.}}

{{term|term=blue water|content=[[blue water]]}}
{{defn|no=1|That part of the ocean lying more than a few hundred {{nautical term|nautical mile}}s from shore, and thus beyond the outer boundary of {{gli|green water}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|More generally, the open ocean or deep sea.}}

{{term|term=blue-water navy|content=[[blue-water navy]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A navy capable of sustained operations in the open ocean, beyond a few hundred nautical miles from shore.}}
{{defn|no=2|That portion of a navy capable of sustained operations in the open ocean, beyond a few hundred nautical miles from shore.}}

{{term|bluejacket}}
{{defn|{{ghat|also '''blue-jacket'''}}
1.&nbsp;A {{nautical term|sailor}} or [[Enlisted personnel|enlisted person]] of the [[Royal Navy]], [[Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth]] navies, the [[United States Navy]], or the [[United States Coast Guard]]. ''Bluejacket'' derives from a blue jacket naval enlisted personnel once wore while ashore. In the Royal Navy and Commonwealth navies, the term generally is synonymous with ''{{nautical term|rating}}'' and often includes [[petty officer]]s and [[chief petty officer]]s. In the US Navy and US Coast Guard, the term excludes chief petty officers.}}
{{defn|no=2|More loosely, a sailor or enlisted person of any navy.}}

{{term|term=''Bluejacket's Manual''|content=[[The Bluejacket's Manual|''Bluejacket's Manual'']]}}
{{defn|A basic handbook for US Navy personnel.}}

{{term|board}}
{{defn|no=1|To step onto, climb onto or otherwise enter a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|The side of a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=3|The distance a sailing vessel runs between {{nautical term|tacking|tacks}} when working to {{nautical term|windward}}.}}

{{term|term=boat|content=[[boat]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Any small craft or vessel designed to float on and provide transport over or under water.}}
{{defn|no=2|Naval slang for a {{nautical term|submarine}} of any size.}}
{{defn|no=3|A term used in Canada and the United States for a {{nautical term|ship}} of any size used on the Great Lakes.}}

{{term|term=boat hook|content=[[boat hook]]}}
{{defn|A pole with a blunt tip and a hook on the end, sometimes with a ring on its opposite end to which a line may be attached. Typically used to assist in docking and undocking a boat, with its hook used to pull a boat towards a dock and the blunt end to push it away from a dock, as well as to reach into the water to help people catch {{gli|buoy|buoys}} or other floating objects or to reach people in the water.}}

{{term|boat keeper}}
{{defn|A boatkeeper was a [[sailor]] that knew the harbor thoroughly and was able to act as a pilot. He was in command after the last pilot had left to board a ship and brought the pilot boat back to harbor. He was required to know how to use a [[sextant]] as he could be 300 miles from port.}}

{{term|term=boathouse|content=[[boathouse]]}}
{{defn|A building especially designed for the storage of boats, typically located on open water such as a lake or river. Boathouses are normally used to store smaller sports or leisure craft, often [[rowing boat]]s but sometimes craft such as [[Punt (boat)|punts]] or small [[motor boat]]s.}}

{{term|term=boatsteerer|content=[[boatsteerer]]}}
{{defn|A member of the crew of a 19th-century whaling ship responsible for pulling the forward oar of a whaleboat and for harpooning whales.}}

{{term|term=boatswain|content=[[boatswain]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bosun'''.}}
A non-commissioned officer responsible for the sails, ropes, rigging and boats on a ship who issues "piped" commands to seamen.}}

{{term|term=boatswain's call|content=[[bosun's call|boatswain's call]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bosun's call''', '''boatswain's pipe''', '''bosun's pipe''', '''boatswain's whistle''' or '''bosun's whistle'''.}}
A high-pitched pipe or a non-diaphragm-type whistle used on naval ships by a {{gli|boatswain}}, historically to pass commands to the crew but in modern times limited to ceremonial use.}}

{{term|term=boatswain's chair|content=[[bosun's chair|boatswain's chair]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bosun's chair'''}}
1.&nbsp;A short board or swatch of heavy canvas, secured in a bridle of ropes, used to hoist a man aloft or over the ship's side for painting and similar work. Modern boatswain's chairs incorporate safety harnesses to prevent the occupant from falling.}}
{{defn|no=2|A metal chair used for ship-to-ship personnel transfers at sea while underway.}}

{{term|boatswain's pipe}}
{{term|boatswain's whistle|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|boatswain's call}}.}}}}

{{term|boatwright}}
{{defn|A maker of boats, especially of traditional wooden construction.}}

{{term|bob}}
{{term|bobfly|multi=y}}
{{defn|A pennant or flag bearing the owner's colors and mounted on the {{nautical term|topsail}} trunk.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=bobstay|content=[[bobstay]]}}
{{defn|A stay that holds the {{gli|bowsprit}} downwards, counteracting the effect of the {{gli|forestay}} and the lift of sails. Usually made of wire or chain to eliminate stretching.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|body plan}}
{{defn|In [[shipbuilding]], an end [[elevation (view)|elevation]] showing the contour of the sides of a ship at certain points of her length.}}

{{term|term=boiler|content=[[boiler (power generation)|boiler]]}}
{{defn|A power generation system component that produces steam.}}

{{term|boilerman}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|fireman}}.}}}}

{{term|boiler room}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|fire room}}.}}}}

{{term|boiler uptake}}
{{defn|The exhaust of a boiler, excluding the [[funnel (ship)|funnel]].}}

{{term|term=bolt rope|content=[[bolt rope]]}}
{{defn|A rope, sewn on to reinforce the edges of a sail.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=bollard|content=[[Bollard (mooring)|bollard]]}}
{{defn|From "bol" or "bole", the round trunk of a tree. A substantial vertical pillar to which lines may be made fast. Generally on the quayside rather than the ship.}}

{{term|term=bomb vessel|content=[[bomb vessel]] {{anchor|bomb ketch|bomb ship}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bomb''', '''bombard''', '''bombarde''', '''bomb ketch''' or '''bomb ship'''.}}
A type of specialized naval wooden sailing vessel of the late 17th through mid-19th centuries designed for bombarding fixed positions on land, armed for this purpose with [[Mortar (weapons)|mortars]] mounted forward near the bow.}}

{{term|bombard}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also spelled '''bombarde'''.}}
1.&nbsp;A small, two-masted vessel common in the Mediterranean in the 18th and 19th centuries, similar in design to an English {{gli|ketch}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|An alternative name used in the 18th and 19th centuries for a {{gli|bomb vessel}}.}}

{{term|Bombay runner}}
{{defn|A large cockroach.}}

{{term|bonded jacky}}
{{defn|A type of tobacco or sweet cake.}}

{{term|bone in her teeth}}
{{defn|A phrase describing the appearance of a vessel throwing up a prominent {{gli|bow wave}} while travelling at high speed. From a vantage point in front of the vessel, the wave rising in either side of the bow evokes the image of a dog carrying a bone in its mouth, and the vessel is said to have ''a bone in her teeth''.}}

{{term|term=bonnet|content=[[Bonnet (sail)|bonnet]]}}
{{defn|An additional strip of canvas laced to the foot of a sail to increase its area.{{sfn|March|1972|p=359}} In square-rigged vessels {{circa|1350–1660}} the courses did not have reef points. Instead, the equivalent of a reef was the removal of the bonnet.{{sfn|Harland|1984|p=137}}}}

{{term|term=booby|content=[[booby]]}}
{{defn|A type of bird that has little fear and therefore is particularly easy to catch.}}

{{term|booby hatch}}
{{defn|A raised framework or hood like covering over a small {{gli|hatchway}} on a ship.}}

{{term|term=boom|content=[[boom (navigational barrier)|boom]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A floating barrier to control navigation into and out of rivers and harbors.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|spar}} attached to the foot of a fore-and-aft sail.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}}}}
{{defn|no=3|A spar to extend the foot of gaffsail, trysail or jib.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}}}}
{{defn|no=3|A spar to extend the yards of {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} masts to allow the carrying of {{nautical term|studding sail|studding sails}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}}}}

{{term|boom defence vessel}}
{{defn|An alternative term for a {{nautical term|net laying ship}}.}}

{{term|boomer}}
{{defn|Slang term in the US Navy for a [[ballistic missile submarine]].}}

{{term|boom crutch}}
{{defn|A frame in which the {{gli|boom}} rests when the {{nautical term|sail}} is not {{gli|hoist|hoisted}}.}}

{{term|boom gallows}}
{{defn|A raised crossmember that supports a {{gli|boom}} when the sail is lowered (and which obviates the need for a {{nautical term|topping lift}}).}}

{{term|term=boomie|content=boomie {{anchor|booms'l rig|boomie or booms'l rig}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''booms'l rig'''.}}
A {{gli|ketch}}-rigged barge with {{gli|gaff}} (instead of {{nautical term|spritsail}}) and {{gli|boom}} on main and {{nautical term|mizzen}}. Booms'l rig could also refer to cutter-rigged early barges.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=boom vang|content=[[boom vang]] {{anchor|vang}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''vang'''.}}
A sail control that lets one apply downward tension on a boom, countering the upward tension provided by the sail. The boom vang adds an element of control to sail shape when the sheet is let out enough that it no longer pulls the boom down. Boom vang tension helps control leech twist, a primary component of sail power.}}

{{term|boomkin}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|bumpkin}}.}}}}

{{term|booms}}
{{defn|Masts or yards, lying on board in reserve.}}

{{term|boot-top}}
{{defn|The area on the ship's {{gli|hull}} along the {{nautical term|waterline}}, usually painted a contrasting color.}}

{{term|term=bore|content=bore {{anchor|bore up|bore away}}}}
{{defn|(As in, '''bore up''' or '''bore away''') To assume a position to engage, or disengage, the enemy ships.}}

{{term|bosun}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|boatswain}}.}}}}

{{term|bosun's call}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|boatswain's call}}.}}}}

{{term|bosun's chair}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|boatswain's chair}}.}}}}

{{term|bosun's pipe}}
{{term|bosun's whistle|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|boatswain's call}}.}}}}

{{term|term=bottlescrew|content=[[turnbuckle|bottlescrew]]}}
{{defn|A device for adjusting tension in stays, shrouds and similar lines.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|bottom}}
{{defn|no=1|The underside of a vessel; the portion of a vessel that is always underwater.}}
{{defn|no=2|A ship, most often a [[cargo ship]].}}
{{defn|no=3|A cargo hold.}}

{{term|term=bottomry|content=[[bottomry]]}}
{{defn|Pledging a ship as security in a financial transaction.}}

{{term|term=bow|content=[[bow (watercraft)|bow]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The front of a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|Either side of the front (or bow) of the vessel, i.e. the ''port bow'' and ''starboard bow''. Something ahead and to the left of the vessel is "off the port bow", while something ahead and to the right of the vessel is "off the starboard bow". When "bow" is used in this way, the front of the vessel sometimes is called her ''bows'' (plural), a collective reference to her port and starboard bows synonymous with ''bow'' (singular).}}

{{term|bow chaser}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|chase gun}}.}}}}

{{term|term=bowline|content=[[bowline]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A type of knot producing a strong loop of a fixed size, topologically similar to a sheet bend.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|A rope attached to the side of a sail to pull it towards the {{gli|bow}} (for keeping the windward edge of the sail steady).<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=3|A rope attached to the {{gli|foresail}} to hold it {{gli|aback}} when {{nautical term|tacking}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=4|"Sailing on a bowline" means sailing to weather close-hauled.}}

{{term|bowman}}
{{defn|The person, in a team or among oarsmen, positioned nearest the {{gli|bow}}.}}

{{term|bowpicker}}
{{defn|A {{gli|gillnetter}} that fishes by deploying a [[gillnet]] from her {{gli|bow}}.}}

{{term|bowse}}
{{defn|To pull or hoist.}}

{{term|term=bow sea|content=[[bow sea]]}}
{{defn|Seas approaching a vessel from between 15° and 75° to port or starboard.<ref name="Saunders V3 1965" >{{cite book|title=Hydrodynamics in ship design |volume=3 |chapter=10: Definitions and nomenclature for Seakeeping |page=156 |publisher=Society of Naval Architects and Marine Engineers |location=New York, NY |year=1965 |first=Harold E. |last=Saunders |editor-first=Robert |editor-last=Taggart }}</ref> }}

{{term|bows on}}
{{defn|Said of a vessel directly approaching an observer, e.g., "The ship approached us ''bows on''." }}

{{term|term=bowsprit|content=[[bowsprit]]}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|spar}} projecting from the {{gli|bow}} that is used as an anchor for the {{gli|forestay}} and other rigging. On a barge it may be pivoted so it may be steeved up in harbor.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|bows under}}
{{defn|Said of a vessel shipping water over her {{gli|bow}}, e.g., "The ship was ''bows under'' during the storm." }}

{{term|term=bow thruster|content=[[bow thruster]]}}
{{defn|A small propeller or water-jet at the bow, used for manoeuvring larger vessels at slow speed. May be mounted externally, or in a tunnel running through the bow from side to side.}}

{{term|term=bow visor|content=[[bow visor]]}}
{{defn|A feature of some ships, particularly ferries and roll-on/roll-off ships, that allows a vessel's bow to articulate up and down to provide access to her cargo ramp and storage deck near the waterline.}}

{{term|bow wave}}
{{defn|The wave created on either side of a vessel's bow as she moves through the water.}}

{{term|bow windshield|content=[[bow windshield]]}}
{{defn|A structure fitted on the bow of some containerships to decrease aerodynamic drag and improve fuel efficiency.}}

{{term|term=boxing the compass|content=[[boxing the compass]]}}
{{defn|To state all 32 points of the compass, starting at north and proceeding clockwise. Sometimes applied to a wind that is constantly shifting.}}

{{term|term=boy seaman|content=[[boy seaman]]}}
{{defn|A young sailor, still in training.}}

{{term|brace}}
{{defn|On {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ships, a {{gli|line}} attached to the end of a {{nautical term|yard}} to rotate it around a vertical axis, for {{nautical term|trim|trimming}} the {{nautical term|sail}}. Braces are fitted in pairs to each yard, one at each end.}}

{{term|brace abox}}
{{defn|To bring the {{gli|foreyard|foreyards}} flat {{gli|aback}} to stop the ship.}}

{{term|term=brail|content=[[brail]]}}
{{defn|no=1|To furl a sail by pulling it in towards the mast and/or to the yard or gaff on which it is set . Where the brailing action is mostly moving towards the mast, it is termed "brailing in". If the sail is generally moving up to a spar, that is called "brailing up".{{sfn|Harland|1984|pp=31–33}}}}
{{defn|no=2|A line used to haul the edges or corners of a sail up or in, either preparatory to furling or as the act of furling the sail. Some brails do not have a more specific name, especially on a fore and aft sail. In other cases, {{gli|clewlines}}, [[Clewlines and buntlines|buntlines]] and leechlines may be considered types of brails.{{sfn|Harland|1984|pp=31–33, 84}}}}

{{term|brail net}}
{{defn|A type of net incorporating {{gli|brail}} lines on a small fishing net on a boat.}}

{{term|brailer}}
{{defn|A device consisting of a net of small-mesh webbing attached to a frame, used aboard fishing vessels for unloading large quantities of fish.}}

{{term|brake}}
{{defn|The handle of the pump, by which it is worked.}}

{{term|term=brass monkey|content=brass monkey, {{vanchor|brass monkey weather}}}}
{{defn|Used in the expression "it is cold enough to freeze the balls off a brass monkey".
Apocryphally, it is often claimed that a brass monkey was a frame used to hold cannon balls, and low temperature would cause the frame to contract to a greater degree than the iron balls and thus allow them to roll off.  See [[Brass monkey (colloquial expression)|brass monkey]] for the probable actual etymology.}}

{{term|brass pounder}}
{{defn|Early 20th-century slang term for a vessel's radio operator, so-called because he repeatedly struck a brass key on his transmitter to broadcast in [[Morse code]].}}

{{term|term=breachway|content=[[breachway]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The shore along a channel.}}
{{defn|no=2|The whole area around the place where a channel meets the ocean.}}

{{term|term=break bulk cargo|content=[[break bulk cargo]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''breakbulk cargo'''.}}
Goods that must be loaded aboard a ship individually and not in intermodal containers or in bulk, carried by a general cargo ship.}}

{{term|breaker}}
{{defn|no=1|A shallow portion of a reef over which waves break.}}
{{defn|no=2|A [[breaking wave]] that breaks into foam against the shore, a shoal, a rock or a reef. Sailors use breakers to warn themselves of their vessel's proximity to an underwater hazard to navigation or, at night or during periods of poor visibility, of their vessel's proximity to shore.}}
{{defn|no=3|A [[ship breaker]], often used in the plural, e.g. "The old ship went to the breakers".}}
{{defn|no=4|A small cask of liquid kept permanently in a {{nautical term|ship's boat}} in case of becoming separated from the ship or if used as a lifeboat.}}

{{term|breakwater}}
{{defn|no=1|A structure constructed on a coast as part of a coastal defense system or to protect an {{gli|anchorage}} from the effects of weather and [[longshore drift]].}}
{{defn|no=2|A structure built on the {{gli|forecastle}} of a ship intended to divert water away from the forward superstructure or gun mounts.}}

{{term|term=breeches buoy|content=[[breeches buoy]]}}
{{defn|A ring [[lifebuoy]] fitted with canvas breeches, functionally similar to a [[zip line]], used to transfer people from one ship to another or to rescue people from a wrecked or sinking ship by moving them to another ship or to the shore.}}

{{term|breast hook}}
{{defn|A horizontal {{nautical term|knee}} that joins the sides of a traditional wooden hull to the {{nautical term|stem}}, reinforcing their connection.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|breastrope}}
{{defn|A mooring rope fastened anywhere on a ship's side that goes directly to the quay, so that it is roughly at right angles to both.{{sfn|Mayne|2000}}}}

{{term|term=bridge|content=[[bridge (nautical)|bridge]]}}
{{defn|A structure above the weather deck, extending the full width of the vessel, which houses a command center, itself called by association ''the bridge''.}}

{{term|term=bridge wing|content=[[Bridge (nautical)#Bridge wing|bridge wing]]}}
{{defn|A narrow walkway extending outward from both sides of a {{nautical term|pilothouse}} to the full width of a ship or slightly beyond, to allow {{gli|bridge}} personnel a full view to aid in the maneuvering of the ship, such as when docking.}}

{{term|term=brig|content=[[brig]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A vessel with two {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} masts.}}
{{defn|no=2|An American term for an interior area of a ship that is used to detain prisoners (possibly [[prisoners-of-war]], in wartime) or {{nautical term|stowaway|stowaways}}, and to punish delinquent crew members. Usually resembles a prison cell with bars and a locked, hinged door.}}

{{term|term=brig sloop|content=[[brig sloop]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{nautical term|sloop-of-war}} introduced in the 1770s that had two {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} masts like a {{gli|brig}} (in contrast to ''ship sloops'' of the time, which had three masts).}}

{{term|term=brigantine|content=[[brigantine]] {{anchor|hermaphrodite brig}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''hermaphrodite brig'''.}}
A two-masted vessel, {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} on the {{gli|foremast}} but {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} on the {{nautical term|mainmast}}.}}

{{term|term=brightwork|content=[[brightwork]]}}
{{defn|Exposed varnished wood on a boat or ship.{{sfn|Mayne|2000}}<!-- Note that the cited reference comments that this is one of the few cases where the Oxford English Dictionary is in error – brightwork is '''not''' polished metalwork.-->}}

{{term|bring to}}
{{defn|To cause a ship to be stationary by arranging the sails.}}

{{term|term=broach|content=[[Broach (nautical)|broach]]}}
{{defn|no=1|When a sailing or power vessel loses directional control when travelling with a following sea. The vessel turns sideways to the wind and waves and in more serious cases may {{gli|capsize}} or {{nautical term|pitchpole}}. Advice on dealing with heavy weather includes various strategies for avoiding this happening.<ref name="Read 2014b">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014b |c= B |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms//b-16190}}</ref><ref name="Ocean Navigator">{{cite web |title=Powering in Heavy Weather |url=http://www.oceannavigator.com/September-2010/Powering-in-heavy-weather/ |website=Ocean Navigator |date=15 September 2010 |access-date=2 December 2019 |archive-date=2019-12-02  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191202202835/http://www.oceannavigator.com/September-2010/Powering-in-heavy-weather/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=2|An unintentional appearance above the surface of all or part of a submerged {{nautical term|submarine}}, a dangerous event when the submarine is in proximity to enemy forces or near any ship which might collide with her.}}

{{term|broad}}
{{defn|Wide in appearance from the vantage point of a lookout or other person viewing activity in the vicinity of a ship, e.g. another ship off the {{nautical term|starboard}} {{gli|bow}} with her side facing the viewer's ship could be described as "broad on the starboard bow" of the viewer's ship.}}

{{term|term=Broad Fourteens|content=[[Broad Fourteens]]}}
{{defn|An area of the southern [[North Sea]] which is fairly consistently 14 [[fathom]]s (84 feet; 26 metres) deep. On a nautical chart with depths indicated in fathoms, it appears as a broad area with many "14" notations.}}

{{term|broadhorn}}
{{defn|An alternate term for a {{gli|flatboat}}.}}

{{term|term=broadside|content=[[Broadside (naval)|broadside]]}}
{{defn|no=1|One side of a vessel above the {{nautical term|waterline}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|All the guns on one side of a warship or mounted (in rotating turrets or {{gli|barbette|barbettes}}) so as to be able to fire on the same side of a warship.}}
{{defn|no=3|The simultaneous firing of all the guns on one side of a warship or able to fire on the same side of a warship.}}
{{defn|no=4|''Weight of broadside'': the combined weight of all projectiles a ship can fire in a broadside engagement, or the combined weight of all the shells which a group of ships that have formed a {{gli|line of battle}} can collectively fire on the same side.}}

{{term|term=Brouwer Route|content=[[Brouwer Route]]}}
{{defn| A route used by ships in the 17th century while sailing east from the [[Cape of Good Hope]] to the [[Netherlands East Indies]] which took advantage of the strong westerly winds in the southern [[Indian Ocean]] known as the "[[Roaring Forties]]" to speed the trip but required ships to turn north in the eastern Indian Ocean to reach the East Indies. With no accurate means of determining [[longitude]] at the time, ships which missed the northward turn ran the risk of being wrecked on the west coast of Australia.}}

{{term|brow}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|gangplank}}.}}}}

{{term|brown water}}
{{defn|no=1|A collective term for rivers and coastal waters.}}
{{defn|no=2|Maritime waters which lie over the [[continental shelf]].}}

{{term|brown-water navy}}
{{defn|no=1|A navy capable of operating on rivers and/or in coastal environments.}}
{{defn|no=2|That portion of a navy designed and intended to operate on rivers and/or in coastal environments.}}
{{defn|no=3|A navy whose capabilities limit it to operating on rivers and/or in coastal environments.}}

{{term|bucket}}
{{defn|Alternative name for a paddle on a paddlewheel.}}

{{term|buffer}}
{{defn|The chief {{gli|boatswain|bosun}}'s mate (in the Royal Navy), responsible for discipline.}}

{{term|term=bug shoe|content=[[bug shoe]]}}
{{defn|A length of hardened material placed on a {{nautical term|skeg}} to protect the skeg from damage by [[shipworm]]s.<ref>{{cite web|title=AFSC Historical Corner: ''Scoter'', the Agency's Bristol Bay Boat|url=https://www.afsc.noaa.gov/history/vessels/boats/scoter.htm|website=NOAA|access-date=6 April 2018}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=bugeye|content=[[bugeye]]}}
{{defn|A type of sailboat developed in the Chesapeake Bay by the early 1880s for oyster dredging, superseded as the chief oystering boat in the bay by the {{nautical term|skipjack}} at the end of the 19th century.}}

{{term|term=bulbous bow|content=[[bulbous bow]]}}
{{defn|A protruding bulb at the {{gli|bow}} of a ship just below the {{nautical term|waterline}} which modifies the way water flows around the hull, reducing drag and thus increasing speed, range, fuel efficiency and stability.}}

{{term|term=bulk cargo|content=[[bulk cargo]]}}
{{defn|Commodity cargo that is transported unpackaged in large quantities.}}

{{term|term=bulk carrier|content=[[bulk carrier]] {{anchor|bulk freighter|bulker}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bulk freighter''' or '''bulker'''.}}
A merchant ship specially designed to transport unpackaged {{gli|bulk cargo}} in its cargo holds.}}

{{term|term=bulkhead|content=[[bulkhead (partition)|bulkhead]]}}
{{defn|An upright wall within the {{gli|hull}} of a ship, particularly a watertight, load-bearing wall.}}

{{term|bull ensign}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''boot ensign''' or '''George ensign'''.}}
The senior ensign of a US Navy command (i.e., a ship, squadron or shore activity).}}

{{term|term=bullseye|content=[[porthole|bullseye]]}}
{{defn|A glass window above the captain's cabin to allow viewing of the sails above deck.}}

{{term|bulward}}
{{term|bulwark|multi=y}}
{{defn|
[[File:Bulwark2 (PSF).png|thumb|Bulwark (or bulward)]]
The extension of a ship's side above the level of the {{nautical term|weather deck}}.}}

{{term|term=bumboat|content=[[bumboat]]}}
{{defn|A private boat selling goods.}}

{{term|term=bumpkin|content=[[boomkin|bumpkin]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''boomkin'''}}
1.&nbsp;A {{nautical term|spar}}, similar to a {{gli|bowsprit}}, but which projects from the {{nautical term|stern}} rather than the {{gli|bow}}. May be used to attach the {{gli|backstay}} or mizzen sheets{{Sfn|Underhill|1955|p=107}}
}}
{{defn|no=2|An iron bar projecting outboard from a ship's side to which the lower and {{nautical term|topsail}} brace blocks are sometimes hooked.}}

{{term|bunk}}
{{defn|A built-in bed on board ship.}}

{{term|term=bunker|content=[[Fuel bunker|bunker]]}}
{{defn|A container for storing coal or fuel oil for a ship's engine.}}

{{term|term=bunker fuel|content=[[bunker fuel]]}}{{anchor|bunkers}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bunkers'''}}
Fuel oil for a ship.}}

{{term|bunt}}
{{defn|no=1|Middle cloths of a square sail.{{Sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}
{{defn|no=2|Centre of a furled square sail.{{Sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|bunt-gasket}}
{{defn| Canvas apron used to fasten the bunt of a square sail to the yard when furled.{{Sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|term=bunting tosser|content=[[bunting tosser]]}}
{{defn|A signalman who prepares and flies flag hoists. Also known in the United States Navy as a ''skivvy waver''.}}

{{term|term=buntline|content=[[clewlines and buntlines|buntline]]}}
{{defn|One of the lines leading from the foot of a square sail over a block at the head and down to the deck; and used to haul it up to the yard when furling.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|term=buoy|content=[[buoy]]}}
{{defn|A floating object, usually anchored at a given position and fulfilling one of a number of uses, recognised by a defined shape and color for each, including {{gli|aids to navigation}}, warnings of danger such as submerged wrecks or divers, or for attaching mooring lines, [[lobster pot]]s, etc.}}

{{term|buoyed up}}
{{defn|Lifted by a {{gli|buoy}}, especially a cable that has been lifted to prevent it from trailing on the bottom.}}

{{term|term=burthen|content=[[Builder's Old Measurement|burthen]]}}
{{defn|The [[Builder's Old Measurement]], expressed in "tons bm" or "tons BOM", a volumetric measurement of cubic cargo capacity, ''not'' of weight. This is the [[tonnage]] of a ship, based on the number of [[Tun (unit)|tuns]] of wine that it could carry in its holds. One 252-gallon tun of wine takes up approximately 100 cubic feet, and weighs 2,240 lbs (1 [[long ton]], or Imperial ton).}}

{{term|term=burgee|content=[[burgee]]}}
{{defn|A small flag, typically triangular, flown from the masthead of a yacht to indicate yacht-club membership.}}

{{term|term=burgoo|content=[[burgoo]]}}
{{defn|A dish of ships biscuit crumbs and minced salt pork, usually a meal of last resort for officers when other food stores are exhausted.}}

{{term|butt}}
{{defn|Where the butt of one plank joins with the butt of another.}}

{{term|by and large}}
{{defn|''By'' means into the wind, while ''large'' means with the wind. "By and large" is therefore used to indicate all possible situations, e.g. "the ship handles well both by and large".}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/by+and+large|title=Definition of BY AND LARGE|date=10 March 2026|website=www.merriam-webster.com}}</ref>

{{term|by the board}}
{{defn|Anything that has gone {{nautical term|overboard}}.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==C==
{{Glossary}}
{{term|term=cabin|content=[[Cabin (ship)|cabin]]}}
{{defn|An enclosed structure with at least one room on a {{gli|deck}} or flat, especially one used as living quarters.}}

{{term|term=cabin boy|content=[[cabin boy]]}}
{{defn|An attendant to passengers and crew, often a young man.}}

{{term|term=cabin cruiser|content=[[cabin cruiser]]}}
{{defn|A type of [[power boat|powered]] pleasure craft that provides accommodation for its crew and passengers inside the structure of the craft. A cabin cruiser usually is {{convert|7.6|to|13.7|m|0}} in length, with a powered pleasure craft larger than that considered a [[motor yacht]].}}

{{term|cabin sole}}
{{defn|A walking surface inside a vessel.}}

{{term|term=cable|content=[[Wire rope|cable]]}}
{{defn|no=1|An especially large or thick rope.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|cable length}}.}}

{{term|term=cable length|content=[[cable length]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Sometimes simply called a '''cable'''.}}
A measure of length or distance equivalent to {{convert|1/10|nmi|ft m|0|abbr=off}} in the United Kingdom and {{convert|100|fathom|ft m|0|abbr=off}} in the United States; other countries use different equivalents.}}

{{term|term=caboose|content=[[Caboose (ship's galley)|caboose]]}}
{{defn|A small ship's kitchen or {{gli|galley}} on deck.}}

{{term|term=cabotage|content=[[cabotage]]}}
{{defn|The transport of goods or passengers between two points in the same country along coastal routes by a vessel registered in another country. Originally applied only to shipping, the term now also is applied to analogous transport via [[aviation]], [[railway]]s, or [[road transport]].}}

{{term|term=cage mast|content=[[cage mast]]}}
{{defn|See ''{{gli|lattice mast}}''.}}

{{term|camels}}
{{defn|no=1|Loaded vessels lashed tightly, one on each side of another vessel, and then emptied to provide additional buoyancy that reduces the {{gli|draft}} of the ship in the middle.}}
{{defn|no=2|Floating platforms brought alongside for use by yard workers or crew.}}

{{term|can}}
{{defn|A type of {{gli|aid to navigation|navigational}} {{gli|buoy}}, often a vertical drum, but otherwise always square in silhouette, colored red in [[lateral mark#Region A|IALA region A]] (Europe, Africa, Greenland, and most of Asia and Oceania) or green in [[lateral mark#Region B|IALA region B]] (the Americas, Japan, Korea, and the Philippines). In channel marking its use is opposite that of a {{nautical term|nun buoy}}.}}

{{term|canal boat}}
{{defn|A specialized watercraft designed for operation on a [[canal]]. During the [[Age of Sail]], canal boats typically lacked {{nautical term|sail}}s and {{nautical term|mast}}s and relied on {{nautical term|towboat}}s and [[mule]]s to move from place to place.<ref name=canalschooner>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lcmm.org/visit/lois-mcclure/history-of-sailing-canal-schooners/|title=A Brief History of Sailing Canal Schooners – Lake Champlain Maritime Museum|website=www.lcmm.org}}</ref>}}

{{term|canal schooner}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''sailing canal boat''' or '''sailing canal schooner'''.}}
A specialized type of {{gli|canal boat}} developed in [[North America]] in the early 19th century and used on the [[Great Lakes]] and in [[Lake Champlain]]. Unlike conventional canal boats of the era, which lacked a means of propulsion, canal schooners had a {{nautical term|schooner}} {{nautical term|rigging|rig}} which allowed them to {{nautical term|sailing|sail}} from place to place, but could lower their {{nautical term|mast}}s and raise their {{gli|centreboard}}s, allowing [[mule]]s to tow them through [[canal]]s. The design allowed their operators to save money by reducing their reliance on [[towing]] and paying fewer towing charges.<ref name=canalschooner/>}}

{{term|canaller}}
{{defn|A ship designed to transit the locks of the [[Welland Canal]].}}

{{term|term=canister shot|content=[[canister shot]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''langrage''' or simply '''canister'''.}}
A type of antipersonnel cannon load in which lead balls or other loose metallic items were enclosed in a tin or iron shell. On firing, the shell would disintegrate, releasing the smaller metal objects with a shotgun-like effect.}}

{{term|canoe stern}}
{{defn|A design for the {{nautical term|stern}} of a {{nautical term|yacht}} such that it is pointed like a {{gli|bow}}, rather than squared off as a {{nautical term|transom}}.}}

{{term|canvas}}
{{defn|A collective term for all of the {{nautical term|sail|sails}} on a vessel; the total area of all sails aboard her may be expressed as the area of her canvas.}}

{{term|cap}}
{{defn|A fitting or band used to connect the head of one {{nautical term|mast}} to the lower portion of the mast above.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|Cape Horn fever}}
{{defn|A feigned illness from which a [[malingering|malingerer]] is pretending to suffer.}}

{{term|Cape Horn roller}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''graybeard'''.}}
A type of large ocean wave commonly encountered in the stormy seas of the [[Southern Ocean]] south of South America's [[Cape Horn]], often exceeding {{convert|60|ft|m|1}} in height. The geography of the Southern Ocean, uninterrupted by continents, creates an endless {{gli|fetch}} that is favorable for the propagation of such waves.}}

{{term|cap-stay}}
{{defn|A {{gli|backstay}} leading from a mast {{gli|cap}} to the ship's side.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|term=capital ship|content=[[capital ship]]}}
{{defn|One of a set of ships considered a navy's most important warships, generally possessing the heaviest firepower and armor and traditionally much larger than other naval vessels, but not formally defined. During the [[Age of Sail]], capital ships were generally understood to be [[Ship of the line|ships of the line]]; during the second half of the 19th century and the 20th century, they were typically [[battleship]]s and [[battlecruiser]]s; and since the mid-20th century, the term may also include [[aircraft carrier]]s and [[ballistic missile submarine]]s.}}

{{term|term=capsize|content=[[capsize]]}}
{{defn|(of a vessel) To {{gli|list}} so severely that the vessel rolls over, exposing the {{gli|keel}}. On large vessels, this often results in the sinking of the ship. Compare ''{{nautical term|turtling}}''.}}

{{term|term=capstan|content=[[capstan (nautical)|capstan]]}}
{{defn|A large {{nautical term|winch}} with a vertical axis used to wind in {{gli|anchor|anchors}} or to hoist other heavy objects, and sometimes to administer flogging over. A full-sized human-powered capstan is a waist-high cylindrical machine, operated by a number of hands who each insert a horizontal ''capstan bar'' in holes in the capstan and walk in a circle.}}

{{term|captain}}
{{defn|no=1|The person lawfully in command of a vessel. "[[Captain (nautical)|Captain]]" is an informal title of respect given to the commander of a naval vessel regardless of formal rank; aboard a merchant ship, the ship's captain is called her ''master''.}}
{{defn|no=2|A [[captain (naval)|naval officer]] with a rank between [[commander]] and [[commodore (rank)|commodore]].}}
{{defn|no=3|In the US Navy, US Coast Guard, [[United States Public Health Service Commissioned Corps|US Public Health Service]], and [[NOAA Commissioned Officer Corps|National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration]], a [[commissioned officer]] of a grade superior to a [[Commander (United States)|commander]] and junior to a [[Rear admiral (United States)|rear admiral (lower half)]], equal in grade or rank to a US Army, US Marine Corps, or US Air Force, or US Space Force colonel.}}

{{term|term=Captain of the Port|content=[[Captain of the Port]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In the United Kingdom, a Royal Navy officer, usually a [[Captain (Royal Navy)|captain]], responsible for the day-to-day operation of a naval {{gli|dockyard}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|In the United States, a US Coast Guard officer, usually a [[Captain (United States O-6)|captain]], responsible for enforcement of safety, security, and marine environmental protection regulations in a commercial port.}}

{{term|captain's daughter}}
{{defn|Another name for the {{gli|cat o' nine tails}}, which in principle is only used on board on the captain's (or a court martial's) personal orders.}}

{{term|term=car carrier|content=[[car carrier]]}}
{{defn|A cargo ship specially designed or fitted to carry large numbers of automobiles. Modern ''pure car carriers'' have a fully enclosed, box-like superstructure that extends along the entire length and across the entire breadth of the ship, enclosing the automobiles. The similar ''pure car/truck carrier'' can also accommodate trucks.}}

{{term|term=car float|content=[[car float]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''railroad car float''' or '''rail barge'''.}}
An unpowered {{gli|barge}} with railroad tracks mounted on its deck, used to move railroad cars across water obstacles.}}

{{term|term=caravel|content=[[caravel]] {{anchor|caravelle}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''caravelle'''.}}
A small, highly maneuverable sailing ship with a {{gli|lateen rigging|lateen rig}}, used by the Portuguese in the 15th and 16th centuries to explore along the West African coast and into the Atlantic Ocean.}}

{{term|cardinal}}
{{defn|Referring to the four main points of the compass: north, south, east, and west. See also ''{{gli|bearing}}''.}}

{{term|term=careening|content=[[careening]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''heaving down'''.}}
Tilting a ship on its side, usually when {{gli|beaching|beached}}, to clean or repair the {{gli|hull}} below the {{nautical term|waterline}}.}}

{{term|term=cargo liner|content=[[cargo liner]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''passenger-cargo ship''' or '''passenger-cargoman'''.}}
A type of merchant ship that became common just after the middle of the 19th century, configured primarily for the transportation of general cargo but also for the transportation of at least some passengers. Almost completely replaced by more specialized cargo ships during the second half of the 20th century.}}

{{term|term=cargo ship|content=[[cargo ship]]}}
{{defn|Any ship or vessel that carries cargo, goods, and materials from one port to another, including general cargo ships (designed to carry {{gli|break bulk cargo}}), {{gli|bulk carrier|bulk carriers}}, {{gli|container ship|container ships}}, multipurpose vessels, and {{nautical term|tanker|tankers}}. Tankers, however, although technically cargo ships, are routinely thought of as constituting a completely separate category.}}

{{term|cargoman}}
{{defn|A {{gli|cargo ship}}.}}

{{term|carpenter}}
{{defn|no=1|In the [[Age of Sail]], a warrant officer responsible for the {{gli|hull}}, {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, {{nautical term|spar|spars}}, and {{nautical term|ship's boat|boats}} of a vessel, and also for sounding the well to see if the vessel was {{nautical term|making water}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A senior rating responsible for all of the woodwork aboard a vessel.}}

{{term|carpenter's walk}}
{{defn|On a [[tall ship]], a is a narrow unlit passageway or [[bulkhead (partition)|bulkhead]] often with a low (four-foot) ceiling that is fitted around the hull at its waterline.  The carpenter's walk allowed the [[ship's carpenter]] to tour the entire waterline area of a ship to inspect it for water leaks.<ref>{{cite book|title=Heart of Oak: A Sailor's Life in Nelson's Navy|author=James McGuire|date=2002|page=146}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|title=Sovereign of the Seas, 1637|author=John McKay|date=2020}}</ref>  Because of its dark and seldom-visited nature and location far below decks, it was also sometimes used by [[mutiny|mutinous]] sailors as a secluded place to plan a rebellion against the ship's officers.<ref>{{cite book|title=Stephen Biesty's Cross-Sections Man-of-War|author=Richard Platt|date=2019|page=23}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=carrack|content=[[carrack]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''nau'''.}}
A three- or four-masted oceangoing sailing ship used by Western Europeans in the Atlantic Ocean from the 15th through the early 17th centuries.}}

{{term|carrier}}
{{defn|An {{gli|aircraft carrier}}.}}

{{term|term=carronade|content=[[carronade]]}}
{{defn|A short, smoothbore, cast-iron naval cannon, used from the 1770s to the 1850s as a powerful, short-range, anti-ship and anti-crew weapon.}}

{{term|carry away}}
{{defn|To suddenly break a {{nautical term|spar}}, rope or other part of a ship's equipment. May be caused, for example, by overloading, stress of weather, collision or battle damage.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}{{sfn|Mayne|2000}}{{r|smyth}}}}

{{term|carry on}}
{{defn|Resume work or assigned duties.<ref name="NHHC"/>}}

{{term|term=cartel|content=[[Cartel (ship)|cartel]]}}
{{defn|A ship employed on humanitarian voyages, in particular to carry communications or prisoners between belligerents during wartime. A cartel flies distinctive flags, including a [[flag of truce]], traditionally is unarmed except for a lone signaling gun, and under [[international law]] is not subject to seizure or capture during her outbound and return voyages as long as she engages in no warlike acts.}}

{{term|term=carvel-built|content=[[carvel (boat building)|carvel-built]]}}
{{defn|
[[File:Clinker-carvel.svg|thumb|right|A comparison of the '''{{gli|clinker-built|clinker}}''' and '''{{gli|carvel-built|carvel}}''' styles of boat construction]]
A method of constructing a wooden {{gli|hull}} in which planks are butted edge-to-edge on a robust frame, so giving a smooth hull surface; traditionally the planks are not attached to each other, only to the frame, and have only a {{gli|caulk|caulking}} sealant between them to make them watertight.{{sfn|March|1972|p=359}} Contrast ''{{gli|clinker-built}}''.}}

{{term|term=casing|content=[[Casing (submarine)|casing]]}}
{{defn|A light metal structure, usually incorporating a {{gli|deck}}, built over the upper surface of a {{nautical term|submarine}}'s [[Submarine hull#Pressure hull|pressure hull]] to create a flat surface on which crew members can walk. A feature of submarines built prior to the mid-20th century, but not of more modern submarines.}}

{{term|cat}}
{{defn|no=1|To prepare an {{gli|anchor}} after raising it by lifting it with a {{nautical term|tackle}} to the {{gli|cathead}}, prior to securing (''fishing'') it alongside for sea. An anchor raised to the cathead is said to be ''catted''.}}
{{defn|no=2|The {{gli|cat o' nine tails}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|A {{gli|cat rigging|cat-rigged}} boat or {{gli|catboat}}.}}

{{term|term=cat o' nine tails|content=[[cat o' nine tails]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''the cat'''.}}
A short, multi-tailed whip or flail kept by the {{gli|boatswain|bosun}}'s mate to [[Flagellation|flog]] sailors (and soldiers in the army) who had committed infractions while at sea. When not in use, the cat was often kept in a baize bag, a possible origin for the term "cat out of the bag".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.phrases.org.uk/meanings/let-the-cat-out-of-the-bag.html|title='Let the cat out of the bag' – the meaning and origin of this phrase|first=Gary|last=Martin|website=Phrasefinder|date=11 December 2023 }}</ref> "Not enough room to swing a cat" also derives from this.}}

{{term|term=catamaran|content=[[catamaran]]}}
{{defn|Any vessel with two {{gli|hull|hulls}}. Compare ''{{nautical term|trimaran}}''.}}

{{term|term=catboat|content=[[catboat]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|cat rigging|cat-rigged}} vessel with a single mast mounted close to the {{gli|bow}} and only one sail, usually on a {{gli|gaff rig|gaff}}.}}

{{term|term=catenary|content=catenary {{anchor|catenary curve}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''catenary curve'''.}}
The curve of a deployed anchor chain.<ref>{{cite web|website=www.sailmagazine.com|url=https://www.sailmagazine.com/cruising/anchoring-principles|title=Anchoring Principals|date=July 20, 2004|first=Jim|last=Hancock|access-date=April 19, 2022}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=catharpin|content=[[catharpin]]}}
{{defn|A short rope or iron clamp used to brace in the {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}} toward the masts so as to give a freer sweep to the {{nautical term|yard|yards}}.}}

{{term|term=cathead|content=[[cathead]]}}
{{defn|A beam extending out from the hull used to support an {{gli|anchor}} when raised in order to secure or "fish" it.}}

{{term|term=cat's paws|content=[[Cat's paw (wave)|cat's paws]]}}
{{defn|Light, variable winds on calm waters producing scattered areas of small waves.}}

{{term|term=caulk|content=[[caulk]]}}
{{defn|To create a {{nautical term|watertight}} seal between structures. In traditional {{gli|carvel-built|carvel}} construction, this involved hammering {{nautical term|oakum}} (recycled rope fibres) or caulking cotton into the slightly tapered fine gaps between the hull or deck planks and, in older methods, covering with tar. The expansion of the fibres in water tightens up the hull, making it less prone to racking movement, as well as making the joint watertight.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|term=celestial navigation|content=[[celestial navigation]]}}
{{defn|Navigation by the position of celestial objects, including the stars, Sun, and Moon, using tools aboard ship such as a [[sextant]], [[Marine chronometer|chronometer]], and compass, as well as [[ephemerides|published tables]] of the expected positions of celestial objects on specific dates. Celestial navigation was the primary method of navigation until the development of electronic global positioning systems such as [[LORAN]] and [[GPS]].}}

{{term|ceiling}}
{{defn|Planking attached to the inside of the {{gli|frame|frames}} or {{gli|floor|floors}} of a wooden hull. It serves to separate the cargo from the hull planking itself, but also has a structural role, contributing to the strength of the hull.. The ceiling has different names in different places; e.g. {{gli|limber boards}}, {{nautical term|spirketting}}, {{nautical term|quickwork}}, etc. The lower part of the ceiling is, confusingly to a landsman, what you are standing on at the bottom of the {{gli|hold}} of a wooden ship.{{sfn|March|1972|p=359}}{{r|Adams 2013|at=glossary}}}}

{{term|term=center of effort|content={{lang|en-US|center of effort}} {{small|[[American English]]}} {{anchor|center of pressure}}}}
{{term|term=centre of effort|content={{lang|en-GB|centre of effort}} {{small|[[British English]]}} {{anchor|centre of pressure}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''center of pressure''' (American spelling) or '''centre of pressure''' (British spelling).}}
The point of origin of net aerodynamic force upon a {{nautical term|sail}}, roughly located in the geometric center of the sail, though the actual position of the center of effort will vary with {{nautical term|sail plan}}, sail trim, or airfoil profile, boat trim, and {{nautical term#points of sail|point of sail}}.}}

{{term|term=center of lateral resistance|content={{lang|en-US|center of lateral resistance}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=centre of lateral resistance|content={{lang|en-GB|centre of lateral resistance}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|The point of origin of net hydrodynamic resistance on the submerged structure of a boat, especially a sailboat. This is the pivot point the boat turns about when unbalanced external forces are applied, similar to the [[center of gravity]]. On a perfectly balanced sailboat, the {{gli|center of effort}} will align vertically with the center of lateral resistance. If this is not the case, the boat will be unbalanced and will exhibit either {{gli|lee helm}} or {{nautical term|weather helm}} and will be difficult to control.}}

{{term|term=centerboard|content={{lang|en-US|[[centerboard]]}} {{small|[[American English]]}} {{anchor|centerplate}}}}
{{term|term=centreboard|content={{lang|en-GB|[[centreboard]]}} {{small|[[British English]]}} {{anchor|centreplate}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|
[[File:Centerboard (PSF).svg|thumb|right|Diagram of the position of a '''{{gli|centerboard}}''' on a boat]]
{{ghat|Also '''centerplate''' (American spelling), '''centreplate''' (British spelling).}}
A wooden board or metal plate which can be pivoted through a fore-and-aft slot along the {{gli|centerline}} in the {{gli|hull}} of a sailing vessel, functioning as a retractable {{gli|keel}} to help the boat resist {{gli|leeway}} by moving its {{gli|center of lateral resistance}}. Very common in {{gli|dinghy|dinghies}}, but also found in some larger boats. A {{gli|daggerboard}} serves the same purpose but slides vertically rather than pivoting.}}

{{term|term=centerline|content={{lang|en-US|centerline}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=centreline|content={{lang|en-GB|centreline}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|An imaginary line down the center of a vessel lengthwise. Any structure or anything mounted or carried on a vessel that straddles this line and is equidistant from either side of the vessel is said to be "on the centerline".}}

{{term|term=chafing|content=[[chafing (nautical)|chafing]]}}
{{defn|Wear on a line or sail caused by constant rubbing against another surface.}}

{{term|chafing gear}}
{{defn|Material applied to a line or spar to prevent or reduce {{gli|chafing}}. See ''{{gli|baggywrinkle}}'' and ''{{nautical term|puddening}}''.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|chain locker}}
{{defn|A space in the forward part of a ship, typically beneath the {{gli|bow}} in front of the foremost collision {{gli|bulkhead}}, that contains the {{gli|anchor chain}} when the {{gli|anchor}} is secured for sea.}}

{{term|term=chain-shot|content=[[chain-shot]]}}
{{defn|Cannonballs linked with short lengths of chain, designed to be especially damaging to {{nautical term|rigging}} and {{nautical term|mast|masts}}.}}

{{term|term=chain plates|content=[[chain plates]]}}
{{defn|Iron bars bolted to a ship's side to which the {{gli|deadeye|deadeyes}} or rigging screws of the lower figging and the {{gli|back-stay|back-stays}} are bolted.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|chain-wale}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''channel''' and '''chainwale'''.}}
A broad, thick plank that projects horizontally from each of a ship's sides abreast a {{nautical term|mast}} (distinguished as the fore, main, or mizzen channel accordingly), serving to extend the base for the {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}}, which support the mast.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}

{{term|term=chains|content=[[Chains (nautical)|chains]]}}
{{defn|Small platforms built into the sides of a ship to spread the {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}} to a more advantageous angle. Also used as a platform for manual {{gli|depth sounding}}.}}

{{term|''chaloupe''}}
{{defn|See {{gli|chalupa}}.}}

{{term|term=chalupa|content=[[Chalupa (boat)|chalupa]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A small boat that functions as a {{nautical term|shallop}}, {{nautical term|water taxi}}, or {{gli|gondola}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|In [[Portuguese language|Portuguese]], a small boat used for {{gli|cabotage}}, propelled by either oars or sails. Those equipped with sails have a single mast.}}
{{defn|no=3|A type of {{nautical term|whaling boat}} used by the Basques in the mid-16th century in what is now Newfoundland and Labrador.}}

{{term|change tack}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''switch tack'''.}}
To change {{gli|course}} or {{gli|heading}}.}}

{{term|channel fever}}
{{defn|no=1|The impatient excitement in a ship's crew as the end of a voyage becomes imminent. Characteristics include crew members working harder to get the ship sailing faster, off-watch personnel being on deck to keep track of progress, and everyone being packed and in their shore-going clothes (ready to be paid off) the moment the vessel arrives in port.<ref>{{cite book |title=Oxford English Dictionary |year=1939 |url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.228324 |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=Oxford }}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=2|(obsolete usage) A crew member avoiding duties with a feigned illness, usually after leaving port.}}

{{term|term=Charlie Noble|content=[[Charlie Noble (chimney)|Charlie/Charley Noble]] {{anchor|Charley Noble}}}}
{{defn|The metal stovepipe chimney from a cook shack on the deck of a ship or from a stove in a {{gli|galley}}.}}

{{term|term=|charrua|content=[[Charrua (nautical)|charrua]]}}
{{defn|A large sailing ship used as a troopship}}

{{term|term=chartered ship|content=chartered ship {{anchor|charter ship}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''charter ship'''.}}
A term used by the British [[East India Company]] from the 17th to the 19th centuries for a merchant ship it chartered to make a single, often one-way, voyage between England (later the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|United Kingdom]]) and ports east of the [[Cape of Good Hope]], a trade over which the company held a strict monopoly. A charter ship during its single voyage was employed in much the same way as what the company called an {{gli|extra ship}}, though the company usually hired charter ships on special terms and for much shorter periods.<ref name=threedecksshiproles>{{Cite web|url=https://eicships.threedecks.org/|title=East India Company Ships – The Maritime Service 1600 to 1834|website=eicships.threedecks.org|access-date=2021-12-11 |archive-date=2021-12-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211206111726/https://eicships.threedecks.org/|url-status=dead}}</ref>}}

{{term|charthouse}}
{{defn|A compartment from which the ship was navigated, especially in the Royal Navy.}}

{{term|chartplotter}}
{{defn|An electronic instrument that places the position of the ship (from a [[GPS receiver]]) onto a digital {{nautical term|nautical chart}} displayed on a monitor, thereby replacing all manual navigation functions. Chartplotters also display information collected from all shipboard electronic instruments and often directly control autopilots.}}

{{term|term=chase gun|content=[[chase gun]]}}{{anchor|chase piece|chaser}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''chase piece''' or '''chaser'''.}}
A [[cannon]] pointing forward or aft, often of longer range than other guns. Those on the bow ({{gli|bow chaser|bow chasers}}) were used to fire upon a ship ahead, while those on the rear ({{nautical term|stern chaser|stern chasers}}) were used to ward off pursuing vessels. Unlike guns pointing to the side, chasers could be brought to bear in a chase without slowing down the vessel.}}

{{term|term=chasse-marée|content={{lang|fr|[[chasse-marée]]}}}}
{{defn|A decked commercial sailing vessel engaged in the transportation of fresh fish directly from fishing grounds to ports in [[Brittany]] between the 18th century and around the third quarter of the 19th century. Three-{{nautical term|mast|masted}} [[lugger]]s replaced the vessels originally serving in this role; the luggers then were replaced successively by {{gli|dundee|dundees}}, {{gli|brig|brigs}}, and [[schooner]]s.}}

{{term|cheeks}}
{{defn|no=1|Wooden blocks at the side of a {{nautical term|spar}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|Flat plates of iron or wood bolted to the {{nautical term|masthead}} to form angle supports for the {{gli|cross-trees}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=108}}}}
{{defn|no=3|The sides of a block or gun-carriage.}}

{{term|term=chief engineer|content=[[chief engineer]]}}
{{defn|The senior engineering officer (abbreviated ChEng).}}

{{term|term=chine|content=[[chine (boating)|chine]]}}
{{defn|no=1|An angle in the {{gli|hull}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A line formed where the sides of a boat meet the bottom.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/> Soft chine is when the two sides join at a shallow angle, and hard chine is when they join at a steep angle.}}

{{term|chock}}
{{defn|A hole or ring attached to the hull to guide a line via that point; an opening in a ship's {{gli|bulwark}}, normally oval in shape, designed to allow mooring lines to be fastened to {{gli|cleat|cleats}} or bits mounted to the ship's deck. See also ''{{nautical term|Panama chock}}'' and ''{{gli|Dutchman's chock}}''.}}

{{term|chock-a-block}}
{{defn|Rigging {{gli|block|blocks}} that are so tight against one another that they cannot be further tightened.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|chop}}
{{defn|Waves, usually created by the wind, which are smaller and shorter-lived than {{nautical term|swell}}.}}

{{term|term=chronometer|content=[[Marine chronometer|chronometer]]}}
{{defn|A timekeeping device accurate enough to be used aboard a ship to determine [[longitude]] by means of [[celestial navigation]]. The invention of the marine chronometer in the 18th century was a major technical achievement for maritime navigation.}}

{{term|cigarette boat}}
{{defn|See ''{{gli|go-fast boat}}''.}}

{{term|term=citadel|content=[[citadel]]}}
{{defn|A fortified safe room on a vessel to take shelter in the event of {{nautical term|pirate}} attack. Previously, a fortified room to protect ammunition and machinery from damage.}}

{{term|term=civil Red Ensign|content=civil [[Red Ensign]]}}
{{defn|The British Naval Ensign or flag of the [[British Merchant Navy]], a red flag with the [[Flag of the United Kingdom|Union Flag]] in the upper left corner. Colloquially called the "red duster".}}

{{term|term=class|content=[[Ship class|class]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Strictly, a group of government ships, especially naval ships, of the same or similar design.}}
{{defn|no=2|Informally, a group of private or commercial ships of the same or similar design.}}
{{defn|no=3|A standard of construction for merchant vessels, including standards for specific types or specialized capabilities of some types of merchant vessels (see, for example, ''{{nautical term|ice class}}''). A ship meeting the standard is ''in class'', while one not meeting it is ''out of class''.}}

{{term|class leader}}
{{defn|Synonym for ''{{nautical term|lead ship}}''.}}

{{term|term=classification society|content= classification society {{anchor|classification organization|classification organisation}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''classification organisation''' {{small|(American spelling)}} or '''classification organization''' {{small|(British spelling)}}}}
See ''{{nautical term|ship classification society}}''.}}

{{term|clean bill of health}}
{{defn|A certificate issued by a port indicating that a ship carries no infectious diseases. Also called a {{nautical term|pratique}}.}}

{{term|clean slate}}
{{defn|At the {{gli|helm}}, the {{nautical term|watchstanding|watchkeeper}} would record details of speed, distances, headings, etc. on a slate. At the beginning of a new watch the slate would be wiped clean.}}

{{term|clear}}
{{defn|no=1|To perform [[customs]] and [[immigration]] legalities prior to leaving port.}}
{{defn|no=2|More loosely, to leave port.}}

{{term|term=cleat|content=[[Cleat (nautical)|cleat]]}}
{{defn|A stationary device used to secure a rope aboard a vessel.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|clench}}
{{defn|A method of fixing together two pieces of wood, usually overlapping planks, by driving a nail through both planks as well as a washer-like rove. The nail is then burred or riveted over to complete the fastening.}}

{{term|clew}}
{{defn|One of the lower corners of a square sail, or, on a triangular sail, the corner at the end of the {{gli|boom}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=clewlines|content=[[Clewlines and buntlines|clewlines]]}}
{{term|clew-lines|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{gli|line|Lines}} used to truss up the {{gli|clew|clews}}, the lower corners of square sails. Used to reduce and stow a barge's {{nautical term|topsail}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=clinker-built|content=[[Clinker (boat building)|clinker-built]]}}
{{defn|
[[File:Clinker-carvel.svg|thumb|right|A comparison of the '''{{gli|clinker-built|clinker}}''' and '''{{gli|carvel-built|carvel}}''' styles of boat construction]]
A method of constructing {{gli|hull|hulls}} that involves overlapping planks and/or plates, much like Viking longships, resulting in speed and flexibility in small boat hulls. Contrast ''{{gli|carvel-built}}''.}}

{{term|term=clipper|content=[[clipper]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A sailing vessel designed primarily for speed. While the {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} clipper {{gli|full-rigged ship|ships}} of the middle of the 19th century are well known, others, such as [[Baltimore Clipper]]s and opium clippers could be rigged differently, often as {{nautical term|schooner|schooners}}, and a small number of 19th-century clippers were built as [[barque]]s.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|tuna clipper}}.}}

{{term|close aboard}}
{{defn|Very near (the ship).}}

{{term|term=close-hauled|content=[[Points of sail|close-hauled]]}}
{{defn|(of a vessel) {{gli|beating|Beating}} as close to the wind direction as possible.}}

{{term|term=clove hitch|content=[[clove hitch]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|bend}} used to attach a rope to a post or {{gli|bollard}}. Also used to finish tying off the {{gli|foresail}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|club hauling}}
{{defn|A maneuver by which a ship drops one of its {{gli|anchor|anchors}} at high speed in order to turn abruptly. This was sometimes used as a means of obtaining a good firing angle on a pursuing vessel. See ''{{gli|kedge}}''.}}

{{term|Clyde puffer|content=[[Clyde puffer]]}}
{{defn|A type of small, [[coal]]-fired, single-{{nautical term|mast}}ed Scottish {{nautical term|steamboat}} built between 1856 and 1939, primarily on the [[Forth and Clyde Canal]]. They operated as {{gli|cargo ship}}s on the [[canal]] and in other waters of [[Scotland]], including the [[Hebrides]]. The name "puffer" arose because earlier vessels had a simple [[steam engine]] with no [[Condenser (heat transfer)|condenser]], causing them to exhaust [[steam]] from their {{gli|funnel}}s in a series of "puffs."}}

{{term|CO}}
{{term|C.O.|multi=y}}
{{defn|An abbreviation for {{gli|commanding officer}}.}}

{{term|coach roof}}
{{defn|A {{gli|hulk}} top of the cabin from the outside.}}

{{term|coal hulk}}
{{defn|A {{gli|hulk}} used to store coal.}}

{{term|term=coal trimmer|content=[[coal trimmer]] {{anchor|trimmer}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also simply '''trimmer'''.}}
A person responsible for ensuring that a coal-fired vessel remains in "{{nautical term|trim}}" (evenly balanced) as coal is consumed on a voyage.}}

{{term|term=coaling|content=[[Coaling (ships)|coaling]]}}
{{defn|Loading [[coal]] for use as fuel aboard a [[steamship]]. A time-consuming, laborious, and dirty process often undertaken by the entire crew, coaling was a necessity from the early days of steam in the 19th century until the early 20th century, when oil supplanted coal as the fuel of choice for steamships.}}

{{term|term=coaming|content=[[coaming]]}}
{{defn|The raised edge of a hatch, cockpit, or skylight, designed to help keep out water that pools on the surface above.}}

{{term|coaster}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''skoot'''.}}
A [[coastal trading vessel]]; a shallow-hulled ship used for trade between locations on the same island or continent.}}

{{term|term=coble|content=[[coble]]}}
{{defn|A type of open traditional fishing boat with a flat bottom and high bow which developed on the northeast coast of England.}}

{{term|cockbill}}
{{defn|To angle a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} {{nautical term|yard}} away from the horizontal so that it is out of the way for loading or unloading, or so that the ship may lie alongside another ship without the yards touching.}}

{{term|term=cockpit|content=[[Cockpit (sailing)|cockpit]]}}
{{defn|A seating area (not to be confused with the {{gli|deck}}) towards the {{nautical term|stern}} of a small-decked vessel that houses the {{nautical term|rudder}} controls.}}

{{term|term=cofferdam|content=[[cofferdam#Naval architecture|cofferdam]]}}
{{defn|An insulating space between two watertight bulkheads or decks within a ship.}}

{{term|term=cog|content=[[Cog (ship)|cog]]}}
{{defn|A type of sailing ship, with a single {{nautical term|mast}} and a single {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} {{nautical term|sail}} first developed in the 10th century and widely used, particularly in the [[Baltic Sea]] region, in seagoing trade from the 12th through the 14th centuries. It had a distinctive hull design: the flat bottom was {{gli|carvel-built}} and the sides were {{gli|clinker-built}}.<ref name="Greenhill 1995">{{cite book |last1=Greenhill |first1=Basil |title=The archaeology of boats & ships : an introduction |date=1995 |publisher=Naval Institute Press |location=Annapolis, Md. |isbn=1-55750-039-8}}</ref>{{rp|225–227}}}}

{{term|term=coign|content=[[coign (gunnery)|coign]]}}
{{defn|A wedge used to assist in the aiming of a cannon; an older form of "quoin".}}

{{term|term=collier|content=[[Collier (ship)|collier]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|bulk cargo ship}} designed to carry coal, especially such a ship in naval use to supply coal to coal-fired warships.}}

{{term|term=combat loading|content=[[combat loading]]}}
{{defn|A way of loading a vessel that gives military forces embarked aboard her immediate access to weapons, ammunition, and supplies needed when conducting an amphibious landing. In combat loading, cargo is stowed in such a way that unloading of equipment will match up with the personnel that are landing and in the order they land, so that they have immediate access to the gear they need for combat as soon as they land. Combat loading gives primary consideration to the ease and sequence with which troops, equipment, and supplies can be made ready for combat, sacrificing the more efficient use of cargo space that ship operators seek when loading a ship for the routine transportation of personnel and cargo.}}

{{term|term=comber|content=[[comber wave|comber]]}}
{{defn|A long, curving wave breaking on the shore.}}

{{term|come about}}
{{defn|no=1|To {{nautical term|tacking|tack}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|To change tack.}}
{{defn|no=3|To manoeuvre the {{gli|bow}} of a sailing vessel across the wind so that the wind changes from one side of the vessel to the other.}}
{{defn|no=4|To position a vessel with respect to the wind after tacking.}}

{{term|come to}}
{{defn|To stop a sailing vessel, especially by turning into the wind.}}

{{term|commanding officer}}
{{defn|The officer in command of a warship.  Also called "CO", "captain" (regardless of rank), "skipper", or "the old man".}}

{{term|term=commission|content=[[Ship commissioning|commission]]}}
{{defn|To formally place (a naval vessel) into active service, after which the vessel is said to be ''in commission''. Sometimes used less formally to mean placing a commercial ship into service.}}

{{term|term=commissioning pennant|content=[[commissioning pennant]]}}
{{defn|A [[Pennon|pennant]] flown from the {{nautical term|masthead}} of a [[warship]]. Also called a masthead pennant.}}

{{term|commodore}}
{{defn|no=1|([[commodore (rank)|rank]]) Prior to 1997, the title used in the Royal Navy for an officer of the rank of {{gli|captain}} who was given temporary command of a {{nautical term|squadron}}. At the end of the deployment of the squadron, or in the presence of an {{gli|admiral}}, he would revert to his ''de facto'' rank of captain.}}
{{defn|no=2|([[commodore (rank)|rank]]) A military rank used in many navies that is superior to a navy {{gli|captain}} but below a {{nautical term|rear admiral}}. Often equivalent to the rank of "flotilla admiral" or sometimes "counter admiral" in non-English-speaking navies.}}
{{defn|no=3|([[convoy commodore]]) A civilian put in charge of the good order of the merchant ships in British convoys during [[World War II]], but with no authority over naval ships escorting the convoy.}}
{{defn|no=4|([[commodore (yacht club)]]) An officer of a yacht club.}}
{{defn|no=5|([[Commodore (Sea Scouts)]]) A position in the [[Boy Scouts of America]]'s [[Sea Scouts (Boy Scouts of America)|Sea Scouts]] program.}}

{{term|term=communication tube|content=[[speaking tube|communication tube]] {{anchor|voice tube|speaking tube}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''speaking tube''' or '''voice tube'''.}}
An air-filled tube, usually armored, allowing speech between the [[conning tower]] and the below-decks control spaces on a warship.}}

{{term|term=companionway|content=[[companionway]]}}
{{defn|A raised and windowed hatchway in a ship's {{gli|deck}}, with a ladder leading below and the hooded entrance-hatch to the main {{gli|cabin|cabins}}.}}

{{term|complement}}
{{defn|no=1|The number of persons in a ship's {{gli|crew}}, including officers.}}
{{defn|no=2|A collective term for all of the persons in a ship's crew, including officers.}}

{{term|comprise}}
{{defn|To include or contain. As applied to a naval task force, the listing of all assigned units for a single transient purpose or mission (e.g. "The task force ''comprises'' Ship A, Ship B, and Ship C"). "Comprise" means exhaustive inclusion – there are not any other parts to the task force, and each ship has a permanent squadron existence, independent of the task force.}}

{{term|term=concrete ship|content=[[concrete ship]]}}
{{defn|A vessel constructed of steel and [[ferrocement]] (a type of [[reinforced concrete]]) rather than of more traditional materials, such as steel, iron, or wood.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.concreteships.org/history/|title=Concrete Ships: History|website=www.concreteships.org}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=confined waters|content=[[confined waters]]}}
{{defn|Waters where there is little space to maneuver.}}

{{term|term=conn|content=[[Conn (nautical)|conn]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''con''', '''conne''', '''conde''', '''cunde''', or '''cun'''.}}
To direct a ship or submarine from a position of command. While performing this duty, an officer is said to ''have the conn''.}}

{{term|conning officer}}
{{defn|An officer on a naval vessel responsible for instructing the {{gli|helmsman}} on the course to steer. While performing this duty, the officer is said to ''have the conn''.}}

{{term|term=conning tower|content=[[conning tower]]}}
{{defn|no=1|An armored control tower of an iron or steel warship built between the mid-19th and mid-20th centuries from which the ship was navigated in battle.}}
{{defn|no=2|A tower-like structure on the dorsal (topside) surface of a submarine, serving in submarines built before the mid-20th century as a connecting structure between the bridge and pressure hull and housing instruments and controls from which the periscopes were used to direct the submarine and launch [[torpedo]] attacks. Since the mid-20th century, it has been replaced by the ''[[sail (submarine)|sail]]'' (United States usage) or ''fin'' (European and Commonwealth usage), a structure similar in appearance that no longer plays a role in directing the submarine.}}

{{term|term=consort|content=[[Consort (nautical)|consort]]}}
{{defn|Unpowered [[Great Lakes]] vessels, usually a fully loaded {{nautical term|schooner}}, {{gli|barge}}, or [[steamboat|steamer]] barge, towed by a larger steamer that would often tow more than one barge. The consort system was used in the Great Lakes from the 1860s to around 1920.}}

{{term|term=constant bearing, decreasing range|content=[[constant bearing, decreasing range]] ({{vanchor|CBDR}})}}
{{defn|When two boats are approaching each other from any angle and this angle remains the same over time (constant {{gli|bearing}}) they are on a collision course. Because of the implication of collision, "constant bearing, decreasing range" has come to mean a problem or an obstacle which is incoming.<ref>{{cite web |title=CBDR |work=Free Dictionary |url=http://acronyms.thefreedictionary.com/CBDR |access-date=2011-06-24}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=container ship|content=[[container ship]]}}
{{defn|A cargo ship that carries all of her cargo in truck-size [[intermodal containers]].}}

{{term|term=convoy|content=[[convoy]]}}
{{defn|A group of ships traveling together for mutual support and protection.}}

{{term|corinthian}}
{{defn|An [[Amateur sports|amateur]] {{nautical term|yacht|yachter}}.<ref>''Oxford English Dictionary'', ''s.v.'' "corinthian".</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://dictionary.reference.com/browse/corinthian |title=corinthian |work=Dictionary.com}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=corrector|content=[[corrector]]}}
{{defn|A device used to correct the ship's compass, e.g. by counteracting errors due to the magnetic effects of a steel hull.}}

{{term|corsair}}
{{defn|no=1|A [[French corsairs|French privateer]], especially one from the port of [[St-Malo]].}}
{{defn|no=2|Any privateer or pirate.}}
{{defn|no=3|A ship used by privateers or pirates, especially of French nationality.}}
{{defn|no=4|([[corsair (dinghy)]]) A class of {{convert|16|ft|m|abbr=off|adj=on}} three-handed sailing {{gli|dinghy}}.}}

{{term|term=corvette|content=[[corvette]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''corvetto'''.}}
1.&nbsp;A flush-decked sailing warship of the 17th, 18th, and 19th centuries having a single tier of guns, ranked next below a {{gli|frigate}}. In the US Navy, it is referred to as a {{nautical term|sloop-of-war}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A lightly armed and armored warship of the 20th and 21st centuries, smaller than a frigate and capable of transoceanic duty.}}

{{term|cotchel}}
{{defn|A partial load.{{sfn|Benham|Finch|Kershaw|1986|p=186}}}}

{{term|term=cottonclad|content=[[cottonclad warship|cottonclad]]}}
{{defn|A steam-powered wooden warship protected from enemy fire by bales of [[cotton]] lining its sides, most commonly associated with some of the warships employed by the [[Confederate States of America]] during the [[American Civil War]] (1861–1865).}}

{{term|counter}}
{{defn|The part of the {{nautical term|stern}} above the {{nautical term|waterline}} that extends beyond the {{nautical term|rudder}} stock culminating in a small {{nautical term|transom}}. A long counter increases the waterline length when the boat is {{gli|heeled}}, so increasing hull speed. See also ''{{nautical term|truncated counter}}''.}}

{{term|counterflood}}
{{defn|To deliberately flood compartments on the opposite side from already flooded ones. Usually done to reduce a [[Angle of list|list]].}}

{{term|country ship}}
{{defn|A term used by the British [[East India Company]] from the seventeenth to the nineteenth century for a merchant ship owned by local owners east of the [[Cape of Good Hope]] which traded within that area and gathered cargoes for shipment west of the Cape to England (later the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|United Kingdom]]) by the company's "{{gli|chartered ship|chartered ships}}", "{{gli|extra ship|extra ships}}", and "{{nautical term|regular ship|regular ships}}". "Country ships" were strictly prohibited from trading west of the Cape, which would violate the company's strict monopoly on that trade.<ref name=threedecksshiproles/> Country ships were also important in the opium trade from India into China until supplanted by the faster opium clipper.<ref name="Trocki">{{cite book |last1=Trocki |first1=Carl A. |title=Opium, Empire, and the Global Political Economy : a study of the Asian opium trade, 1750-1950 |date=1999 |publisher=Routledge |location=London |isbn=0-415-21500-5 |edition=Kindle}}</ref>{{rp|51}}}}

{{term|course}}
{{defn|no=1|The direction in which a vessel is being steered, usually given in degrees.}}
{{defn|no=2|The lowest square sail on a square rigged mast, except where that mast is the mizzen{{snd}}in which case the name {{gli|cro'jack}} (cross-jack) or mizzen-sail is used.{{sfn|Bennett|2005|p=8}}}}

{{term|cowl}}
{{defn|no=1|A ship's ventilator with a bell-shaped top that can be swiveled to catch the wind and force it below.}}
{{defn|no=2|A vertical projection of a ship's funnel that directs the smoke away from the {{gli|bridge}}.}}

{{term|term=coxswain|content=[[coxswain]] {{anchor|cockswain}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''cockswain'''.}}
The {{gli|helmsman}} or crew member in command of a boat.}}

{{term|CPO country}}
{{defn|The part of a naval vessel containing the residential quarters and {{gli|wardroom}} for {{gli|chief petty officer}}s. ''CPO country'' is off-limits to more junior enlisted personnel unless they are there on official business.}}

{{term|crab}}
{{defn|A winch used for raising the {{gli|leeboard}}, with a barrel for pulling in the {{nautical term|staysail}} sheets.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|crabber}}
{{defn|A fishing vessel rigged for [[crab fishing]].}}

{{term|crane ship}}
{{term|term=crane vessel|content=[[crane vessel]]|multi=y}}
{{defn|A ship with a crane and specialized for lifting heavy loads.}}

{{term|term=cranse iron|content=cranse iron {{anchor|crance iron|crans iron|cranze iron}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''crance''', '''crans''', or '''cranze iron'''.}}
The metal fitting mounted at the end of a {{gli|bowsprit}} to which the {{gli|forestay}} (or jibstay), {{gli|bobstay}}, and bowsprit shrouds are attached. It is also where the tack of the outermost {{gli|headsail}} is fastened.<ref name="Read 2014c">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014c |c= C |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/c-16188}}</ref> }}

{{term|crash boat}}
{{term|term=crash rescue boat|content=[[crash rescue boat]]|multi=y}}
{{defn|A term used in the United States to describe military high-speed offshore rescue boats, similar in size and performance to motor torpedo boats, used to rescue pilots and aircrews of crashed aircraft.}}

{{term|Crazy Ivan}}
{{defn|US Navy slang for a maneuver in which a submerged Soviet or Russian submarine suddenly turns 180 degrees or through 360 degrees to detect submarines following it.}}

{{term|term=crew|content=[[crew]]}}
{{defn|no=1|On warships and merchant ships, all of those members of a {{nautical term|ship's company}} who are not officers.}}
{{defn|no=2|On leisure vessels with no formal chain of command, all of those persons who are not the skipper or passengers.}}

{{term|term=crew boat|content=[[crew boat]]}}
{{defn|A vessel specialized for the transportation of offshore support personnel and cargo to and from offshore installations such as oil platforms, drilling rigs, drill ships, dive ships, and wind farms. Also known as a ''fast support vessel'' or ''fast supply vessel''.}}

{{term|term=crew management|content=[[crew management]]}}
{{defn|The services rendered by specialised shipping companies to manage the human resources and manning of all types of vessels, including recruitment, deployment to vessel, scheduling, and training, as well as the ongoing management and administrative duties of seafarers, such as payroll, travel arrangements, insurance and health schemes, overall career development, and day-to-day welfare. Also known as ''crewing''.}}

{{term|cringle}}
{{defn|A loop of rope, usually at the corners of a sail, for fixing the sail to a {{nautical term|spar}}. They are often reinforced with a metal eye.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=cro'jack|content=cro'jack {{anchor|crossjack}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''crossjack'''.}}
The square sail set on the lower mizzen yard of a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ship. Many full-rigged ships would not set a sail in this position, as it would be interfered with by the {{nautical term|spanker}}{{sfn|Underhill|1946|p=12}}}}

{{term|cross the line}}
{{defn|Cross the [[equator]].}}

{{term|term=crosstrees|content=[[crosstrees]]}}
{{defn|Two horizontal struts at the upper ends of the {{nautical term|topmast|topmasts}} of sailboats, used to anchor the shrouds from the {{nautical term|topgallant}} mast. Lateral spreaders for the topmast shrouds (standing back stays).<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=crow's nest|content=[[crow's nest]]}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|masthead}} constructed with sides and sometimes a roof to shelter the lookouts from the weather, generally by whaling vessels. The term has also become generic for what is properly called a masthead.}}

{{term|term=cruise ship|content=[[cruise ship]]}}
{{defn|A passenger ship used for pleasure voyages, where the voyage itself and the ship's amenities are the purpose of the experience, as are the different destinations along the way. Transportation is not the prime purpose, as cruise ships operate mostly on routes that return passengers to their originating port. A cruise ship contrasts with a ''passenger liner'', which is a passenger ship that provides a scheduled service between published ports primarily as a mode of transportation. Large, prestigious passenger ships used for either purpose are sometimes called ''ocean liners''.}}

{{term|term=cruiser|content=[[cruiser]]}}
{{defn|no=1|From the mid-19th century to the mid-20th century, a classification for a wide variety of gun- and sometimes [[torpedo]]-armed warships, usually but not always armored, intended for independent scouting, raiding, or commerce protection; some were designed also to provide direct support to a battle fleet. Cruisers carried out functions performed previously by the cruising ships (sailing frigates and sloops-of-war) of the [[Age of Sail]].}}
{{defn|no=2|From the early to the mid-20th century, a type of armored warship with varying armament and of various sizes, but always smaller than a [[battleship]] and larger than a [[destroyer]], capable of both direct support of a battle fleet and of independent operations, armed with guns and sometimes torpedoes.}}
{{defn|no=3|After the mid-20th century, various types of warships of intermediate size armed with guided missiles and sometimes guns, intended for air defense of [[aircraft carrier]]s and associated task forces or for anti-ship missile attacks against such forces; virtually indistinguishable from large destroyers since the late 20th century.}}
{{defn|no=4|A yacht with one or more cabins containing the facilities for living aboard, thus capable of making {{nautical term|voyages}}.}}

{{term|crutches}}
{{defn|Metal Y-shaped pins used to fix oars while rowing.}}

{{term|term=cuddy|content=[[cuddy (cabin)|cuddy]]}}
{{defn|A small {{gli|cabin}} in a boat; a cabin, for the use of the captain, in the after part of a sailing ship under the {{nautical term|poop deck}}.}}

{{term|term=Cunningham|content=[[Cunningham (sailing)|Cunningham]]}}
{{defn|A line invented by [[Briggs Cunningham]], used to control the shape of a sail.<ref name="BCuk">{{cite news |last1=Lloyd |first1=Barbara |title=Briggs Cunningham, 96, Racecar Pioneer and Sailing Champ |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/07/05/sports/briggs-cunningham-96-racecar-pioneer-and-sailing-champ.html |access-date=21 May 2020 |work=The New York Times |date=5 July 2003}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=cunt splice|content=[[Rope splicing#Types of splices|cunt splice]] {{anchor|cut splice}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''cut splice'''.}}
A join between two lines, similar to an eye-splice, where each rope end is joined to the other a short distance along, making an opening that closes under tension.}}

{{term|cuntline}}
{{defn|The "valley" between the strands of a rope or cable. Before serving a section of laid rope, e.g. to protect it from {{gli|chafing}}, it may be "wormed" by laying yarns in the cuntlines, giving that section an even cylindrical shape.}}

{{term|cut and run}}
{{defn|When wanting to make a quick escape, a ship might cut lashings to sails or cables for anchors, causing damage to the rigging, or losing an anchor, but shortening the time needed to {{nautical term|make ready}} by bypassing the proper procedures.}}

{{term|cut of his jib}}
{{defn|The "cut" of a sail refers to its shape. Since this would often vary between ships, it could be used both to identify a familiar vessel at a distance, and to judge the possible sailing qualities of an unknown one. During the 16th and 17th centuries, the ships of different nations used visually distinctive types of {{gli|jib|jibs}} that could be determined at a distance, providing an easy way to determine friend from foe.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.dictionary.com/browse/cut-of-one-s-jib|title=Definition of cut of one's jib &#124; Dictionary.com|website=www.dictionary.com}}</ref> Also used figuratively of people.<ref name=Jeans>{{cite book |last=Jeans |first=Peter D |title= Ship to Shore |year=1998 |publisher= [[ABC-Clio]] |location= Oxford, England |isbn= 1-85109-321-4}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=cutter|content=[[cutter (boat)|cutter]]}}
{{defn|
[[File:Cutter rigging.svg|thumb|right|A typical '''{{gli|cutter}}''' rigging: one mast bearing a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} {{nautical term|mainsail}} (A) and {{gli|foresail}} ({{nautical term|staysail|forestaysail}}) (B), a {{gli|bowsprit}} (C), a {{gli|jib}} (D), and a {{gli|flying jib}} (E)]]
}}
{{defn|no=1|A sailing vessel defined by its rig. In European waters this is a single-masted {{gli|fore-and-aft rigging|fore and aft rig}} with two or more {{gli|headsail|headsails}} In North American waters, the definition also considers whether or not the bowsprit is permanently fixed and also takes into account the position of the mast. A standing (permanently fixed) bowsprit and a forward mast position, but with two or more headsails would be classed as a sloop in the North American definition. A running bowsprit, a forestay (carrying a staysail) that is fixed to the stemhead, a jib that is set flying and a mast position that is more aft is a cutter.}}
{{defn|no=2|A type of {{nautical term|ship's boat}} powered by sail or oars, though more optimised for sail than many types of ship's boat. }}
{{defn|no=3|A small- or medium-sized vessel used by governmental agencies or law enforcement in the exercise of official authority, such as harbor [[maritime pilot|pilots']] cutters, [[US Coast Guard]] cutters, and [[UK Border Agency]] cutters.}}
{{defn|no=4|A type of decked sailing vessel originating in the early 18th century designed for speed. Many were used as small {{nautical term|warship}}s. Originally ''cutter'' referred only to a type of {{gli|hull}}, but it came to refer to the {{nautical term|rig}}, which was single-{{nautical term|mast}}ed with both fore-and-aft and square sails. A cutter rig had very large sail areas available for use in light winds. }}

{{term|term=cutting out|content=[[cutting out]]}}
{{defn|A surprise attack by small boats, often at night, against an anchored vessel in which the small-boat crews boarded and captured or destroyed the target vessel. Cutting out became a popular tactic in the latter part of the 18th century and saw extensive use during the [[Napoleonic Wars]]. Cutting out was still in use in the mid-19th century, in conflicts such as the [[Mexican-American War]] and [[American Civil War]].}}

{{term|cutwater}}
{{defn|The forward curve of the {{nautical term|stem}} of a ship.}}

{{Glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==D==
{{glossary}}
{{term|term=daggerboard|content=[[daggerboard]]}}
{{defn|A type of light {{gli|centerboard}} that is lifted vertically; sometimes in pairs, with the {{gli|leeward}} one lowered when {{gli|beating}}.}}

{{term|dan}}
{{term|dan-buoy|multi=y}}
{{defn|A temporary marker {{gli|buoy}} consisting of a long pole with flag and/or light at the top and, lower down, a float and a ballast weight to make it float vertically. May be used with or without an anchor to attach it to the sea bed. In naval use often marks a swept channel created by minesweeping. In other uses may mark fishing equipment (nets or pots), an anchor, or, most commonly, is attached to a lifebuoy to throw into the sea to mark the position of a {{nautical term|man overboard}}.<ref name="Read 2014d">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014d |c= D |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/d-16186}}</ref>{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|dandy}}
{{defn|no=1|A rig with a small {{nautical term|mizzen}} {{gli|abaft}} the {{nautical term|steering post}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|In British usage, another name for a {{nautical term|yawl}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|In British usage, a small after-sail on a yawl.}}

{{term|term=danlayer|content=[[danlayer]]}}
{{defn|A [[Minelaying|mine warfare]] vessel, usually a small {{nautical term|trawler}}, fitted for laying {{gli|dan|dans}}. Danlayers served as a part of {{nautical term|minesweeping}} {{gli|flotilla|flotillas}} during and immediately after [[World War II]] (1939–1945).}}

{{term|dart}}
{{defn|To run dart; to run dead before the wind.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=davit|content=[[davit]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A spar formerly used on board ships as a crane to hoist the flukes of the {{gli|anchor}} to the top of the {{gli|bow}} without injuring the sides of the ship.}}
{{defn|no=2|A crane, often working in pairs and usually made of steel, used to lower things over the side of a ship, including {{gli|lifeboat|lifeboats}}.}}

{{term|term=Davy Jones's Locker|content=[[Davy Jones's Locker]]}}
{{defn|An idiom for the bottom of the sea.}}

{{term|term=day beacon|content=[[day beacon]]}}
{{defn|An unlighted fixed structure equipped with a {{gli|dayboard}} for daytime identification.}}

{{term|day-blink}}
{{defn|The moment at dawn where, from some point on the {{nautical term|mast}}, a lookout can see above low-lying mist around the ship.}}

{{term|dayboard}}
{{defn|The daytime identifier of an {{gli|aid to navigation}} presenting one of several standard shapes (square, triangle, or rectangle) and colors (red, green, white, orange, yellow, or black).}}

{{term|day boat}}
{{defn|no=1| A sailing boat without overnight accommodation, sometimes with a small cabin, used for pleasure sailing. Typically around {{convert|20|ft|m|1}} in length.<ref name="Read 2014d"/> }}
{{defn|no=2|(United States): a steamboat built for daytime service; as opposed to a {{nautical term|night boat}}.}}

{{term|dead ahead}}
{{defn|Exactly ahead; directly ahead; directly in front.}}

{{term|dead horse}}
{{defn|Debt owed for advanced pay.<ref name="NHHC"/> The "[[Flogging a dead horse#Earlier related terms|flogging a dead horse]]" ceremony at sea celebrated discharge of the debt.}}

{{term|dead in the water}}
{{defn|Not moving (used only when a vessel is afloat and neither tied up nor anchored). The term is abbreviated to DIW by the [[US Navy]]. It is often used to indicate that a pirate or drug runner vessel has been immobilised.}}

{{term|dead run}}
{{defn|See ''{{nautical term|running}}''.}}

{{term|dead slow}}
{{defn|See ''{{nautical term|steerageway}}''.}}

{{term|dead wake}}
{{defn|The trail of a fading disturbance in the water. See also ''{{nautical term|wake}}''.}}

{{term|term=deadeye|content=[[deadeye]]}}
{{defn|A wooden block with three holes (but no pulleys) spliced to a {{nautical term|shroud}}. It adjusts the tension in the {{nautical term|standing rigging}} of large sailing vessels, by lacing through the holes with a lanyard to the deck. It performs the same job as a {{nautical term|turnbuckle}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|deadhead}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|snag}}.}}

{{term|deadlight}}
{{defn|A strong shutter fitted over a porthole or other opening that can be closed in bad weather.}}

{{term|term=dead reckoning|content=[[dead reckoning]]}}
{{defn|A method of navigation that estimates a ship's position from the distance run measured by the log and the course steered. If corrections for factors such as tide and leeway are then made, this provides an estimated position.<ref name="Bill Anderson">{{cite book |last1=Anderson |first1=Bill |title=Navigation Exercises for Yachtsmen |date=1974 |publisher=Stanford Maritime |location=London |isbn=0-540-07142-0 |page=14}}</ref> Dead reckoning contrasts with fixing a position with [[astronomical navigation]] or [[satellite navigation]]. Some sources consider that a dead reckoning position does include adjustments for wind and tide, so care is needed in interpretation of this term.<ref>{{cite book |title=Oxford English Dictionary |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=Oxford |url=https://www.oed.com/view/Entry/47680?redirectedFrom=dead+reckoning#eid |access-date=3 September 2019}}</ref>}}

{{term|deadrise}}
{{defn|The angle of the {{gli|hull}} surface, relative to horizontal, as measured on either side of the {{gli|keel}} on a line drawn towards the turn of the {{gli|bilge}}. Without any other qualifier, it is taken at the midships cross-section of the hull. This can be expressed in degrees or sometimes as a vertical linear measure (such as inches) at a standard distance from the keel. A hull with a lot of deadrise has an obvious "V" shape to the bottom of the hull, whereas no deadrise denotes sheer sides and a flat-bottomed hull. It is usually taken to be one of several measures of the "sharpness" of a hull. It can also be referred to as the "rise of {{gli|floor}}".<ref name="Gardiner 1993">{{cite book |last1=Gardiner |first1=Robert |title=The Advent of Steam – The Merchant Steamship before 1900 |date=1993 |publisher=Conway Maritime Press |location=London |isbn=0-85177-563-2 |chapter=glossary}}</ref>}}

{{term|deadwood}}
{{defn|In a traditional wooden hull, blocks of timber on the top of the keel that form the shape of the hull where its section is too narrow for the method of construction employed elsewhere. It is often used forward of the {{nautical term|sternpost}}.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|term=death roll|content=[[death roll]]}}
{{defn|In a keel boat, the act of {{gli|broach|broaching}} to {{nautical term|windward}}, putting the {{nautical term|spinnaker pole}} into the water and causing a crash-{{gli|gybe}} of the {{gli|boom}} and {{nautical term|mainsail}}, which sweep across the deck and plunge down into the water. During a death roll, the boat {{nautical term|roll|rolls}} from side to side, becoming gradually more unstable until either it {{gli|capsize|capsizes}} or the skipper reacts correctly to prevent it.}}

{{term|term=debarcation|content=[[Debark (ship)|debarcation]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''disembarkation'''.}}
The process of leaving a ship or aircraft, or removing goods from a ship or aircraft.}}

{{term|debunk}}
{{defn|The process of removing fuel from a vessel. After a shipwreck, a "debunkering" operation will be performed in an effort to minimize damage and protect the environment from fuel spills.}}

{{term|term=deck|content=[[Deck (ship)|deck]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The top of a ship or vessel; the surface that is removed to accommodate the seating area.}}
{{defn|no=2|Any of the structures forming the approximately horizontal surfaces in the ship's general structure. Unlike flats, they are a structural part of the ship.}}

{{term|term=deck gun|content=[[deck gun]]}}
{{defn|A gun mounted on the deck of a {{nautical term|submarine}} for use in surface combat. Common on submarines of the first half of the 20th century, deck guns became obsolete as submarines became capable of sustained underwater operations after [[World War II]].}}

{{term|term=deck hand|content=deck hand {{anchor|decky}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''decky'''.}}
A person whose job involves aiding the deck supervisor in (un)mooring, anchoring, maintenance, and general evolutions on deck.}}

{{term|deck supervisor}}
{{defn|The person in charge of all evolutions and maintenance on deck; sometimes split into two groups: forward deck supervisor and aft deck supervisor.}}

{{term|term=deckhead|content=[[deckhead]]}}
{{defn|The underside of the {{gli|deck}} above. The inside of the boat is normally paneled over to hide the structure, pipes, electrical wires. It can be in thin wood planks, often covered with a vinyl lining, or in thin PVC or now even in fiberglass planks.}}

{{term|deckhouse}}
{{defn|A {{gli|cabin}} that protrudes above a ship's {{gli|deck}}.}}

{{term|decks awash}}
{{defn|A situation in which the {{gli|deck}} of the vessel is partially or wholly submerged, possibly as a result of excessive {{gli|list|listing}} or a loss of buoyancy.}}

{{term|term=decommission|content=[[Ship decommissioning|decommission]]}}
{{defn|To formally take (a naval vessel) out of active service, after which the vessel is said to be ''out of commission'' or ''decommissioned''. Sometimes used less formally to mean taking a commercial ship out of service.}}

{{term|term=degaussing|content=[[degaussing]]}}
{{defn|A process to reduce a warship's magnetic signature.}}

{{term|term=demurrage|content=[[demurrage]]}}
{{defn|A fee paid by a charter party to a shipowner if the time taken to load or unload a vessel exceeds the {{gli|laytime}}{{snd}}the amount of time stipulated for loading or unloading{{snd}}specified in a [[voyage charter]].}}

{{term|term=depot ship|content=[[depot ship]]}}
{{defn|A ship that acts as a mobile or fixed base for other ships and submarines or that supports a naval base.}}

{{term|depth of hold}}
{{defn|The distance between the underside of the main {{gli|deck}} (or its supporting beams) and the top of the limber boards (the part of the ceiling that lies alongside the {{gli|keelson}}), measured at the middle frame. It is one of the key measurements in working out the measurement tonnage in most systems.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|term=derrick|content=[[derrick]]}}
{{defn|A lifting device composed of one {{nautical term|mast}} or pole and a {{gli|boom}} or {{gli|jib}} hinged freely at the bottom.}}

{{term|term=despatch|content=[[Demurrage|despatch]]}}
{{defn|A fee paid by a shipowner to a charter party if the time taken to load or unload a vessel is less than the {{gli|laytime}}{{snd}}the amount of time stipulated for loading or unloading{{snd}}specified in a [[voyage charter]].}}

{{term|despatch boat}}
{{defn|An alternate spelling of {{gli|dispatch boat}}.}}

{{term|term=destroyer|content=[[destroyer]]}}
{{defn|A type of fast and maneuverable small warship introduced in the 1890s to protect {{gli|capital ship|capital ships}} from [[torpedo boat]] attack, and since increased in size and capabilities to become a long-endurance warship intended to escort larger vessels in a fleet, convoy, or battle group and defend them against submarines, surface ships, aircraft, or missiles. Originally ''torpedo boat destroyer''. In US Navy slang, also called a ''tin can'' or ''small boy''.}}

{{term|destroyer depot ship}}
{{defn|See ''{{gli|destroyer tender}}''.}}

{{term|term=destroyer escort|content=[[destroyer escort]]}}
{{defn|A US Navy term for a smaller, lightly armed warship built in large numbers during [[World War II]] (and in smaller numbers thereafter), cheaper, slower, and less-well-armed than a {{gli|destroyer}} but larger and more heavily armed than a {{gli|corvette}} and designed to escort convoys of merchant ships or naval auxiliaries or second-line naval forces. Employed primarily for anti-submarine warfare, but also used to provide some protection against aircraft and smaller surface ships. Generally known as {{gli|frigate|frigates}} in other navies, and designated as such in the US Navy as well by the 1970s.}}

{{term|term=destroyer leader|content=[[destroyer leader]]}}
{{defn|A large {{gli|destroyer}} suitable for commanding a {{gli|flotilla}} of destroyers or other small warships; a type of {{gli|flotilla leader}}.}}

{{term|term=destroyer tender|content=[[destroyer tender]]}}
{{defn|A naval {{gli|auxiliary ship}} designed to provide maintenance support to a flotilla of destroyers or other small warships. Known in British English as a ''destroyer depot ship''.}}

{{term|devil seam}}
{{defn|The devil was possibly a slang term for the garboard seam, hence "{{gli|between the devil and the deep blue sea}}" being an allusion to keel hauling, but a more popular version seems to be the seam between the waterway and the {{nautical term|stanchion|stanchions}}, which would be difficult to get at, requiring a cranked caulking iron, and a restricted swing of the caulking mallet.}}

{{term|devil to pay}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''devil to pay, and no pitch hot'''.}}
"Paying" the devil is sealing the {{gli|devil seam}}. It is a difficult and unpleasant job (with no resources) because of the shape of the seam (up against the stanchions) or if the devil refers to the garboard seam, it must be done with the ship slipped or {{gli|careening|careened}}.}}

{{term|term=devil's claw|content=[[Anchor windlass#Devil's claw|devil's claw]]}}
{{defn|A type of chain stopper often used to secure an anchor in its {{gli|hawsepipe}}. Consists of a two-pronged hook that fits over a link of chain, a {{nautical term|turnbuckle}} and a short chain fastened to a strong point.}}

{{term|term=dhow|content=[[dhow]]}}
{{defn|The generic name of a number of traditional sailing vessels with one or more masts with {{gli|lateen sail|lateen sails}} used in the Red Sea and Indian Ocean region, typically weighing 300 to 500 tons, with a long, thin hull. They are trading vessels primarily used to carry heavy items, like fruit, fresh water, or merchandise. Crews vary from about thirty to around twelve, depending on the size of the vessel.}}

{{term|diamonds}}
{{defn|Glass prisms that were laid between the wooden deck planks to allow natural light below were referred to as diamonds due to the sparkle they gave off in the sunlight.}}

{{term|term=dinghy|content=[[dinghy]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A type of small boat, often carried or towed as a {{nautical term|ship's boat}} by a larger vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|A small racing yacht or recreational open sailing boat, often used for beginner training rather than sailing full-sized yachts.}}
{{defn|no=3|Utility dinghies are usually rowboats or have an outboard motor, but some are rigged for sailing.}}

{{term|dipping the eye}}
{{defn|
[[File:Multiple mooring correct.png|thumb|upright|Dipping the eye]]
A method of attaching more than one {{gli|hawser}} to a single {{gli|bollard}}, so that each can be lifted off without disturbing the other(s). The second hawser is passed under the first, then up through the eye of the first (hence the name), before being secured over the bollard.}}

{{term|term=dipping the flag|content=[[flag dipping|dipping the flag]]}}
{{defn|A method of rendering honors at sea by lowering and raising a ship's flag.}}

{{term|directional light}}
{{defn|A light illuminating a sector or very narrow angle and intended to mark a direction to be followed.}}

{{term|disembark}}
{{term|disembarcation|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|debarcation}}.}}}}

{{term|term=dispatch boat|content=[[dispatch boat]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''{{gli|dispatch vessel}}'''}}
A vessel ranging in size from a small boat to a large ship tasked to carry military dispatches from ship to ship, from ship to shore, or, occasionally, from shore to shore.}}

{{term|term=displacement|content=[[Displacement (ship)|displacement]]}}
{{defn|The weight of water displaced by the immersed volume of a ship's hull, exactly equivalent to the weight of the whole ship.}}

{{term|displacement [[hull (watercraft)|hull]]}}
{{defn|A hull designed to travel through the water, rather than planing over it.}}

{{term|term=disposable ship|content=[[disposable ship]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''{{gli|drogher}}''', '''raft ship''', '''timber drogher''', or '''timber ship'''.}}
A barely seaworthy ship of the 19th century assembled from large timbers lashed or pegged together and designed to make a single voyage from North America to the United Kingdom and then to be disassembled so that her timbers could be sold, thus avoiding high British taxes on lumber imported as cargo. When British taxes on imported lumber fell, the construction of disposable ships ceased.}}

{{term|disrate}}
{{defn|To reduce in rank or rating; to demote.}}

{{term|term=distinctive mark|content=[[distinctive mark]]}}
{{defn|A flag flown to distinguish ships of one seagoing service of a given country from ships of the country's other seagoing service(s) when ships of more than one of the country's seagoing services fly the same {{gli|ensign}}.}}

{{term|ditty bag}}
{{defn|Bag or box for personal items.<ref name="NHHC"/>}}

{{term|term=division|content=[[Division (naval)|division]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Especially in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, a permanent battle formation of a fleet, often smaller than a squadron, equipped and trained to operate as a tactical unit under the overall command of a higher command, such as a fleet or squadron.}}
{{defn|no=2|Especially in modern usage, an administrative naval command, smaller than a squadron and often subordinate to an administrative squadron, responsible for the manning, training, supply, and maintenance of a group of ships or submarines but not for directing their operations at sea.{{citation needed|date=September 2019}}}}

{{term|term=Divisional Transport Officer|content=[[Divisional Transport Officer]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''Divisional Naval Transport Officer'''}}
In British usage, a shore-based naval officer responsible for the efficient working of the {{nautical term|transport|transports}} and boats of the {{gli|flotilla}}, {{gli|division}}, or {{nautical term|squadron}} under his charge.}}

{{term|term=dock|content=[[Dock (maritime)|dock]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In American usage, a fixed structure attached to shore to which a vessel is secured when in port, generally synonymous with ''pier'' and ''wharf'', except that ''pier'' tends to refer to structures used for tying up commercial ships and to structures extending from shore for use in fishing, while ''dock'' refers more generally to facilities used for tying up ships or boats, including recreational craft.}}
{{defn|no=2|In British usage, the body of water between two piers or wharves that accommodates vessels tied up at the piers or wharves.}}
{{defn|no=3|To tie up along a pier or wharf.}}

{{term|term=dockyard|content=[[dockyard]]}}
{{defn|A facility where ships or boats are built and repaired. Routinely used as a synonym for ''shipyard'', although ''dockyard'' is sometimes associated more closely with a facility used for maintenance and basing activities, while ''shipyard'' sometimes is associated more closely with a facility used in construction.}}

{{term|dodger}}
{{defn|A hood forward of a hatch or cockpit to protect the crew from wind and spray. Can be soft or hard.}}

{{term|term=dog|content=[[Dog (engineering)#Door dog|dog]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''door dog''' and '''hatch dog'''.}}
Device to secure doors and hatches. Typically used for watertight openings, but can apply elsewhere. "Dogging the hatches" is a common phrase.}}

{{term|term=dog watch|content=[[dog watch]]}}
{{defn|A short [[Watch system|watch]] period, generally half the usual time (e.g., a two-hour watch rather than a four-hour one). Such watches might be included in order to rotate the system over different days for fairness, or to allow both watches to eat their meals at approximately normal times.}}

{{term|doghouse}}
{{defn|A slang term (in the US, mostly) for a raised portion of a ship's deck. A doghouse is usually added to improve headroom below or to shelter a hatch.}}

{{term|dogvane}}
{{defn|A small [[weather vane]], sometimes improvised with a scrap of cloth, yarn, or other light material mounted within sight of the {{gli|helmsman}}. See ''{{nautical term|tell-tale}}''.}}

{{term|term=doldrums|content=[[doldrums]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''equatorial calms'''.}}
The equatorial trough, with special reference to the light and variable nature of the winds generally encountered there.<ref>{{cite book |editor-first1=Todd S. |editor-last1=Glickman |location=Cambridge, Massachusetts |date=June 2000 |url=http://glossary.ametsoc.org/?p=1&query=doldrums |title=Doldrums |chapter=Meteorology Glossary |format=electronic |edition=2nd |publisher=[[American Meteorological Society]] |access-date=March 10, 2014}}</ref>}}

{{term|dolly winch}}
{{defn|A small winch mounted on the {{nautical term|windlass}}, used as an alternative to the {{gli|brail|brails}} winch when that is obstructed in some way (e.g. by deck cargo).<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=dolphin|content=[[Dolphin (structure)|dolphin]]}}
{{defn|A structure consisting of a number of piles driven into the seabed or riverbed as a marker.}}

{{term|dolphin striker}}
{{defn|A spar protruding vertically beneath a [[bowsprit]], usually attached to the boswprit cap, used provide a mechanically advantageous run for the [[martingale stay]], and other ropes of a ship's rigging.}}

{{term|term=donkey engine|content=[[donkey engine]]}}
{{defn|A small auxiliary engine used either to start a larger engine or independently, e.g. for pumping water on steamships.<ref>{{cite book |page=533 |title=Encarta: World English Dictionary |publisher=[[St. Martin's Press]], [[Microsoft]] |location=New York |year=1999}}</ref>}}

{{term|donkeyman}}
{{defn|One of a ship's engineering crew. Often a crewman responsible for maintaining a [[steam donkey]], or any machinery other than the main engines. On some ships, the Petty Officer in charge of engineroom ratings.}}

{{term|term=dorade box|content=[[dorade box]]}}
{{defn|A dorade box (also called a dorade vent, collector box, or simply a "ventilator") is a type of vent that permits the passage of air in and out of the cabin or engine room of a boat while keeping rain, spray, and sea wash out.}}

{{term|term=dory|content=[[Dory (boat)|dory]] {{anchor|dorey|dori|doree}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''doree''', '''dori''', or (Royal Navy) '''dorey'''.}}
A shallow-draft, lightweight boat, about {{convert|5|to|7|m|ft}} long, with high sides, a flat bottom, and sharp {{gli|bow|bows}}. Traditionally used as fishing boats, both in coastal waters and in the open sea.}}

{{term|double-banked}}
{{defn|(of the arrangement of oars on a boat) having two oarsmen seated on each {{nautical term|thwart}}, each of whom operates one oar on their side of the boat. This contrasts with {{nautical term|single-banked}}, where only one oarsman is seated on each thwart operating one oar on one side of the boat, with the oars alternating between port and starboard along the length of the boat. A third arrangement is to have one rower on each thwart working two oars, one on each side of the boat.<ref name="McKee 1983">{{cite book |last1=McKee |first1=Eric |title=Working Boats of Britain, Their Shape and Purpose |date=1983 |publisher=Conway Maritime Press |location=London |isbn=0-85177-277-3 |edition=1997}}</ref>{{rp|135}}}}

{{term|double-shotted}}
{{defn|The practice of loading smoothbore cannon with two cannonballs.}}

{{term|doubling the angle on the bow}}
{{defn|A technique for establishing the distance from a point on land, such as a headland that is being passed. This is a type of {{nautical term|running bearing}} which requires no plotting on the chart. The ship is sailed on a constant course and speed. The distance shown on the log is noted when the {{nautical term|relative bearing}} of a fixed point is taken, and the increase in that bearing is watched until it is twice the original bearing, and the log is read again. The distance travelled between the two bearings is the distance of the ship from the fixed point when the second bearing was taken. Allowances for tidal streams may or may not be allowed for, depending on the accuracy required.<ref>{{cite web |title=Double the angle on the bow |url=https://deckskills.tripod.com/cadetsite/id100.html |website=Deckskills |access-date=18 February 2021 |archive-date=2021-04-22  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210422164329/https://deckskills.tripod.com/cadetsite/id100.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Read 2014d"/> }}

{{term|Dover cliffs}}
{{defn|A slang term for very rough seas with large white-capped waves.}}

{{term|downbound}}
{{defn|no=1|Travel downstream, with a following current.<ref name="USCG NavRules">{{cite web |url=https://www.navcen.uscg.gov/pdf/navRules/navrules.pdf |title=Navigational Rules: International—Inland |publisher=United States Coast Guard |pages=21, 31 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190503012547/https://www.navcen.uscg.gov/pdf/navRules/navrules.pdf |archive-date=3 May 2019 |url-status=live |access-date=17 November 2019}}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=2|Eastward travel in the [[Great Lakes]] region (terminology used by the [[Great Lakes St. Lawrence Seaway Development Corporation]]).<ref name="SLSDC">{{cite web |url=http://www.greatlakes-seaway.com/en/pdf/overview_brochure.pdf |title=Great Lakes/St. Lawrence Seaway System: An overview of North America's most dynamic waterway |publisher=Saint Lawrence Seaway Development Corporation |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190221025340/http://www.greatlakes-seaway.com/en/pdf/overview_brochure.pdf |archive-date=21 February 2019 |url-status=live |access-date=17 November 2019}}</ref>}}

{{term|downflooding}}
{{defn|The entry of water through any opening into the {{gli|hull}} or {{nautical term|superstructure}} of an undamaged vessel, such as an open door or {{nautical term|porthole}}, loose or open hatch, ventilator opening, etc. Downflooding can occur due to a ship's {{nautical term|trim}}, if she {{gli|heel|heels}} or {{gli|list|lists}},  or if she becomes totally or partially submerged.}}

{{term|term=downhaul|content=[[downhaul]]}}
{{defn|A line used to control either a mobile {{nautical term|spar}}, or the shape of a sail. A downhaul can also be used to retrieve a sail back on deck.}}

{{term|drabbler}}
{{defn|An extra strip of canvas secured below a {{gli|bonnet}}, further to increase the area of a course.}}

{{term|term=draft|content=[[Draft (hull)|draft]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''draught'''.}}
The depth of a ship's {{gli|keel}} below the {{nautical term|waterline}}.}}

{{term|dragger}}
{{defn|no=1|A fishing {{nautical term|trawler}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|dredger}}.}}

{{term|term=dragon boat|content=[[dragon boat]]}}
{{defn|One of a family of traditional paddled long boats of various designs and sizes found throughout Asia, Africa, and the Pacific islands. For competitive events, they are generally rigged with decorative Chinese dragon heads and tails. Dragon boat races are traditionally held during the annual summer solstice festival.}}

{{term|draught}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Alternative spelling of ''{{gli|draft}}''.}}}}

{{term|term=dreadnought|content=[[dreadnought]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{gli|battleship}} designed with an "all-big-gun" armament layout in which the ship's primary gun power resided in a primary battery of its largest guns intended for use at long range, with other gun armament limited to small weapons intended for close-range defense against [[torpedo boat]]s and other small warships. Most, but not all, dreadnoughts also had steam turbine propulsion. Predominant from 1906, dreadnoughts differed from earlier steam battleships, retroactively dubbed ''[[Pre-dreadnought battleship|predreadnoughts]]'', which had only a few large guns, relied on an intermediate secondary battery used at shorter ranges for most of their offensive power, and had [[triple-expansion steam engine]]s.}}

{{term|term=dredger|content=[[Dredging|dredger]]}}
{{defn|A vessel specialized for use in the excavation of material from a water environment and equipped with heavy machinery for this purpose.}}

{{term|term=dressing overall|content=[[dressing overall]]}}
{{defn|To string [[International Code of Signals]] flags, arranged at random, from {{nautical term|stemhead}} to {{nautical term|masthead}}, between mastheads (if the vessel has more than one mast), and then down to the {{nautical term|taffrail}}, on a ship in harbor as a sign of celebration of a national, local, or personal anniversary, event, holiday, or occasion. When a ship is properly dressed overall, {{gli|ensign|ensigns}} fly at each masthead unless displaced by another flag (e.g. that of a {{gli|flag officer}} on board), in addition to the ensign flown in the usual position at the {{nautical term|stern}}.}}

{{term|dressing down}}
{{defn|no=1|Treating old sails with oil or wax to renew them.}}
{{defn|no=2|A verbal reprimand.}}

{{term|dressing lines}}
{{defn|Lines running from {{nautical term|stemhead}} to {{nautical term|masthead}}, between mastheads, and then down to the {{nautical term|taffrail}}, to which flags are attached when a ship is {{gli|dress overall|dressed overall}}.}}

{{term|term=drifter|content=[[Drifter (fishing boat)|drifter]]}}
{{defn|A type of fishing boat designed to catch [[herring]] in a long [[drift net]], long used in the Netherlands and Great Britain.}}

{{term|drink}}
{{defn|Overboard and into the water (e.g. "it fell into the drink").}}

{{term|driver}}
{{defn|The large sail flown from the {{nautical term|mizzen}} {{gli|gaff}}.}}

{{term|driver-mast}}
{{defn|The fifth {{nautical term|mast}} of a six-masted {{gli|barquentine}} or {{gli|gaff}} {{nautical term|schooner}}. It is preceded by the {{gli|jigger mast}} and followed by the {{nautical term|spanker mast}}. The sixth mast of the only seven-masted vessel, the gaff schooner ''[[Thomas W. Lawson (ship)|Thomas W. Lawson]]'', was normally called the pusher-mast.}}

{{term|drogher}}
{{defn|See ''{{gli|disposable ship}}''.}}

{{term|term=drogue|content=[[drogue]]}}
{{defn|A device to slow a boat down in a storm so that it does not speed excessively down the slope of a wave and crash into the next one. It is generally constructed of heavy flexible material in the shape of a cone. See also ''{{nautical term|sea anchor}}''.}}

{{term|term=drudging|content=[[drudging]]}}
{{defn|A technique of maintaining {{nautical term|steerageway}} when going downstream with neither engine nor wind to sail. The vessel uses its anchor to draw itself head-to-stream, then lifts the anchor and drifts stern-first downstream, ferry gliding to maintain position within the stream. As steerage begins to reduce, the vessel anchors again and then repeats the whole procedure as required.}}

{{term|term=drydock|content=[[drydock]]}}
{{defn|A narrow basin or vessel used for the construction, maintenance, and repair of ships, boats, and other watercraft that can be flooded to allow a load to be floated in, then drained to allow that load to come to rest on a dry platform.}}

{{term|drying harbour}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''drying mooring'''.}}
A harbour where the water wholly or partly recedes as the tide goes out, leaving any vessel moored there {{gli|aground}}.}}

{{term|dump scow}}
{{defn|A type of [[scow]] designed to dump waste material at sea.}}

{{term|dunnage}}
{{defn|no=1|Loose packing material used to protect a ship's cargo from damage during transport. See also ''{{gli|fardage}}''.}}
{{defn|no=2|Personal baggage.}}

{{term|term=Dutch barge|content=[[Dutch barge]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''schuyt'''.}}
Any of several types of traditional flat-bottomed shoal-draught sailing barge, originally used for carrying cargo in the Zuyder Zee and on the rivers of the Netherlands.}}

{{term|term=Dutch built|content=[[Dutch built]]}}
{{defn|Term of abuse implying shoddiness or (when directed at a person) stupidity or stubbornness, usually embellished with other oaths and insults tagged on fore and aft.}}
{{Glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==E==
{{glossary}}
{{term|earings}}
{{defn|Small lines by which the uppermost corners of the largest sails are secured to the {{nautical term|yardarm|yardarms}}.}}

{{term|term=East Indiaman|content=[[East Indiaman]]}}
{{defn|Any ship operating under charter or license to the East India Company (England), or to the [[Danish East India Company]], [[Louis XIV's East India Company|French East India Company]], [[Dutch East India Company]], [[Portuguese East India Company]], or [[Swedish East India Company]] from the 17th to the 19th centuries.}}

{{term|term=echo sounding|content=[[echo sounding]]}}
{{defn|The measurement of the depth of a body of water using a [[SONAR]] device. See also ''{{nautical term|sounding}}'' and ''{{nautical term|swinging the lead}}''.}}

{{term|embayed}}
{{defn|A condition in which a sailing vessel (especially one that sails poorly to {{nautical term|windward}}) is confined between two capes or headlands by a wind blowing directly onshore.}}

{{term|en echelon}}
{{defn|
[[File:Minas Geraes-class battleships.jpg|thumb|right|Diagram showing the ''Minas Geraes''-class battleship with its central guns arranged ''en echelon''.]]
An arrangement of gun turrets whereby the turret on one side of the ship is placed further {{gli|aft}} than the one on the other side, so that both turrets can fire to either side.}}

{{term|term=engine order telegraph|content=[[engine order telegraph]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''chadburn'''.}}
A communications device used by the pilot to order engineers in the {{gli|engine room}} to power the vessel at a certain desired speed.}}

{{term|term=engine room|content=[[engine room]]}}
{{defn|One of the machinery spaces of a vessel, usually the largest one, containing the ship's prime mover (usually a diesel or steam engine or a gas or steam turbine). Larger vessels may have more than one engine room.}}

{{term|ensign}}
{{defn|no=1|[[Ensign (flag)|(flag)]] The principal flag or banner flown by a ship to indicate her nationality.}}
{{defn|no=2|([[ensign (rank)|rank]]) The lowest grade of [[commissioned officer]] in the US Navy.}}

{{term|term=escort carrier|content=[[escort carrier]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{gli|aircraft carrier}}, smaller and slower than a {{gli|fleet carrier}}, used by some navies in World War II to escort convoys, ferry aircraft, conduct {{gli|anti-submarine warfare}}, and provide air support for [[amphibious operation]]s.}}

{{term|estimated position}}
{{defn|An approximate geographical position obtained by making allowances for leeway, tide, and currents to a {{gli|dead reckoning}} position (which is calculated from the distance run and the course steered).}}

{{term|term=evaporator|content=[[Evaporator (marine)|evaporator]]}}
{{defn|A piece of ship's equipment used to produce fresh drinking water from sea water by distillation.}}

{{term|executive officer}}
{{defn|The officer second in command on a warship.  Also called "X.O." in the United States and "Number One" in the Royal Navy and Commonwealth navies.}}

{{term|term=express boat}}
{{defn|An alternative term for a {{gli|flyboat}}, a high-speed {{gli|canal boat}} operating in England in the 1830s and 1840s.}}

{{term|extra ship}}
{{defn|A term used by the British [[East India Company]] from the seventeenth to the nineteenth century for merchant ships it hired to make voyages for it between England (later the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|United Kingdom]]) and ports east of the [[Cape of Good Hope]], a trade over which the company held a strict monopoly. "Extra ships" were chartered for a single round-trip voyage beginning during a single sailing season (September to April) and augmented the voyages of "{{nautical term|regular ship|regular ships}}", which were merchant ships under long-term charter to make repeated voyages for the company over many seasons. However, if an "extra ship" operated well and the company needed its services, the company often chartered it repeatedly over a number of seasons.<ref name=threedecksshiproles/>}}

{{term|extremis}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''in extremis'''.}}
The point under International Rules of the Road ([[International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea|Navigation Rules]]) at which the privileged (or stand-on) vessel on collision course with a burdened (or give-way) vessel determines it must maneuver to avoid a collision. Prior to extremis, the privileged vessel must maintain course and speed and the burdened vessel must maneuver to avoid collision.}}

{{term|term=eye splice|content=[[eye splice]]}}
{{defn|A closed loop or eye at the end of a line, rope, cable, etc. It is made by unraveling its end and joining it to itself by intertwining it into the lay of the line. Eye [[rope splicing|splices]] are very strong and compact and are frequently employed in moorings and docking lines, among other uses.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==F==
<!--
<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
-->
{{glossary}}
{{term|term=factory ship|content=[[factory ship]]}}
{{defn|A large oceangoing vessel with extensive on-board facilities for processing and freezing caught fish or whales. Some also serve as {{nautical term|mother ship|mother ships}} for smaller fishing or whaling vessels. Those used for processing fish are also known as ''fish processing vessels''.}}

{{term|fair}}
{{defn|no=1|A smooth curve, usually referring to a line of the {{gli|hull}} with minimum localised deviations.}}
{{defn|no=2|To make something flush.}}
{{defn|no=3|A {{gli|line}} is fair when it has a clear run.}}
{{defn|no=4|A wind or current is fair when it offers an advantage to a boat.}}

{{term|fair winds and following seas}}
{{defn|A blessing wishing the recipient a safe journey and good fortune.}}

{{term|term=fairlead|content=[[fairlead]]}}
{{defn|A device used to keep a {{gli|line}} or chain running in the correct direction or to give it a {{gli|fair}} {{gli|lead}} to prevent it rubbing or fouling.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|1=fairing}}
{{defn|2=1|1=(noun) A structure that improves the streamlining of a vessel.}}
{{defn|2=2|1=(verb) The process of making  a curve or structure {{gli|fair}}. }}

{{term|1=fairwater}}
{{defn|2=1|1=A structure that improves the streamlining of a vessel.}}
{{defn|2=2|1=On submarines: The superstructure ( [[Sail (submarine)|sail]] ) of the boat. The fairwater serves as a streamlined housing for the submarine's periscopes and other sensor masts, as well as the air intake, snorkel masts (if applicable).
It also provides a degree of elevation above the sea, and is therefore typically equipped with a small [[bridge (nautical)]].
If the submarine is equipped with a [[conning tower]], this structure will also be located somewhere inside of the fairwater.}}

{{term|term=fairway|content=[[Fairway (navigation)|fairway]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A navigable channel (e.g., in a harbor or offshore) that is the usual course taken by vessels in the area.}}
{{defn|no=2|In military and naval terms, a channel from offshore, in a river, or in a harbor that has enough depth to accommodate the {{gli|draft}} of large vessels.}}

{{term|fake}}
{{defn|A single turn of rope in a coil or on a drum. A group of fakes is known as a {{nautical term|tier}}. See also ''{{gli|fake down}}''.<ref name="Read 2014f">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014f |c= F |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/f-16182}}</ref>{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}<ref name=smyth>{{cite book |last=Smyth|first=William Henry |author-link=William Henry Smyth |title=The Sailor's Word-Book|url=https://archive.org/details/sailorswordbook00smytgoog|year=1867|publisher=Blackie & Co|location=Glasgow}}</ref>{{rp|200, 286}}}}

{{term|fake down}}
{{defn|To lay a coil of rope down so that it will run easily; that is, with rope feeding off the top of the coil and the bitter end at the bottom. Often confused with {{gli|flake}}. See also ''{{nautical term|range}}''.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=falkuša|content=[[falkuša]]}}
{{defn|A traditional fishing boat with a {{gli|lateen}} sail on a single mast used by fishermen from the town of [[Komiža]] on the Adriatic island of [[Vis (island)|Vis]].}}

{{term|fall}}
{{defn|The part of the {{nautical term|tackle}} that is hauled upon.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|fall off}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''bear down''', '''bear away''', '''bear off''', or '''head down'''.}}
To change the direction of sail so as to point in a direction that is more downwind; to bring the {{gli|bow}} {{gli|leeward}}. This is the opposite of pointing up or heading up.}}

{{term|fantail}}
{{defn|The {{gli|aft}} end of a ship, also known as the {{nautical term|poop deck}}.}}

{{term|fardage}}
{{defn|Wood placed in the bottom of a ship to keep cargo dry. See also ''{{gli|dunnage}}''.}}

{{term|fashion boards}}
{{defn|Loose boards that slide in grooves to close off a {{gli|companionway}} or {{gli|cabin}} entrance.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|fast}}
{{defn|Fastened or held firmly (e.g. "fast {{gli|aground}}": stuck on the seabed; or "made fast": tied securely).<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=fast attack craft|content=[[fast attack craft]]}}
{{defn|A small, fast, agile {{nautical term|warship}} armed with anti-ship missiles, guns, or torpedoes. The fast attack craft replaced the {{nautical term|torpedo boat}} during the second half of the 20th century in the role of a cheap, offensively-oriented {{gli|inshore}} warship.}}

{{term|term=fast combat support ship|content=[[fast combat support ship]]}}
{{defn|The largest type of US Navy combat logistics ship, designed to serve as a combined oiler, ammunition ship, and supply ship. The first fast combat support ship entered service in the mid-1960s.}}

{{term|fast supply vessel}}
{{term|fast support vessel|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|crew boat}}.}}}}

{{term|term=fathom|content=[[fathom]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A unit of length equal to {{convert|6|ft|m}}, roughly measured as the distance between a man's outstretched hands. Particularly used in {{nautical term|sounding}} as a measurement of the depth of a body of water.}}
{{defn|no=2|To measure the depth of water; to engage in {{nautical term|sounding}}.}}

{{term|fathomer}}
{{defn|A person engaged in {{nautical term|sounding}} to determine the depth of water.}}

{{term|term=fathometer|content=[[fathometer]]}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|sounding|depth finder}} that uses sound waves to determine the depth of water.}}

{{term|favored side}}
{{defn|The side of the course that gets you to the next mark faster, due to more wind, favorable shifts, less current, smaller waves, etc.}}

{{term|term=felucca|content=[[felucca]]}}
{{defn|A traditional wooden sailing boat with a {{nautical term|rig}} consisting of one or two {{gli|lateen}} sails, used in protected waters of the [[Red Sea]] and eastern [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]] and particularly along the [[Nile River|Nile]] in Egypt and Sudan, as well as in Iraq.}}

{{term|fend off}}
{{defn|A command given to the crew to stop what they are now doing and to immediately manually prevent the boat from banging into the {{gli|dock|docks}} or other boats.}}

{{term|term=fender|content=[[Fender (boating)|fender]]}}
{{defn|A flexible bumper used in boating to keep boats from banging into docks or each other. Often an old car tire.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=ferry|content=[[ferry]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''ferryboat'''}}
A {{nautical term|merchant ship}} used to carry passengers, and sometimes vehicles and cargo, across a body of water.}}

{{term|ferry glide}}
{{defn|To hold a vessel against and at an angle to the current/stream such that the vessel moves sideways over the bottom due to the effect of the current operating on the upstream side of the vessel.}}

{{term|term=ferry slip|content=[[ferry slip]]}}
{{defn|A specialized docking facility designed to receive a ferryboat or train ferry.}}

{{term|fetch}}
{{defn|no=1|The distance across water a wind or waves have traveled.}}
{{defn|no=2|To reach a mark without {{nautical term|tacking}}.}}

{{term|fid}}
{{defn|no=1|A tapered wooden tool used for separating the strands of rope for splicing.}}
{{defn|no=2|A bar used to fix an upper {{nautical term|mast}} in place.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|term=Fiddley|content=[[Fiddley]] {{anchor|Fiddley}}}}
{{defn|the vertical space above a vessel's engine room extending into its stack, usually covered by an iron grating. Also applied to the framework around the opening itself }}

{{term|term=fife rail|content=[[fife rail]]}}
{{defn|A freestanding {{nautical term|pinrail}} surrounding the base of a {{nautical term|mast}} and used for securing that mast's sails' {{gli|halyard|halyards}} with a series of {{gli|belaying pin|belaying pins}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|term=Fifie|content=[[Fifie]]}}
{{defn|A sailing boat with two masts with a standard rig consisting of a main dipping {{gli|lug sail}} and a mizzen standing lug sail. Developed in Scotland and used for commercial fishing from the 1850s until the 20th century.}}

{{term|fig}}
{{defn|US Navy slang for a guided-missile {{gli|frigate}}, especially of the [[Oliver Hazard Perry-class frigate|''Oliver Hazard Perry'' class]], derived from its class designation ("FFG").}}

{{term|fight his ship}}
{{defn|To fight his ship (or to fight her ship) is a naval term that denotes a captain taking their vessel into combat or directing their vessel in combat.}}

{{term|fighting top}}
{{defn|An enlarged {{nautical term|top}} designed to allow gunfire downward onto an enemy ship. A fighting top could have small guns installed in it or could serve as a platform for [[sniper]]s armed with [[musket]]s or [[rifle]]s.}}

{{term|figure of eight}}
{{defn|A stopper knot.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=figurehead|content=[[figurehead (object)|figurehead]]}}
{{defn|A symbolic image, particularly a carved effigy, at the head of a traditional sailing ship or early steamer.}}

{{term|fin}}
{{defn|A term used in European and Commonwealth countries for a tower-like structure on the dorsal (topside) surface of a submarine; called a ''[[Sail (submarine)|sail]]'' in the United States.}}

{{term|fine}}
{{defn|Narrow in appearance from the vantage point of a {{gli|lookout}} or other person viewing activity in the vicinity of a ship, e.g. another ship off the {{nautical term|starboard}} {{gli|bow}} with her bow or {{nautical term|stern}} facing the viewer's ship could be described as "fine on the starboard bow" of the viewer's ship.}}

{{term|fine lines}}
{{defn|Descriptive term for a vessel with a hull shape designed for an efficient flow of water around the hull. Simply described by comparing the hull shape to a [[rectangular cuboid]] with the same length, breadth and height as the submerged part of the hull. The more that you have to carve off that cuboid to get the hull's shape, the finer the lines. More accurately this is measured as the [[block coefficient]] or the [[prismatic coefficient]].}}

{{term|term=fireboat|content=[[fireboat]]}}
{{defn|A specialized vessel equipped with [[firefighting]] equipment such as pumps and nozzles for fighting shipboard and shoreline fires.}}

{{term|term=fireman|content=[[fireman (steam engine)|fireman]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''stoker''', '''boilerman''', or '''watertender'''.}}
1.&nbsp;A job associated with tending the fire for a boiler.}}
{{defn|no=2|A US Navy {{nautical term|rating|rate}} in the engineering department equivalent to {{nautical term|seaman}}.}}

{{term|term=fire ship|content=[[fire ship]]}}
{{defn|A ship loaded with flammable materials and explosives and sailed into an enemy port or fleet either already burning or ready to be set alight by its crew (who would then abandon it) in order to collide with and set fire to enemy ships.}}

{{term|term=fire room|content=[[fire room]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''boiler room'''.}}
The compartment in which a ship's boilers or furnaces are stoked and fired.}}

{{term|term=first-rate|content=[[first-rate]]}}
{{defn|The classification for the largest sailing warships of the 17th through the 19th centuries. Such vessels often had up to three masts, 850+ crew, and 100+ guns.}}

{{term|term=first lieutenant|content=[[first lieutenant]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In the Royal Navy, the senior [[lieutenant]] on board; responsible to the [[commanding officer]] for the domestic affairs of the {{nautical term|ship's company}}. Also known as 'Jimmy the One' or 'Number One'. Removes his cap when visiting the mess decks as a token of respect for the privacy of the crew in those quarters. Officer in charge of {{gli|cable|cables}} on the {{gli|forecastle}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|In the US Navy, the officer on a ship serving as the senior person in charge of all {{gli|deck hand|deck hands}}.}}

{{term|term=first mate|content=[[chief mate|first mate]]}}
{{defn|The second-in-command of a commercial ship.}}

{{term|fish}}
{{defn|no=1|To repair a {{nautical term|mast}} or {{nautical term|spar}} with a fillet of wood.}}
{{defn|no=2|To secure an {{gli|anchor}} on the side of a ship for sea (otherwise known as "catting".)}}
{{defn|no=3|A slang term for a self-propelled [[torpedo]].}}

{{term|fisherman's reef}}
{{defn|A sailing tactic for handling winds too strong for the sail area hoisted when reefing the sails is not feasible or possible. The {{gli|headsail}} is set normally while the {{nautical term|mainsail}} is let out until it is constantly {{gli|luffing}}. This creates a loss of force on the main and also reduces the efficiency of the headsail while still retaining sailing control of the vessel.}}

{{term|fisherman's sail}}
{{defn|On a {{nautical term|staysail}} {{nautical term|schooner}}, the fisherman is a quadrilateral sail set between the two masts above the main staysail. It is used in light to moderate airs.}}

{{term|term=fitting-out|content=[[fitting-out]]}}
{{defn|The period after a ship is [[ship naming and launching|launched]] during which all the remaining construction of the ship is completed and she is readied for {{nautical term|sea trial|sea trials}} and delivery to her owners.}}

{{term|fixed propeller}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|propeller}} mounted on a rigid shaft protruding from the hull of a vessel, usually driven by an inboard motor; steering must be done using a {{nautical term|rudder}}. See also ''{{nautical term|outboard motor}}'' and ''{{nautical term|sterndrive}}''.}}

{{term|flag hoist}}
{{defn|A number of {{nautical term|signal flag|signal flags}} strung together to convey a message, e.g. "[[England expects that every man will do his duty]]".}}

{{term|term=flag of convenience|content=[[flag of convenience]]}}
{{defn|The business practice of registering a {{nautical term|merchant ship}} in a sovereign state different from that of the ship's owners, and flying that state's civil {{gli|ensign}} on the ship. The practice allows the ship's owner to reduce operating costs or avoid the regulations of the owner's country.}}

{{term|term=flag officer|content=[[flag officer]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A commissioned officer senior enough to be entitled to fly a flag to mark the ship or installation under his or her command, in English-speaking countries usually referring to the senior officers of a navy, specifically to those who hold any of the {{gli|admiral}} ranks and in some cases to those holding the rank of {{gli|commodore}}. In modern American usage, additionally applied to [[United States Coast Guard|U.S. Coast Guard]], [[NOAA Commissioned Officer Corps|National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration Commissioned Officer Corps]], and [[United States Public Health Service Commissioned Corps|U.S. Public Health Service Commissioned Corps]] officers and [[general officer]]s in the [[United States Army|U.S. Army]], [[United States Air Force|U.S. Air Force]], and [[United States Marine Corps|U.S. Marine Corps]] entitled to fly their own flags.}}
{{defn|no=2|A formal rank in the mid-19th century [[United States Navy]], conveyed temporarily upon senior {{gli|captain|captains}} in command of squadrons of ships, soon rendered obsolete by the creation of the ranks of [[Commodore (United States)|commodore]] and [[Rear Admiral (United States)|rear admiral]].}}

{{term|term=flagship|content=[[flagship]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A vessel used by the commanding officer of a group of naval ships. The term derives from the custom of a commander of such a group of ships, characteristically a {{gli|flag officer}}, flying a distinguishing flag aboard the ship on which he or she is embarked.}}
{{defn|no=2|Used more loosely, the lead ship in a {{gli|fleet}} of naval or commercial vessels, typically the first, largest, fastest, most heavily armed, or, in terms of media coverage, best-known.}}

{{term|flake}}
{{defn|To set down in folds, as in stowing a sail or to {{nautical term|range}} a cable on deck so that it is clear to run. Not to be confused with {{gli|fake down}}.<ref name="Read 2014f"/> }}

{{term|term=flank|content=[[Flank speed|flank]]}}
{{defn|The maximum speed of a ship. Faster than "full speed".}}

{{term|flare}}
{{defn|no=1|A curvature of the topsides outward towards the {{gli|gunwale}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A pyrotechnic signalling device, usually used to indicate distress.}}

{{term|flatback}}
{{defn|A [[Great Lakes]] slang term for a vessel without any self-unloading equipment.}}

{{term|term=flatboat|content=[[flatboat]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''broadhorn'''.}}
A rectangular, flat-bottomed boat with square ends used to transport freight and passengers on inland waterways in the United States during the 18th and 19th centuries.}}

{{term|flattop}}
{{defn|A slang term for an {{gli|aircraft carrier}}.}}

{{term|fleet}}
{{defn|no=1|''[[Naval fleet]]'': The highest operational echelon of command of ships commanded by a single person in a navy, and typically the largest type of naval formation commanded by a single person. In modern times, usually (but not necessarily) a permanent formation.}}
{{defn|no=2|During the [[Age of Sail]], a [[Royal Navy]] term for any naval command larger than a squadron in size, or commanded by a [[Rear admiral (Royal Navy)|rear admiral]] and composed of five [[Ship-of-the-line|ships-of-the-line]] and any number of smaller vessels.}}
{{defn|no=3|''[[Merchant navy|Merchant fleet]]'', a collective term for the {{nautical term|merchant marine}} (known in the United Kingdom and Commonwealth countries as the {{nautical term|merchant navy}}) of a particular country.}}
{{defn|no=4|''[[Fishing fleet]]'': A term for an aggregate of commercial fishing vessels, commonly used either to describe all fishing vessels belonging to a single country, operating in a single region, operating out of a particular port, or engaged in particular type of fishing (e.g., ''the tuna fishing fleet''). The term does not imply that the vessels operate as part of a single organization.}}
{{defn|no=5|Informally, any grouping (based on physical proximity or sharing of a common organizational subordination) of naval or civilian vessels.}}
{{defn|no=6|Of a person, to move from one location to another aboard a vessel, or to change positions within a naval organization.}}
{{defn|no=7|To move up a rope{{snd}}especially when drawing the blocks of a tackle part{{snd}}to allow a greater advantage in {{gli|haul|hauling}}.}}
{{defn|no=8|To cause a rope or chain to slip down the barrel of a {{gli|capstan}} or {{nautical term|windlass}}.}}
{{defn|no=9|A former term for the process aboard a vessel of moving {{gli|deadeye|deadeyes}} when the {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}} become too long.}}
{{defn|no=10|A location where {{gli|barge|barges}} are secured.}}

{{term|term=fleet in being|content=[[fleet in being]]}}
{{defn|A naval force that extends a controlling influence on maritime operations without ever leaving port by forcing an opposing navy to maintain forces on station to {{gli|blockade}} it in port and oppose it if it comes out to fight. A navy which operates its forces as a ''fleet in being'' generally seeks to avoid actual combat with an enemy fleet for fear of losing a naval battle and thereby its ability to influence events and activities at sea.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.usnwc.edu/getattachment/b128b7e6-aa98-494e-be55-8000ba30db29/The-Idea-of-a--Fleet-in-Being--in-Historical-Persp.aspx|title=Hattendorf, John B. "The Idea of a "Fleet in Being" in Historical Perspective,"  ''Naval War College Review'' (Winter 2014)|access-date=2021-07-22 |archive-date=2014-05-08 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140508061726/https://www.usnwc.edu/getattachment/b128b7e6-aa98-494e-be55-8000ba30db29/The-Idea-of-a--Fleet-in-Being--in-Historical-Persp.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref>}}

{{term|flemish}}
{{defn|To coil a {{gli|line}} that is not in use so that it lies flat on the deck.}}

{{term|flettner rotor}}
{{defn|A spinning cylinder that uses the [[Magnus effect]] to harness wind power to propel a ship.}}

{{term|term=flight deck|content=[[flight deck]]}}
{{defn|A flat deck on an {{gli|aircraft carrier}} used for the launch and recovery of aircraft. In the United States Navy, the term ''flight deck'' also refers to a ''{{gli|helicopter deck}}'' on other types of ships.}}

{{term|floating futtock}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|futtock}} that is not attached to the other futtocks in the {{nautical term|frame}} of which it is part. Instead, it is attached to the hull planking, {{nautical term|ceiling}} and other longitudinal members. This demonstrates that building was done either shell-first or was frame-led, with phases of planking, then fitting of futtocks, followed by another band of planking.<ref name="Grenier et al v3 2007">{{cite book |last1=Grenier |first1=Robert |editor1-last=Grenier |editor1-first=Robert |editor2-last=Bernier |editor2-first=Marc-Andre |editor3-last=Stevens |editor3-first=Willis |title=The Underwater Archaeology of Red Bay. Vol. 3: The 24M Hull|date=2007 |publisher=Parks Canada |location=Ottawa |isbn=9780660196527}}</ref>{{rp|81, 125, 258, 274}}}}

{{term|flog the glass}}
{{defn|The act of vibrating or shaking a half-hour {{nautical term|marine sandglass}} — used until the early 19th century to time the length of a watch — to speed the passage of the sand in order to get off watch duty earlier.<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/oi/authority.20110803095824410|title=to flog the glass|journal=Oxford Reference|doi=10.1093/oi/authority.20110803095824410}}</ref>}}

{{term|floor}}
{{defn|Transverse structural {{nautical term|timber}}s which form that part of the lower {{gli|frame}} of a traditional wooden ship's {{gli|hull}} that sits immediately above the {{gli|keel}}. The frames continue upwards as pieces called {{gli|futtock}}s. A {{gli|keelson}} is usually fastened over the top of the floors.{{sfn|Greenhill|1988|pp=114-115, 236}}}}

{{term|floorhead}}
{{defn|Any of the upper extremities of the {{gli|floor}} of a vessel.}}

{{term|term=flotilla|content=[[flotilla]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In naval usage, a group of warships under a single commander that is smaller than a {{gli|fleet}} but otherwise not formally defined. A flotilla often is larger than a {{nautical term|squadron}}, and usually is made up of smaller vessels than those assigned to a squadron, but some flotillas are smaller than squadrons and some include larger vessels. In some navies, the term ''flotilla'' is reserved for naval formations that operate on inland bodies of water, while the terms ''fleet'' and ''squadron'' denote naval formations that operate at sea. A flotilla may be a permanent or temporary formation. In modern times, a flotilla sometimes is an administrative naval unit responsible for maintaining and supporting vessels but not for commanding their operations at sea.}}
{{defn|no=2|Informally, a group of naval or civilian vessels operating together or in close proximity to one another.}}

{{term|flotilla holiday}}
{{defn|A group of chartered {{nautical term|yacht|yachts}} that set out together on the same route.}}

{{term|term=flotilla leader|content=[[flotilla leader]]}}
{{defn|A warship suitable for commanding a {{gli|flotilla}} of destroyers or other small warships, typically a small {{gli|cruiser}} or a large {{gli|destroyer}}, in the latter case known as a ''{{gli|destroyer leader}}''.}}

{{term|term=flotsam|content=[[Flotsam and jetsam|flotsam]]}}
{{defn|Debris or cargo that remains afloat after a {{nautical term|shipwreck}}. See also ''{{gli|jetsam}}''.}}

{{term|term=fluke|content=[[wikt:Special:Search/fluke|fluke]]}}
{{defn|The wedge-shaped part of an {{gli|anchor}}'s arms that digs into the solid bottom beneath a body of water.}}

{{term|term=flush deck|content=[[flush deck]]}}
{{defn|An upper {{gli|deck}} of a vessel that extends unbroken from {{nautical term|stem}} to {{nautical term|stern}}.}}

{{term|term=flush decker|content=[[flush decker]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Any vessel with a {{gli|flush deck}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A US Navy {{gli|destroyer}} of the [[World War I]]-era {{sclass|Caldwell|destroyer|5}}, {{sclass|Wickes|destroyer|5}}, or {{sclass|Clemson|destroyer|4}}, produced in very large numbers.}}

{{term|flushing board}}
{{defn|A board inserted vertically in a {{gli|cabin}} entrance.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=fluyt|content=[[fluyt]] {{anchor|flute|fluit}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''fluit''' or '''flute'''.}}
A Dutch transoceanic sailing cargo vessel, {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} with two or three masts that were much taller than the masts of a {{gli|galleon}}, developed in the 16th century and widely used in the 17th and 18th centuries.}}

{{term|term=flyboat|content=[[flyboat]]}}
{{ghat|Also '''fly-boat'''.}}
{{defn|no=1|A light, agile vessel of Dutch origin used in Europe in the late 16th and early 17th centuries. Flyboats were designed as merchant cargo vessels, but many served as auxiliary warships. The flyboat was replaced by the {{gli|fluyt}} in the early 17th century.}}
{{defn|no=2|In England, a term used for the Dutch {{gli|fluyt}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|A high-speed horsedrawn {{gli|canal boat}} operated in England in the 1830s and 1840s. Also called an "express boat" or a "gig boat."}}
{{defn|no=4|A {{nautical term|narrowboat}} operating day and night without mooring on the canal system in England to provide an express cargo service. These "flyboats" were common by 1834 and operated until ca. 1914.}}

{{term|term=fly by night|content=[[wikt:fly-by-night|fly by night]]}}
{{defn|A large sail used only for sailing downwind, requiring little attention.}}

{{term|term=folding propeller|content=[[folding propeller]]}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|propeller}} with folding blades, furling so as to reduce drag on a sailing vessel when not in use.}}

{{term|term=following sea|content=[[following sea]]}}
{{defn|Waves going in the same direction as a ship, or within 15° of the heading, at a speed slower than the ship. See ''{{nautical term|overtaking sea}}'' for waves travelling faster than the ship.<ref name="Saunders V3 1965" />}}

{{term|foo-foo band}}
{{defn|An impromptu musical band on late 19th-century sailing vessels, made up from members of the ship's crew.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.arhoolie.com/world/the-foo-foo-band.html?sl=EN |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140311062811/http://www.arhoolie.com/world/the-foo-foo-band.html?sl=EN |website=Arhoolie Records |title=The Foo Foo Band |archive-date=2014-03-11}}</ref><ref>Lubbock, B. (1921): [https://archive.org/details/colonialclippers00lubbrich ''The Colonial Clippers''] pp. 158–59. – Via Internet Archive.</ref>}}

{{term|term=foot|content=[[Leach (sail)|foot]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The lower edge of any {{nautical term|sail}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|The bottom of a {{nautical term|mast}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|An Imperial unit of length equivalent to {{convert|12|in|cm}}.}}

{{term|footloose}}
{{defn|If the {{gli|foot}} of a sail is not secured properly, it is footloose, blowing around in the wind.}}

{{term|footboat}}
{{defn|A barge's boat or {{gli|dinghy}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=footrope|content=[[footrope]]}}
{{defn|Each yard on a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} sailing ship is equipped with a footrope for sailors to stand on while setting or stowing the sails.}}

{{term|force}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|Beaufort scale}}.}}}}

{{term|term=fore|content=fore {{anchor|forward|for'ard}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''forward''' (often written as '''for'ard''').}}
Toward the {{gli|bow}} of a vessel.}}

{{term|term=fore-and-aft rig|content=[[fore-and-aft rig]]}}
{{defn|A sailing {{nautical term|rig}} consisting mainly of sails that are set along the line of the {{gli|keel}} rather than perpendicular to it. Such sails, and the vessel itself, are said to be ''fore-and-aft-rigged''.}}

{{term|fore-and-afters}}
{{defn|Removable wooded beams running along the centre of the hold openings, beneath the hatches that they support.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|fore horse}}
{{defn|A transverse wooden or iron beam {{gli|afore}} the main mast to which the {{gli|foresail}} sheet is attached.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=forecastle|content=[[forecastle]]}}
{{defn|(pronounced {{IPAc-en|ˈ|f|oʊ|k|s|əl}}) A partial {{gli|deck}} above the upper deck and at the head of the vessel; traditionally the location of the sailors' living quarters. The name is derived from the castle fitted to bear archers in time of war.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|foredeck}}
{{defn|The portion of the {{gli|deck}} that is {{gli|forward}} of the forward {{nautical term|mast}}. }}

{{term|term=forefoot|content=[[Forefoot (ship)|forefoot]]}}
{{defn|The lower part of the {{nautical term|stem}} of a ship.}}

{{term|forehold}}
{{defn|The forward (i.e., front) part of a {{gli|hold}}.}}

{{term|foremast jack}}
{{defn|An enlisted sailor, one who is housed before the {{gli|foremast}}.}}

{{term|forepeak}}
{{defn|The part of the {{gli|hold}} of a ship within the angle of the {{gli|bow}}.}}

{{term|term=foresail|content=[[foresail]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} sail set on the {{gli|foremast}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|The lowest sail set on the foremast of a {{gli|full-rigged ship}} or other {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} vessel.}}

{{term|term=forestay|content=[[forestay]]}}
{{defn|A long {{gli|line}} or {{gli|cable}} reaching from the bow of the vessel to the {{nautical term|masthead|mastheads}}, used to support the {{nautical term|mast}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|forestaysail}}
{{defn|A triangular sail set on the {{gli|forestay}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|fother}}
{{defn|To cover a leak in a hull with a sail or other piece of canvas, usually by hauling it down the ships side using ropes passing under the keel.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=foul|content=[[Foul (nautical)|foul]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Having freedom of motion interfered with by collision or entanglement; entangled; the opposite of {{gli|clear}}. For instance, a rope is foul when it does not run straight or smoothly, and an anchor is foul when it is caught on an obstruction.}}
{{defn|no=2|A ship's bottom is foul when it is overgrown with marine life such as [[barnacle]]s.}}
{{defn|no=3|An area of water treacherous to navigation due to many shallow obstructions such as reefs, sandbars, rocks, etc.}}
{{defn|no=4|A breach of racing rules.}}
{{defn|no=5|''Foul the range'': To block another vessel from firing her guns at a target.}}

{{term|foulies}}
{{defn|A slang term for {{nautical term|oilskin|oilskins}}, the foul-weather clothing worn by sailors.}}

{{term|term=founder|content=[[wikt:founder|founder]]}}
{{defn|To fill with water and sink.}}

{{term|term=four piper|content=[[four piper]]}}
{{defn|A term sometimes used to refer to [[United States Navy]] four-funneled {{gli|destroyer|destroyers}} of the {{Sclass|Bainbridge|destroyer|5}}, {{Sclass|Paulding|destroyer|5}}, {{Sclass|Wickes|destroyer|5}}, and {{Sclass|Clemson|destroyer|5}} classes, all built for service in [[World War I]].}}

{{term|term=fourth rate|content=[[fourth rate]]}}
{{defn|In the British [[Royal Navy]] during the first half of the 18th century, a {{nautical term|ship-of-the-line}} mounting between 46 and 60 guns.}}

{{term|frame}}
{{defn|A transverse structural member that gives the {{gli|hull}} strength and shape. Wooden frames may be sawn, bent, or laminated into shape; planking is then fastened to the frames. In traditional wooden ship building, an individual frame may be made of the following individual parts: {{gli|floor}}, several {{gli|futtock}}s, then a top timber as the last component closest to the deck. If the hull is built frame-first, these frame components are fastened to each other. In a shell-first (or planking-first) construction, they are only fastened to the hull planking.{{sfn|Steffy|1994|p=271}}}}

{{term|term=freeboard|content=[[Freeboard (ship)|freeboard]]}}
{{defn|The height of a ship's {{gli|hull}} (excluding the superstructure) above the {{nautical term|waterline}}; the vertical distance from the current waterline to the lowest point on the highest continuous watertight deck. This usually varies from one part to another.}}

{{term|term=freighter|content=[[Cargo ship|freighter]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|cargo ship}}.}}

{{term|term=frigate|content=[[frigate]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In the 17th century, any warship built for speed and maneuverability.}}
{{defn|no=2|In the 18th and early 19th centuries, a sailing warship with a single continuous gun deck, typically used for patrolling, blockading, etc., but not in line of battle.}}
{{defn|no=3|In the second half of the 19th century, a type of warship combining sail and steam propulsion, typically of ironclad timber construction, with all guns on one deck.}}
{{defn|no=4|In the 20th and 21st centuries, a warship, smaller than a {{gli|destroyer}}, originally introduced during [[World War II]] as an anti-submarine vessel but now general-purpose.}}
{{defn|no=5|In the US Navy from the 1950s until the 1970s, a type of [[guided-missile]] [[antiaircraft]] ship built on a destroyer-sized hull, all of which were reclassified as "[[guided-missile cruiser]]s" in 1975.}}

{{term|full and by}}
{{defn|Sailing into the wind (''by''), but not as {{gli|close-hauled}} as might be possible, so as to make sure the sails are kept ''full''. This provides a margin for error to avoid being taken {{gli|aback}} in a tricky sea (a serious risk for {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} vessels). Figuratively it implies getting on with the job but in a steady, relaxed way, without undue urgency or strain.}}

{{term|term=full-rigged ship|content=[[full-rigged ship]] {{anchor|full-rigged|full rigged|full rigged ship|full rigging|fully-rigged|fully rigged|fully-rigged ship|fully rigged ship}}}}
{{defn|A sailing vessel with three or more {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, all of them {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}}. A full-rigged ship is said to have a "ship rig".}}

{{term|full steam ahead}}
{{defn|With as much speed as possible.}}

{{term|term=funnel|content=funnel {{anchor|stack}}}}
{{defn|no=1|([[Funnel (ship)|funnel]]) ''Also '''stack'''''. The smokestack of a ship, used to expel boiler steam and smoke or engine exhaust.}}
{{defn|no=2|''Ventilation funnel'': A curved, rotatable tube protruding from the deck of a vessel, designed to direct fresh air into her interior.}}

{{term|term=Furious Fifties|content=[[Furious Fifties]]}}
{{defn|Strong westerly winds found in the [[Southern Hemisphere]], generally between the latitudes of [[50th parallel south|50]] and [[60th parallel south|60]] degrees. They are stronger than the similar "[[Roaring Forties]]" to their north.}}

{{term|furl}}
{{defn|To roll or gather a {{nautical term|sail}} against its {{nautical term|mast}} or {{nautical term|spar}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|furnace}}
{{defn|{{gli|Boiler}} component where fuel is burned.}}

{{term|furring}}
{{defn|A method of improving the stability of a wooden vessel by increasing the breadth of the hull. The planking is removed and pieces of wood are added to the outside of the frames. Then the planking is replaced. An increase in breadth of about {{convert|1|ft|mm}} could typically be achieved on each side. This was a common remedial technique at a time before shipwrights were able to carry out mathematical stability calculations.<ref name="Adams 2013">{{cite book |last1=Adams |first1=Jonathan |title=A maritime archaeology of ships: innovation and social change in late medieval and early modern Europe |date=2013 |publisher=Oxbow Books |location=Oxford |isbn=9781782970453}}</ref>{{rp|at=ch 6 the Gresham Ship}}}}

{{term|term=fusta|content=[[fusta]] {{anchor|fuste|foist|galliot}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''fuste''', '''foist''', '''galiot''', or '''galliot'''.}}
A narrow, light, and fast ship with a shallow {{gli|draft}}, powered both by oars and sail, with a single mast carrying a {{gli|lateen}} sail; a favorite of North African {{gli|corsair|corsairs}} during the 16th and 17th centuries.}}

{{term|term=futtock shrouds|content=[[futtock shrouds]]}}
{{defn|Rope, wire, or chain links in the rigging of a traditional {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ship running from the outer edges of a {{nautical term|top}} downwards and inwards to a point on the {{nautical term|mast}} or lower {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}}. They carry the load of the shrouds that rise from the edge of the top, preventing the top from tilting relative to the mast.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|futtock}}
{{defn|The part of a ship's {{gli|frame}} that continues the structure above the {{gli|floor}}s. These often exist as individual pieces termed first futtock, second futtock and third futtock, numbered moving away from the keel.{{sfn|Steffy|1994|p=271}} See also ''{{gli|floating futtock}}''}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==G==
{{glossary}}

{{term|gabbart|content=[[gabbart]]}}
{{defn|The typical Scottish {{nautical term|sailing}} {{gli|barge}} or {{gli|lighter}} of the 17th through 19th centuries, used mainly on inland waterways and especially on the [[River Clyde]]. A gabbart was a long, narrow, flat, single-{{nautical term|mast}}ed vessel with a hatchway that extendied almost the full length of her {{gli|deck}}, and some gabbarts had a mast that could be lowered to allow them to pass under [[bridge]]s. Most later Scottish [[canal]] craft had their design origins in that of the gabbart.}}

{{term|gaff}}
{{defn|no=1|([[gaff rig]]) A {{nautical term|spar}} that holds the upper edge of a four-sided {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-mounted}} sail. On a hoisting gaff, the lower end is supported by gaff jaws which partly encircle the mast; it is hoisted using {{nautical term|peak}} and {{nautical term|throat}} {{gli|halliard|halliards}}. A standing gaff remains aloft, its sails brailed when not in use.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}
{{defn|no=2|([[fishing gaff]]) A hook on a long pole used to haul in fish.}}

{{term|term=gaff rig|content=[[gaff rig]] {{anchor|gaff rigged}}}}
{{defn|A boat rigged with a four-sided {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} sail set {{gli|abaft}} the mast, its head being spread by a {{gli|gaff}}. The gaff may be standing (permanently in position) with the sail being brailed up to the gaff when not in use, or, more commonly, is hoisted using two halliards: the peak and the throat.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|gaff topsail}}
{{defn|A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} sail set above a {{gli|gaff rig|gaff-rigged}} sail, with the {{gli|clew}} sheeted to the end of the  {{gli|gaff}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|gaff vang}}
{{defn|A {{gli|line}} rigged to the end of a {{gli|gaff}} and used to adjust a gaff sail's {{nautical term|trim}}.}}

{{term|gale}}
{{term|gali|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|ghali}}.}}}}

{{term|galiot|content=[[galiot]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''galliot'''.}}
1.&nbsp;Also ''foist'' and ''fuste'', a narrow, light, and fast ship with a shallow {{gli|draft}}, powered both by {{gli|oar}}s and {{gli|sail}}, with a single {{gli|mast}} carrying a {{gli|lateen sail}}; a favorite of [[North Africa]]n {{gli|corsair}}s during the 16th and 17th centuries.}}
{{defn|no=2|A type of Dutch or German merchant ship of the 17th through 19th centuries, similar to a {{gli|ketch}}, with a rounded {{gli|bow}} and {{nautical term|stern}} like a {{gli|fluyt}} and a  nearly flat bottom, used especially for coastal navigation in the [[North Sea]] and [[Baltic Sea]]. Modernized after 1830 with a sharper bow similar to a {{nautical term|schooner}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|Also ''galiote'', a type of French warship of the 17th through 19th centuries with one or two {{nautical term|mast}}s, {{gli|lateen sail}}s, and a bank of {{nautical term|oar}}s. When built with only one mast, little more than a large ''{{gli|chaloupe}}'' or {{gli|launch}}. A ''galiote a bombes'' was a ''galiote'' armed with a [[Mortar (weapon)|mortar]] for use in bombarding the coast.}}
{{defn|no=4|Also ''galiote'', a horse-drawn {{gli|barge}} used on canals and rivers in France from the mid-17th century through the 19th century.}}
{{defn|no=5|Also ''galiote'' or ''scute'', a flat-bottomed boat with a simple sail used to transport wine in the [[Duchy of Anjou|Anjou]] region of France.}}

{{term|term=galleass|content=[[galleass]]}}
{{defn|no=1|An oared warship of the 16th century equipped with a {{gli|gun deck}}; larger and equipped with more sails than a {{gli|galley}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A flat-bottomed commercial sailing vessel of the [[North Sea]] and western [[Baltic Sea]].}}

{{term|term=galleon|content=[[galleon]]}}
{{defn|
[[File:15th century galleon.JPG|thumb|right|Illustration of a typical 16th century '''{{gli|galleon}}''']]
A large, multi-decked sailing ship with a prominent, squared-off, raised {{nautical term|stern}}, generally carrying three or more {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, typically {{gli|lateen}} {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} on the rear mast and {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} on the {{nautical term|mainmast}} and {{gli|foremast}}. Galleons were used primarily as armed cargo carriers and sometimes as {{nautical term|warship|warships}} by European states from the 16th to the 18th centuries.}}

{{term|galley}}
{{defn|no=1|([[galley (kitchen)]]) The compartment of a ship where food is cooked or prepared; a ship's kitchen.}}
{{defn|no=2|([[galley]]) A type of ship propelled by {{nautical term|oar|oars}}, used especially in the [[Mediterranean]] for warfare, piracy, and trade from the 8th century BC to the 16th century AD, with some in use until the early 19th century.}}
{{defn|no=3|A type of oared {{gli|gunboat}} built by the United States in the late 18th century, akin to a {{gli|brigantine}} but termed "galley" for administrative and funding purposes.}}

{{term|galliot}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|galiot}}.}}}}

{{term|term=gam|content=[[Gam (nautical term)|gam]]}}
{{defn|A meeting of two (or more) {{nautical term|whaler|whaling ships}} at sea. The ships each send out a boat to the other, and the two captains meet on one ship, while the two chief mates meet on the other.<ref>{{cite book |last=Melville|first=Herman|title=Moby-Dick; or, The Whale|publisher=Harper and Brothers|year=1851|chapter=53|chapter-url=http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Moby-Dick/Chapter_53}}</ref>}}

{{term|gammon iron}}
{{defn|The {{gli|bow}} fitting that clamps the {{gli|bowsprit}} to the {{nautical term|stem}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|gangplank}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''brow'''.}}
A movable bridge used in boarding or leaving a ship at a pier.}}

{{term|gangway}}
{{defn|An opening in the {{gli|bulwark}} of a ship to allow passengers to board or leave the ship.}}

{{term|gantline}}
{{defn|A rope running through a {{gli|block}} at or near the {{nautical term|masthead}}, with both ends reaching the {{gli|deck}}. It is used solely for hoisting and lowering crew members and/or tools into the {{nautical term|rigging}} for maintenance and repair work.}}

{{term|garbling}}
{{defn|The illegal practice of mixing cargo with garbage.}}

{{term|garboard}}
{{defn|The {{nautical term|strake}} closest to the {{gli|keel}} (from Dutch ''gaarboard'').}}

{{term|garboard planks}}
{{defn|The planks immediately on either side of the {{gli|keel}}.}}

{{term|garland}}
{{defn|A frame of wood or rope for holding shot, usually attached to a {{gli|coaming}} or ledge on a ship's deck.}}

{{term|gash}}
{{defn|Any refuse or rubbish discarded into a refuse container or dustbin, also known as "gash fanny" (South African Navy).}}

{{term|gasket|content=[[gasket (sailing)]]}}
{{defn|A rope used to secure a sail (particularly the {{nautical term|topsail}}) when stowed.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|gate ship}}
{{defn|An alternative term for a {{nautical term|net-laying ship}}.}}

{{term|gear}}
{{defn|A collective term for a vessel's {{nautical term|sail|sails}} and {{nautical term|rigging}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|geedunk}}
{{defn|Ice cream, snacks, etc. Also the place selling such items.<ref name="NHHC">{{cite web|website=Naval History and Heritage Command|url=https://www.history.navy.mil/research/library/online-reading-room/title-list-alphabetically/o/origin-navy-terminology.html#|title=Origin of Navy Terminology|access-date=March 22, 2022}}</ref>}}

{{term|general quarters}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|battle stations}}.}}}}

{{term|term=gennaker|content=[[gennaker]]}}
{{defn|A large, lightweight sail used for sailing a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig}} down or across the wind, intermediate between a {{gli|genoa}} and a {{nautical term|spinnaker}}.}}

{{term|term=Genoa (sail)|genoa|content=[[Genoa (sail)|genoa]] {{anchor|genny}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''genny'''. (both {{IPAc-en|ˈ|dʒ|ɛ|n|i}})}}
A large {{gli|jib}}, strongly overlapping the {{nautical term|mainmast}}.}}

{{term|term=ghali|content=[[Ghali (ship)|ghali]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''gali''' or '''gale'''.}}
Any of several types of {{gli|galley}}-like ships from the [[Nusantara (archipelago)|Nusantara]] archipelago in Southeast Asia. The term refers both to Mediterranean vessels built by local people and to native vessels with Mediterranean influence.}}

{{term|ghost}}
{{defn|To sail slowly when there is apparently no wind.}}

{{term|ghost fleet}}
{{defn|In the modern United States, an informal term for a {{nautical term|reserve fleet}}.}}

{{term|gibe}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|gybe}}.}}}}

{{term|term=gig|content=[[Gig (boat)|gig]]}}
{{defn|A type of open boat designed primarily for propulsion under oar, but often fitted with a sailing rig for appropriate conditions. Used most often for the swift transport of one or a few people, as in a pilot gig or as a naval [[ship's boat]]. In US Navy usage, a captain's gig is reserved for use by a ship's captain and, in modern times, is a power-boat.}}

{{term|term=gig boat}}
{{defn|An alternative term for a {{gli|flyboat}}, a high-speed {{gli|canal boat}} operating in England in the 1830s and 1840s.}}

{{term|term=gillnetter|content=[[gillnetter]]}}
{{defn|A fishing vessel that employs [[gillnetting]] as its means of catching fish.}}

{{term|gin-pole}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''jin-pole'''.}}
A pole that is attached perpendicular to a {{nautical term|mast}}, to be used as a lever for raising the mast.}}

{{term|girt}}
{{defn|no=1|Said of a vessel moored by cables to two {{gli|anchor|anchors}} in such a way that the force of a current or tide causes her to swing against one of the cables.}}
{{defn|no=2|To {{gli|capsize}} because of forces exerted on a cable by another vessel attached to it. ''Tug girting'' specifically refers to girting that causes a {{nautical term|tugboat}} to capsize because of forces placed on a cable attached to her by another vessel attached to the same cable.}}

{{term|give-way}}
{{defn|In a situation where two vessels are approaching one another so as to involve a risk of collision, the vessel directed to keep out of the way of the other.}}

{{term|glass}}
{{defn|no=1|A marine [[barometer]]. Older barometers used mercury-filled glass tubes to measure and indicate barometric pressure.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|marine sandglass}}.}}

{{term|term=Global Positioning System|content=[[Global Positioning System]] (GPS){{anchor|Global Positioning System|GPS}}}}
{{defn|A satellite-based radionavigation system providing continuous worldwide coverage of geolocation and time information to air, marine, and land users wherever there is an unobstructed line of sight to at least four GPS satellites developed and operated by the United States Department of Defense but publicly available for use by anyone with an enabled GPS receiver.}}

{{term|term=go-fast boat|content=[[go-fast boat]]}}
{{defn|A small, fast boat designed with a long narrow platform and a planing {{gli|hull}} to enable it to reach high speeds. Colloquially equivalent to a "rum-runner" or a "cigarette boat".}}

{{term|term=goat locker|content=[[goat locker]]}}
{{defn|A mess hall reserved for [[chief petty officer]]s in the United States Navy.}}

{{term|going about}}
{{defn|Changing from one {{nautical term|tack}} to another by going through the wind. See also ''{{gli|gybe}}''.}}

{{term|term=gondola|content=[[gondola]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A traditional, flat-bottomed Venetian rowing boat.}}
{{defn|no=2|An alternative term for a {{gli|gundalow}}.}}

{{term|gooseneck}}
{{defn|A fitting that attaches a {{gli|boom}} to a {{nautical term|mast}} yet allows it to move freely.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|term=goosewinged|content=[[goosewing]]ed}}
{{defn|(of a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} vessel) Sailing directly away from the wind, with the sails set on opposite sides of the vessel (e.g. with the {{nautical term|mainsail}} to port and the {{gli|jib}} to starboard) so as to maximize the amount of canvas exposed to the wind. See also ''{{nautical term|running}}''.}}

{{term|term=Gozo boat|content=[[Gozo boat]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{nautical term|settee}}-rigged sailing vessel used as the main means of transportation across the [[Gozo Channel]] between [[Gozo]] and the [[Malta (island)|island of Malta]] in [[Malta]] beginning in the 1880s. Gozo boats were converted into fishing vessels or abandoned during the 1960s and 1970s, when {{nautical term|steamship}}s replaced them.}}

{{term|GPS|noid=true}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|Global Positioning System}}.}}}}

{{term|term=grapeshot|content=[[grapeshot]]}}
{{defn|Small balls of lead fired from a [[cannon]], analogous to [[shotgun]] shot but on a larger scale; similar to {{gli|canister shot}} but with larger individual shot. Intended specifically to injure personnel and damage {{nautical term|rigging}} more than to cause structural damage.}}

{{term|grave}}
{{defn|To clean a ship's bottom.}}

{{term|term=graving dock|content=[[graving dock]]}}
{{defn|A narrow basin, usually made of earthen berms and concrete, closed by gates or by a [[Caisson (engineering)|caisson]], into which a vessel may be floated and the water pumped out, leaving the vessel supported on blocks; the classic form of {{gli|drydock}}.}}

{{term|graybeard}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|Cape Horn roller}}.}}}}

{{term|term=great-circle navigation|content=[[great-circle navigation]]}}
{{defn|The practice of navigating a vessel along the arc of a [[great circle]]. Such routes yield the shortest possible distance between any given pair of points on the surface of the Earth.}}

{{term|green-to-green}}
{{defn|A passage of two vessels moving in the opposite direction on their {{nautical term|starboard}} sides, so called because the green navigation light on one of the vessels faces the green light on the other vessel.}}

{{term|term=Greenlandman|content=[[Greenlandman]]}}
{{defn|A British term used in the 18th and 19th centuries for any {{nautical term|whaling ship}} operating in the [[Arctic Ocean]] or northern waters near the Arctic.}}

{{term|green water}}
{{defn|no=1|That portion of the ocean lying generally within a few hundred {{nautical term|nautical mile}}s of shore but beyond the edge of the [[continental shelf]], and thus between "{{gli|brown water}}" over the continental shelf and "{{gli|blue water}}" farther out to sea.}}
{{defn|no=2|A large amount of water on or passing over or across a ship's {{gli|deck}} or {{nautical term|superstructure}} after a large wave strikes her, e.g., ''The ship took green water over her {{gli|bow}} during the storm''.}}

{{term|green-water navy}}
{{defn|A navy capable of sustained operations beyond coastal areas out to a few hundred {{nautical term|nautical mile}}s from shore, i.e., in "{{gli|green water}}," but not farther into the open ocean, i.e., in "{{gli|blue water}}." While a green-water navy can possess ships capable of operating farther out to sea than in "green water," it requires logistical support from foreign countries to sustain such longer-range operations.}}

{{term|gridiron}}
{{defn|A large metal cross-frame on which vessels are placed at high water for examination, cleaning, and repairs after the tide falls.}}

{{term|gripe}}
{{defn|A temporary eye in a {{gli|line}} (rope).}}

{{term|griping}}
{{defn|The tendency of a ship to turn into the wind despite the efforts of the {{gli|helmsman}}, usually due to either the design of a ship or more commonly the incorrect distribution of weight on and within the hull.}}

{{term|gripie}}
{{defn|A Cockney (London dialect) name for a {{gli|barge}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=grog|content=[[grog]]}}
{{defn|Watered-down pusser's [[rum]] consisting of half a gill with an equal part of water, issued to all seamen over twenty (CPOs and POs were issued with neat rum). From the British [[Edward Vernon|Admiral Vernon]] who, in 1740, ordered the men's [[Rum Ration|ration of rum]] to be watered down. He was called "Old Grogram" because he often wore a [[glossary of textile manufacturing|grogram]] coat, and the watered rum came to be called grog. Specific quantities of grog were often traded illegally as a form of currency; a sailor might repay a colleague for a favour by giving him part or all of his grog ration, ranging from "sippers" (a small amount) via "gulpers" (a larger quantity) to "grounders" (the entire tot). Additional issues of grog were made on the command "{{nautical term|splice the mainbrace}}" for celebrations or as a reward for performing especially onerous duties. The Royal Navy discontinued the practice of issuing rum in 1970.}}

{{term|groggy}}
{{defn|Drunk from having consumed a lot of {{gli|grog}}.}}

{{term|term=grommet|content=[[grommet]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A metal or plastic ring inserted in canvas to prevent wear.}}
{{defn|no=2|A ring of rope.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}
{{defn|no=3|An [[grommet (sportsperson)|inexperienced surfer]] or extreme sports participant.}}

{{term|ground}}
{{defn|The bed of the sea; the underwater surface or sea floor to which an {{gli|anchor}} holds.}}

{{term|grounding}}
{{defn|When a ship (while afloat) touches the {{gli|ground|bed of the sea}}, or runs {{gli|aground}}. A moored vessel that grounds as the tide goes out is said to "take the ground".}}

{{term|groundway}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''ground way'''.}}
A substantial foundation of wood or stone for the blocks on which a vessel is built, typically lying on either side of the {{gli|keel}} of a ship under construction, which also serves to support and guide the blocks when they slide to carry the vessel into the water when she is launched.}}

{{term|growler}}
{{defn|A small [[iceberg]] or ice floe barely visible above the surface of the water.}}

{{term|Guineaman}}
{{defn|Another name for a {{nautical term|slave ship}}, coined after the emergence of the transatlantic slave trade from Africa in the 15th century.}}

{{term|guards}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''paddle guards''' and '''wheel guards'''.}}
1.&nbsp;(on an oceangoing sidewheel {{nautical term|steamship}}) Horizontal structures, usually of wood, built around the {{nautical term|paddle box|paddle boxes}} just above their lowest point and extending a short distance {{gli|forward}} and {{gli|aft}}, designed to protect them from damage and to provide additional support for the paddle shaft.<ref name=ridgely-nevitt1981_p371/>}}
{{defn|no=2|(on an American sidewheel steamboat) Extensions of the [[main deck]] beyond the hull to the outer extremity of the paddle boxes, and tapering to the {{gli|bow}} and {{nautical term|stern}} (thus giving the deck a characteristic oval shape), to increase the available deck space for passengers, cargo, and/or machinery.<ref name=ridgely-nevitt1981_p371>Ridgely-Nevitt 1981. p. 371.</ref>}}

{{term|term=guard ship|content=[[guard ship]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Any vessel that makes the rounds of a {{gli|fleet}} at anchor to see that due watch is kept at night.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|warship}} stationed at a port or harbour to act as a guard there.}}
{{defn|no=3|In former times in the British Royal Navy, a ship that received men impressed for naval service, often the {{gli|flagship}} of the {{gli|admiral}} commanding along the coast.}}
{{defn|no=4|In Soviet and Russian terminology, a guard ship (''storozhevoj korabl{{'}}'') is a small, general-purpose patrol or escort vessel.}}

{{term|term=gun deck|content=[[gun deck]]}}
{{defn|no=1|Up through the 19th century, a {{gli|deck}} aboard a ship that was primarily used for the mounting of {{gli|cannon}} to be fired in {{gli|broadside|broadsides}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|On smaller vessels (of {{gli|frigate}} size or smaller) up through the 19th century, the completely covered level under the upper deck, though in such smaller ships it carried none of the ship's guns.}}
{{defn|no=3|On marine seismic survey vessels, the lowest deck on the ship, which carries the seismic source arrays, consisting of air guns arranged in clusters.}}
{{defn|no=4|In naval slang, to fabricate or falsify something; in modern usage, meaning especially to falsify documentation in order to avoid doing work or make present conditions seem acceptable without having made a real effort to improve them.}}

{{term|gundecking}}
{{defn|Falsifying of records and reports.<ref name="NHHC"/>}}

{{term|term=gundalow|content=[[gundalow]]}}
{{defn|A type of flat-bottomed sailing {{gli|barge}} with a single large {{gli|lateen sail}} {{gli|brail|brailed}} to a heavy {{nautical term|yard}}, used on rivers in [[Maine]] and [[New Hampshire]] from the mid-17th century to the early 20th century. Sometimes referred to as a ''{{gli|gondola}}'' in period accounts.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.gundalow.org/about-us/what-is-a-gundalow/|title=The Gundalow Company : About Us : Gundalows &#124; Gundalow Company|date=11 November 2009}}</ref>}}

{{term|gunner's daughter}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|kissing the gunner's daughter}}.}}}}

{{term|gunport}}
{{defn|An opening in the side of a ship or in a turret through which a gun fires or protrudes.}}

{{term|gunroom}}
{{defn|In a sailing vessel, the compartment in the stern in which {{nautical term|chase gun|stern chasers}} were mounted. This is also where the tiller enters through a tiller port, and so either the lower end of the [[whipstaff]] or the steering ropes that run to the wheel are present. In later ships, a mess in a warship used by sub-lieutenants, midshipmen and cadets.{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=101}}}}

{{term|term=gunter rig|content=[[gunter rig]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''sliding gunter''' or '''gunter lug'''.}}
A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} sail set {{gli|abaft}} (behind) the mast, approximately triangular in shape, with the top half of the {{gli|luff}} (front) of the sail attached to a {{nautical term|yard}} which extends the sail above the top of the mast. The yard is raised and lowered with the sail.<ref name="Read 2014g">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014g |c= G |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/g-16180}}</ref> This traditional sail is popular in small boats and produces aerodynamic performance close to that of the highly developed {{gli|Bermuda rig}}.<ref name="Nichols 2014">{{cite book |last1=Nichols |first1=David L |title=The Working Guide to Traditional Small-Boat Sails: A How-To Handbook for Owners and Builders |publisher=Breakaway Books |location=Halcotsville NY |isbn=978-1-62124-017-4 |year=2014}}</ref>}}

{{term|term=gunwale|content=[[gunwale]] {{anchor|gunnel}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Rarely '''gunnel'''. (both {{IPAc-en|ˈ|ɡ|ʌ|n|əl}})}}
Generally, the upper edge of the {{gli|hull}}; more specifically, in an open (undecked) boat of timber construction, the longitudinal stringer that connects the top of the ribs.<ref name="Read 2014g"/>{{sfn|Palmer|1975|p=101}}}}

{{term|gurdy}}
{{defn|A mechanical crank used to set and retrieve fishing lines.}}

{{term|guy}}
{{defn|no=1|A rope or {{nautical term|stay}} leading to the side of the vessel.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}
{{defn|no=2|A rope used to steady a {{gli|boom}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=109}}}}

{{term|term=gybe|content=[[Jibe (sailing)|gybe]] {{anchor|jibe}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''jibe'''. (both {{IPAc-en|dʒ|aɪ|b}})}}
To change from one {{nautical term|tack}} to the other away from the wind, with the {{nautical term|stern}} of the vessel turning through the wind. See also ''{{gli|going about}}'' and ''{{nautical term|wearing ship}}''.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|gypsy winch}}
{{defn|A type or component of an anchor winch. The "gypsy" or "gypsy wheel" engages the anchor chain.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==H==
{{glossary}}
{{term|half-breadth plan}}
{{defn|In [[shipbuilding]], an [[elevation (view)|elevation]] of the lines of a ship, viewed from above and divided lengthwise.}}

{{term|term=halyard|content=[[halyard]] {{anchor|halliard}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''halliard'''.}}
Originally, ropes used for hoisting a {{nautical term|spar}} with a {{nautical term|sail}} attached; today, a {{gli|line}} used to raise the head of any sail.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=hammock|content=[[hammock]]}}
{{defn|Canvas sheets, slung from the {{gli|deckhead}} in {{nautical term|messdecks}}, in which seamen slept. "Lash up and stow" was a piped command to tie up hammocks and stow them (typically) in racks inboard of the ship's side so as to protect the crew from splinters from shot and provide a ready means of preventing flooding caused by damage.}}

{{term|hamper}}
{{defn|Articles that normally are indispensable aboard ship but at certain times are in the way.}}

{{term|hand}}
{{defn|To lower or furl a {{nautical term|sail}}.<ref name="Read 2014h">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014h |c= H |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/h-16178}}</ref> }}

{{term|hand bomber}}
{{defn|A ship using coal-fired boilers shoveled in by hand.}}

{{term|hand over fist}}
{{defn|To climb steadily upwards, from the motion of a sailor climbing {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}} on a sailing ship (originally "hand over hand").}}

{{term|handsomely}}
{{defn|With a slow even motion, as when hauling on a {{gli|line}} "handsomely".}}

{{term|handy billy}}
{{defn|A loose block and tackle with a hook or tail on each end, which can be used wherever it is needed. Usually made up of one single and one double block.}}

{{term|hangar deck}}
{{defn|An enclosed {{gli|deck}} on an {{gli|aircraft carrier}}, usually beneath the {{gli|flight deck}} and intended for use as a [[hangar]] in servicing and storing aircraft.}}

{{term|hank}}
{{defn|A fastener attached to the {{gli|luff}} of the {{gli|headsail}} that attaches the headsail to the {{gli|forestay}}. Typical designs include a bronze or plastic hook with a spring-operated gate, or a strip of cloth webbing with a snap fastener.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}

{{term|term=harbor of refuge|content={{lang|en-US|harbor of refuge}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=harbour of refuge|content={{lang|en-GB|harbour of refuge}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|An artificial {{gli|harbour}} constructed on a coast without a {{nautical term|natural harbour}} to provide shelter for small vessels.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=harbor|content={{lang|en-US|[[harbor]]}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=harbour|content={{lang|en-GB|harbour}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''haven'''.}}
A place where ships or smaller craft may shelter from the weather, are unloaded/loaded, or stored. Harbours can be man-made or natural.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=harbor dues|content={{lang|en-US|harbor dues}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=harbour dues|content={{lang|en-GB|harbour dues}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|The fees charged by the owners or operators of a harbour to those vessels using the harbour. Under British legislation, the person in charge of a vessel must report to the {{gli|harbourmaster}} within 24 hours of arrival in a port where harbour dues are payable.<ref name="Read 2014h"/>{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=harbormaster|content={{lang|en-US|harbormaster}} {{small|[[American English]]}}}}
{{term|term=harbourmaster|content={{lang|en-GB|[[harbourmaster]]}} {{small|[[British English]]}}|multi=y}}
{{defn|A person in charge of a {{gli|harbour}}, with powers including the collection of the {{gli|harbour dues}}, instructing the masters of vessels where to moor, and overall safety within the area of the harbour, often including pilotage and navigational aids. In most countries the powers of a harbour master are laid down by legislation, and can be quite extensive.<ref name="Read 2014h"/>{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|hard}}
{{defn|A section of otherwise muddy shoreline suitable for mooring or hauling out.}}

{{term|hard-a-lee}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|lee-oh}}.}}}}

{{term|harden in}}
{{defn|To haul in the sheet and tighten the sails.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|harden up}}
{{defn|To turn towards the wind; to sail closer to the wind.}}

{{term|harness cask}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''harness tub'''.}}
A large, usually round tub lashed to a vessel's deck and containing dried and salted provisions for daily use.}}

{{term|term=hardtack|content=[[hardtack]]}}
{{defn|A hard and long-lasting dry biscuit, used as food on long journeys. Also called a "ship's biscuit".}}

{{term|hatch}}
{{term|hatchway|content=[[hatchway]]|multi=y}}
{{defn|A covered opening in a ship's {{gli|deck}} through which cargo can be loaded or access made to a lower deck; the cover to the opening is called a ''hatch''.}}

{{term|haul}}
{{defn|no=1|To steer (a vessel) closer to the direction of the wind.}}
{{defn|no=2|To shift forward, i.e. more toward the {{gli|bow}} of the vessel.}}

{{term|hauling wind}}
{{defn|Pointing the ship towards the direction of the wind; generally not the fastest point of travel on a sailing vessel.}}

{{term|term=hawsepipe|content=[[hawsepipe]]{{anchor|hawsehole|hawse}} }}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''hawsehole''' or '''hawse'''.}}
The shaft or hole in the side of a vessel's {{gli|bow}} through which the {{gli|anchor chain}} passes. "In through the hawsepipe" describes someone with experience and savvy.<ref name="NHHC"/>}}

{{term|term=hawsepiper|content=[[hawsepiper]]}}
{{defn|An informal term for an officer of a {{nautical term|merchant ship}} who began their career as an unlicensed merchant seaman, and so did not attend a traditional maritime academy to earn their officer's licence. See also ''{{gli|before the mast}}''.}}

{{term|term=hawser|content=[[hawser]]}}
{{defn|A large cable or rope used for mooring or towing a vessel.}}

{{term|term=head|content=[[Head (watercraft)|head]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The forwardmost or uppermost portion of the ship.}}
{{defn|no=2|The forwardmost or uppermost portion of any individual part of the ship, e.g. {{nautical term|masthead}}, {{gli|beakhead}}, {{nautical term|stemhead}}, etc.}}
{{defn|no=3|The top edge of a sail.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=4|The [[toilet]] or [[latrine]] of a vessel, which in sailing ships usually projected from the {{gli|bow}} and therefore was located in the "head" of the vessel.}}

{{term|head boat}}
{{defn|A fishing boat that takes recreational fishermen out for a fee paid individually by each person (i.e. per head). A head boat differs from a {{gli|charter boat}}, which is a fishing boat that a party of fishermen hires for an agreed-upon period.}}
{{term|head of navigation |content= [[Head of navigation]]}}{{defn|The farthest point above the mouth of a river that can be navigated by ships.}}

{{term|head rail}}
{{defn|A curved rail that extends from the {{gli|figurehead}} to the {{gli|bow}} of a ship.}}

{{term|head rope}}
{{defn|no=1|The mooring rope that goes from the bow of a vessel to a point on a jetty a distance ahead of the bows.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=2|Part of the {{gli|bolt rope}}, at the head of a {{nautical term|sail}}, running from the {{nautical term|mast}} to the {{nautical term|sprit}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|head sea}}
{{defn|A sea in which waves are directly opposing the motion of the ship, or approaching within 15° of ahead.<ref name="Saunders V3 1965" />}}

{{term|head-sail}}
{{defn|no=1|Jibs and staysails set between the {{gli|bowsprit}} and the fore{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}
{{defn|no=2|Sometimes refers to the square sails on the fore-mast of a square rigged vessel.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}

{{term|head-stays}}
{{defn|Stays between the {{gli|bowsprit}} and the foremost mast.
{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}

{{term|header}}
{{defn|A change in the wind direction that forces the {{gli|helmsman}} of a {{gli|close-hauled}} sailboat to steer away from its current course to a less favorable one. This is the opposite of a {{gli|lift}}.}}

{{term|term=heading|content=[[Course (navigation)#Course, track, route and heading|heading]]}}
{{defn|The direction in which the nose of a vessel is pointing (which is not necessarily the same as the direction in which the vessel is actually moving).}}

{{term|term=headsail|content=[[headsail]]}}
{{defn|Any {{nautical term|sail}} set in front of the most forward {{nautical term|mast}}. A sailing vessel may have one or more headsails. A headsail may be {{gli|hank}}ed to a stay, or may be set flying, with the {{gli|luff}} being kept taut by the tension of the halyard. Where several headsails are set, a complex arrangement might be termed (from the front and top) flying jib, outer jib, inner jib, and (fore) staysail; less complex would be {{gli|jib}} and {{nautical term|staysail}}{{sfn|Mayne|2000|p=142}}}}

{{term|headstick}}
{{defn|The {{nautical term|spar}} laced to the head of the {{nautical term|topsail}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|heave}}
{{defn|A vessel's transient, vertical, up-and-down motion.}}

{{term|heave down}}
{{defn|To turn a ship on its side (for cleaning), a process which is also known as ''{{gli|careening}}''.}}

{{term|heave ho!}}
{{defn|An exclamation sailors make when pulling forcefully on a rope.}}

{{term|term=heave to|content=[[heave to]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|hove to}}.}}}}

{{term|heavy weather}}
{{defn|A combination of high winds and rough seas that may be dangerous for a ship or boat, sometimes requiring changes to a passage plan (such as a precautionary diversion to a safe harbour), {{gli|heaving to}}, running under bare poles, or other similar survival strategies.}}

{{term|term=heel|content=[[Heeling (sailing)|heel]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The lean caused by the wind's force on the {{nautical term|sail|sails}} of a sailing vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|The inclination or canting of a vessel to one side or the other from the vertical as she maneuvers, e.g. "The ship heeled to port as she turned to starboard".}}
{{defn|no=3|The lowest or last part of something, such as the ''heel of the mast'' or the ''heel of the vessel''.}}

{{term|helicopter deck}}
{{defn|A [[Helipad|helicopter pad]] on the {{gli|deck}} of a {{nautical term|ship}}. In the United States Navy, a helicopter deck is referred to as a ''{{gli|flight deck}}''.}}

{{term|helm}}
{{defn|no=1|A ship's steering mechanism, such as a {{nautical term|tiller}} or {{nautical term|ship's wheel}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|The wheel and/or {{nautical term|wheelhouse}} area.}}
{{defn|no=3|(v.) To take over the steering of a vessel.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=helmsman|content=[[helmsman]] {{anchor|steersman}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''steersman'''.}}
A member of the {{gli|crew}} who is responsible for steering the ship.}}

{{term|term=herring buss|content=[[herring buss]]}}
{{defn|A type of seagoing fishing vessel used by Dutch and Flemish herring fishermen from the 15th through the early 19th century.}}

{{term|highfield lever}}
{{defn|A type of tensioning lever, usually for running {{gli|backstay|backstays}}. Their use allows the {{gli|leeward}} backstay to be completely slackened so that the {{gli|boom}} can be let fully out.}}

{{term|term=hitch|content=[[List of hitch knots|hitch]]}}
{{defn|A knot used to tie a rope or {{gli|line}} to a fixed object. See also ''{{gli|bend}}''.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|hobby horsing}}
{{defn|Harmonic [[Ship motions|pitching]] of a vessel forward and backward.}}

{{term|hog}}
{{defn|no=1|A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} structural member of the hull fitted over the keel to provide a fixing for the {{gli|garboard}} planks.}}
{{defn|no=2|A rough, flat scrubbing brush for cleaning a ship's bottom under water.}}
{{defn|no=3|A semi-permanent bend in a ship's keel, especially in wooden-hulled ships, caused over time by the ship's center being more buoyant than her {{gli|bow}} or {{nautical term|stern}}.}}

{{term|hog frame}}
{{defn|A heavy wooden [[truss]] fitted lengthwise along each side of a large American steamboat, secured to the {{gli|hull}} and rising above deck just outside the {{nautical term|superstructure}}, to provide support for the hull and prevent {{gli|hogging}}. Similar in appearance and function to a [[truss bridge]]. See also ''{{gli|king post}}''.}}

{{term|term=Hog Islander|content=[[Hog Islander]]}}
{{defn|Slang term used for [[Design 1022 ship|Design 1022]] {{gli|cargo ship|cargo ships}} and [[Design 1024 ship|Design 1024]] {{nautical term|troop transport|troop transports}} constructed at [[Hog Island, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania|Hog Island]] in [[Philadelphia]], [[Pennsylvania]], to address {{nautical term|merchant marine}} shortfalls in the United States during {{nowrap|[[World War I]]}}. Completed too late for {{nowrap|World War I,}} Hog Islanders saw [[United States Navy]] and [[United States Merchant Marine]] service prior to and during {{nowrap|[[World War II]].}}}}

{{term|term=hogging|content=[[Hogging and sagging|hogging]]}}
{{defn|no=1|A condition in which the {{gli|hull}} of a vessel bends upward such that the ends of the {{gli|keel}} are lower than the middle. Hogging can occur when the peak of a wave is {{gli|amidships}} or during loading or unloading of a vessel and can damage her or even break her in half. Contrast ''{{nautical term|sagging}}''.}}
{{defn|no=2|A permanent distortion of the hull in the same manner as above, caused over time by the bow and stern of a ship being less buoyant than the midships section. During the [[Age of Sail]], shipwrights employed a number of different designs of braces to stiffen ships' hulls against this warping.}}

{{term|hogging line}}
{{defn|A line passed under a ship from side to side to pull a collision mat into place over a leak.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/hogging%20line#:~:text=%3A%20a%20line%20or%20chain%20used,area%20of%20a%20ship's%20hull |title=Hogging line |website=merriam-webster.com |access-date=10 August 2020}}</ref> Also a line passed under a ship from side to side used as a reference to indicate position of a frame during underwater inspections.<ref>{{cite book|url=https://navytribe.files.wordpress.com/2015/11/navsea-s0600-aa-pro-170.pdf |title=S0600-AA-PRO-170 Underwater ship husbandry manual |chapter=17: Inspection procedures |publisher= Naval Sea Systems Command |edition=Change B |date=15 May 2003 }}</ref>}}

{{term|hoist}}
{{defn|The height of a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} sail as measured next to the {{nautical term|mast}} or {{nautical term|stay}}.}}

{{term|term=hold|content=[[Hold (ship)|hold]]}}
{{defn|The lower part of the interior of a ship's {{gli|hull}}, especially when considered as storage space, as for cargo. In earlier use, the term referred to all interior spaces below the {{nautical term|orlop deck}}; in later merchant vessels it extended up through the decks to the underside of the {{nautical term|weather deck}}.}}

{{term|holiday}}
{{defn|A gap in the coverage of newly applied paint, slush, tar, or another preservative.}}

{{term|term=holystone|content=[[holystone]]}}
{{defn|A chunk of [[sandstone]] used to scrub a ship's {{gli|deck|decks}}. The name comes from both the kneeling position sailors adopt to scrub the deck (reminiscent of genuflection for prayer), and the stone itself (which resembled a Bible in shape and size).}}

{{term|term=home port|content=[[home port]]}}
{{defn|The {{nautical term|port}} at which a vessel is based. Often confused with the ship's {{nautical term|port of registry}}, which is the port listed in the vessel's registration documents and lettered on her {{nautical term|stern}} but which may differ from her home port. In the {{gli|cruise ship}} industry, the term "home port" is often incorrectly used to refer to a ship's port of departure.}}

{{term|homeward bounder}}
{{defn|A slang term for a sail repair, especially one done with large herringbone stitches.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|honey barge}}
{{defn|Slang term for a vessel that transports sewage.}}

{{term|hoop}}
{{defn|Wooden or metal hoops used to secure the {{nautical term|topsail}} to the [[topmast]] so it can be speedily raised or lowered.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|horn}}
{{defn|A sound signal that uses electricity or compressed air to vibrate a disc diaphragm.}}

{{term|term=hornpipe|content=[[hornpipe]]}}
{{defn|A type of dance.}}

{{term|horns}}
{{defn|Shaped ends to the {{gli|chock|chocks}} where the main {{gli|horse}} is bolted.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|horn timber}}
{{defn|A {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} structural member of the hull sloping up and backwards from the {{gli|keel}} to support the {{gli|counter}}.}}

{{term|horse}}
{{defn|no=1|A metal bar (sometimes a shaped aluminium extrusion), running {{gli|athwartships}}, to which a {{nautical term|sheet}} is attached with a [[Traveller (nautical fitting)|traveller]] that slides along the horse or is adjusted to be fixed in one position on it. Commonly used for a {{nautical term|mainsheet}}, but also seen with some {{gli|headsail|headsails}}, particularly a {{nautical term|staysail}} fitted with a {{gli|boom}}.<ref name="Read 2014h"/> }}
{{defn|no=2|Sand lying mid-channel.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=3|(verb) To move or adjust a sail by manual force (i.e. directly with the hands) rather than by using running {{nautical term|rigging}}.}}
{{defn|no=4|(verb) A term used since the end of the 17th century for the action of a strong, favorable current on a sailing vessel allowing her to make good progress despite insufficient wind for sailing; the vessel is considered to be ''horsed'' by the current, riding it in the way a human rides a horse.}}

{{term|term=horse latitudes|content=[[horse latitudes]]}}
{{defn|The [[latitude]]s between [[30th parallel north|30]] and [[35th parallel north|35]] degrees in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and between [[30th parallel south|30]] and [[35th parallel south|35]] degrees in the [[Southern Hemisphere]] in which weather patterns often result in sailing vessels being becalmed in mid-ocean.}}

{{term|term=hospital ship|content=[[hospital ship]]}}
{{defn|A ship designated and equipped to serve primarily as a floating medical healthcare facility or hospital, usually operated by military forces such as navies for use in or near war zones, or for the support of disaster relief and other humanitarian operations.}}

{{term|hounds}}
{{defn|Attachments point of {{nautical term|stay|stays}} to {{nautical term|mast|masts}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|hotel load}}
{{defn|The [[electrical load]] for all non-propulsion systems on a ship, including lighting, climate control, and services used by the crew and passengers.}}

{{term|term=hove to|content=[[hove to]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In a sailing vessel, stopping her by {{gli|back and fill|backing}} some of the sails and lashing the {{gli|helm}} to {{gli|leeward}}. In a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} {{nautical term|sloop}}, this involves backing the {{gli|headsail}} and allowing the {{nautical term|mainsail}} to fill somewhat (the precise arrangement varies from one vessel to another). The vessel will gradually drift to leeward, with the speed of the drift depending on the vessel's design.}}
{{defn|no=2|In a powered vessel, stopping her by stopping her engines.}}

{{term|hoveller}}
{{defn|no=1|Someone who does {{nautical term|salvage}} work, such as that done by [[Deal, Kent#Boatmen|Deal boatmen]].{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=96}}}}
{{defn|no=2|An additional crewman who assists getting a vessel in and out of harbour. See also ''{{gli|huffler}}'' (regional usage of these words varies substantially, with strongly held views on the differences).{{sfn|Stuckey|2000|p=97}}{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=96}}}}

{{term|how's your head?}}
{{defn|A question asked of the {{gli|helmsman}} to report the vessel's {{gli|course}} at that moment. The actual course may differ from the course to steer that has been ordered.}}

{{term|hoy}}
{{defn|no=1|A {{gli|cutter}}-rigged craft, having a pole masted with a boomless {{gli|gaff}} {{nautical term|mainsail}} and a steeved-up {{gli|bowsprit}}. Hoys were square, swim-headed Thames estuary barges of 40 to 150 tons burthen.{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=55}}}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|barge}} making regular passages on a fixed route with mixed third-party cargoes. Also ''passage barge'' or ''goods barge''.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=55}}}}

{{term|hufflers}}
{{defn|Additional crew taken on to enter harbour or navigate in confined waters, particularly applying to Thames barges. See also ''{{gli|hoveller}}''.{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=96}}}}

{{term|term=hulk}}
{{defn|no=1||[[Hulk (ship type)|Hulk]]: A ship, often an old ship or one that has become obsolete or uneconomical to operate, that has had its rigging or internal equipment removed and is incapable of going to sea, but that is still {{gli|afloat}} and continues to serve a useful function, such as providing living, office, training, storage, or prison space.}}
{{defn|no=2|(v.) To convert a ship into a hulk.}}
{{defn|no=3|A ship that has been [[ship naming and launching|launched]] but not completed.}}
{{defn|no=4|An abandoned {{nautical term|shipwreck|wreck}} or shell of a ship.}}
{{defn|no=5|[[Hulk (medieval ship type)|Hulk]], also '''holk''': A type of [[medieval ship]] of [[Europe]] which apparently originated as a river vessel in the [[Low Countries]] sometime prior to 1000. A hulk had a {{gli|forecastle}}, an {{gli|aftercastle}}, and a single {{nautical term|mast}} {{gli|amidships}} with a square {{nautical term|sail}}. Hulks apparently were employed as {{gli|cargo ship}}s by [[England]] and the [[Hanseatic League]], although some may have operated as {{nautical term|warship}}s. The {{gli|caravel}} replaced the hulk in the 15th century.}}

{{term|term=hull|content=[[Hull (watercraft)|hull]]}}
{{defn|The shell and framework of the basic flotation-oriented part of a ship.}}

{{term|term=hull speed|content=[[hull speed]]}}
{{defn|The maximum efficient speed of a displacement-hulled vessel.}}

{{term|term=hull-down|content=[[hull-down]]}}
{{defn|Of a vessel when only her upper parts (e.g. {{gli|funnel}}, {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, and {{nautical term|superstructure}}) are visible on the horizon but her {{gli|hull}} remains below the horizon. Contrast ''{{gli|hull-up}}''.}}

{{term|term=hull-up|content=[[Hull-down|hull-up]]}}
{{defn|Of a vessel when her {{gli|hull}} as well as her upper parts (e.g., {{gli|funnel}}, {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, and {{nautical term|superstructure}}) are visible on the horizon. Contrast ''{{gli|hull-down}}''.}}

{{term|term=hydrofoil|content=[[hydrofoil]]}}
{{defn|A boat with wing-like foils mounted on struts below the hull, lifting the hull entirely out of the water at speed and therefore greatly reducing water resistance.}}

{{term|term=hydroplane|content=[[Hydroplane (boat)|hydroplane]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|also '''hydro''' or '''thunderboat'''}}
A fast motorboat with a {{gli|hull}} shaped so that at speed {{nautical term|Plane|planing}} forces support the boat's weight, rather than simple {{gli|buoyancy}}. A hydroplane moving at speed thus relies on the water for [[Lift (force)|lift]] instead of buoyancy.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

* I
{{glossary}}
{{term|term=ice class|content=[[ice class]]}}
{{defn|A notation assigned by a {{nautical term|ship classification society}} or a national government authority to denote a ship's level of strengthening and other arrangements enabling her to navigate through sea ice. In some cases, an ice class also establishes the performance requirements for a vessel operating in sea ice.}}

{{term|term=icebreaker|content=[[icebreaker]]}}
{{defn|A special-purpose ship or boat designed to move and navigate through ice-covered waters.}}

{{term|term=icing|content=[[Icing (nautical)|icing]]}}
{{defn|A serious hazard where cold temperatures — below about {{convert|−10|C}} — combined with high wind speed (typically force 8 or above on the [[Beaufort scale]]) result in spray blown off the sea freezing immediately upon contact with the ship. If the weight of the ice becomes too great, the ship will become top-heavy and [[capsize]].}}

{{term|idlers}}
{{defn|Members of a {{nautical term|ship's company}} not required to serve watches. In general, these were specialist tradesmen such as the carpenter and the sailmaker.}}

{{term|in ballast}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''in ballast condition'''.}}
(of a vessel) Having only {{gli|ballast}}, and no cargo, as a load.}}

{{term|in irons}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''in [[Stays (nautical)|stays]]'''.}}
When a sailing vessel has lost all power in its sails along with all of its forward momentum preventing the vessel from steering. In high enough wind, backwards momentum can be reached from the wind pushing the vessel backwards. <ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=in ordinary|content=[[in ordinary]]}}
{{defn|An 18th- and 19th-century term originally used to refer to a naval vessel that is out of service for repair or maintenance, later coming to mean naval ships in reserve with no more than a caretaker crew.}}

{{term|term=in the way|content=[[in the way]]}}
{{defn|Humorous slang for when a crew's sole role on a sailboat, typically on a racing vessel, is to be used as a human ballast, or more commonly referred to as railmeat by sailors, by sitting on the rail or edge of the vessel to balance the weight. <ref>{{Cite web|url=https://gritcitymag.com/2019/06/if-youve-ever-wanted-to-try-sailing-this-is-your-chance/|title=If You’ve Ever Wanted to Try Sailing, This Is Your Chance|first=Sierra|last=Hartman|date=1 June 2019}}</ref>}}


{{term|term=in-water survey|content=[[in-water survey]]}}
{{defn|A method of surveying the underwater parts of a ship while it is still afloat instead of having to {{gli|drydock}} it for examination of these areas as was conventionally done.}}

{{term|in way of}}
{{defn|In the vicinity of; in the area of.}}

{{term|inboard}}
{{defn|no=1|Situated within a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=2|Situated within a vessel and positioned close (or closer relative to another object) to her {{gli|centerline}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|Situated outside a vessel but nearer to her {{gli|hull}}, e.g. "The larger boat was tied up alongside the ship inboard of the smaller boat."}}
{{defn|no=4|Nearer the pier or shore, e.g. "The tanker and cargo ship were tied up at the pier alongside one another with the tanker inboard of the cargo ship."}}

{{term|term=inboard motor|content=[[inboard motor]]}}
{{defn|An engine mounted within the {{gli|hull}} of a vessel, usually driving a fixed {{nautical term|propeller}} by a shaft protruding through the {{nautical term|stern}}. Generally used on larger vessels. See also ''{{nautical term|sterndrive}}'' and ''{{nautical term|outboard motor}}''.}}

{{term|inboard-outboard drive system}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{nautical term|sterndrive}}.}}}}

{{term|term=Inglefield clip|content=[[Inglefield clip]]}}
{{defn|A type of clip for attaching a flag to a flag {{gli|halyard}}.}}

{{term|inshore}}
{{defn|no=1|Near (especially in sight of) or toward the shore.}}
{{defn|no=2|(of a wind) Blowing from the sea to the land.}}

{{term|interloper}}
{{defn|A term used by the British [[East India Company]] in the seventeenth century for a merchant ship operating in violation of the company's monopoly over trade between England (later the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|United Kingdom]]) and ports east of the [[Cape of Good Hope]]. If caught, an "interloper" and her cargo could be confiscated, and her crew faced harsh penalties.<ref name=threedecksshiproles/>}}

{{term|invoke}}
{{defn|To signal another ship.}}

{{term|Iron Mike}}
{{defn|A slang term for autopilot.}}

{{term|iron topsail}}
{{defn|An auxiliary motor on a {{nautical term|schooner}}.}}

{{term|iron wind}}
{{defn|What sailors call inboard engines.}}

{{term|ironboat}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''oreboat'''}}
A [[Great Lakes]] term for a vessel primarily used in the transport of iron ore.}}

{{term|term=ironclad|content=[[Ironclad warship|ironclad]]}}
{{defn|A steam-propelled warship protected by iron or steel armor plates of the period from 1859 until the 1890s (when the term "ironclad" fell out of use).}}

{{term|island}}
{{defn|The [[superstructure]] of an [[aircraft carrier]] that extends above the [[flight deck]]. A carrier that lacks one is said to be {{gli|flush deck|flush-decked}}.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==J==
{{glossary}}
{{term|jack}}
{{defn|no=1|''Also '''jack tar''' or just '''tar'''''. A {{nautical term|sailor}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|([[jack (flag)]]) A national or other official flag flown on a short {{gli|jackstaff}} at the {{gli|bow}} of a vessel indicating nationality or subordination to a navy or other particular seagoing service or to a government department or subnational government (such as a state or province), or to indicate membership in a {{nautical term|yacht club}}. Typically, crew members spoke of the jack as if it were a member of the crew. A jack contrasts with an {{gli|ensign}}, which is a flag with a generally similar purpose flown from the vessel's {{nautical term|stern}}. Typically, vessels fly a jack while in port and an ensign while at sea (in daylight hours).}}
{{defn|no=3|Informally, any flag flown by a ship.}}

{{term|term=jackass-barque|content=[[jackass-barque]] {{anchor|jackass bark}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''jackass bark'''.}}
A sailing ship with three or more {{nautical term|mast|masts}}, of which the {{gli|foremast}} is {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} and the main is partially {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ({{nautical term|topsail}}, {{nautical term|topgallant}}, etc.) and partially {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} (course). The {{nautical term|mizzen mast}} is fore-and-aft-rigged.}}

{{term|jack dusty}}
{{defn|A naval stores clerk.}}

{{term|term=jack tar|content=[[jack tar]]}}
{{defn|A sailor dressed in "square rig" with square collar. Formerly with a tarred pigtail.}}

{{term|term=jackline|content=[[jackline]]}}
{{defn|On a {{nautical term|yacht}}, a deck lifeline of rope or (preferably) flat tape, running fore and aft, to which the crew can clip their harnesses for safety. Sometimes called a {{gli|jackstay}}, though this is a misnomer as a jackline is a {{gli|line}} rather than a {{nautical term|stay}}. The line must be very strong to take the weight of all crew clipped to it.}}

{{term|term=jackstaff|content=[[jackstaff]]}}
{{defn|A small vertical pole on the {{gli|bow}} of a vessel upon which is flown its flag, or {{gli|jack}}. The jackstaff was introduced in the 18th century.}}

{{term|jackstay|content=jackstay {{anchor|jack stay}}}}
{{defn|no=1|A rope, bar, or {{gli|batten}} running along a ship's {{nautical term|yard}}, to which is attached the head of a square sail.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|stay}} for racing or cruising vessels used to steady the {{nautical term|mast}} against the strain of the {{gli|gaff}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|A {{gli|cable}} between two ships or from a ship to a fixed point that supports a load during transfer of personnel or materiel along the cable.}}
{{defn|no=4|On a {{nautical term|yacht}}, a deck lifeline of rope or (preferably) flat tape may be called a jackstay, though this is a misnomer as a jackstay is a stay rather than a line.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=Jacob's ladder|content=[[Jacob's ladder (nautical)|Jacob's ladder]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''Jacobs ladder'''.}}
1.&nbsp;A flexible hanging [[ladder]] consisting of vertical ropes or chains supporting horizontal rungs, used to allow access over the side of a ship, either to transfer between the ship and another vessel alongside it or to perform maintenance tasks along the side of the ship. Sometimes mistakenly referred to as a {{nautical term|pilot ladder}}, which differs from a Jacob's ladder in its use of spreaders and in terms of specific regulations governing step size and step spacing.}}
{{defn|no=2|A vertical ladder from the [[ratlines]] found on {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ships, used to get around the {{nautical term|top}} while climbing between the lower mast and the [[topmast]].}}

{{term|jetty}}
{{defn|A man-made pier in a marina or open water, typically made of wood or rocks and rising several feet above high tide in order to create a breakwater, shelter, pr channel, to control erosion, or to perform another function.}}

{{term|term=jetsam|content=[[Flotsam and jetsam|jetsam]]}}
{{defn|Floating debris ejected from a ship. See also ''{{gli|flotsam}}''.}}

{{term|term=jib|content=[[jib]]}}
{{defn|A triangular {{gli|headsail}} at the front of a sailing vessel. The {{nautical term|tack}} is attached to the {{gli|bow}} or to a {{gli|bowsprit}}. May be the only headsail, or one of several{{snd}}in which case the jib is set forward of the fore staysail. A large jib that overlaps the {{nautical term|mainmast}} is called a {{gli|genoa}} or genny.}}

{{term|jib top}}
{{defn|A high-clewed overlapping headsail for beam reaching in medium to strong winds<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://dictionary.sailgeeks.com/word/Jib%20Top|title = Sail Geeks Dictionary}}</ref>}}

{{term|jibboom}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|spar}} used to extend the {{gli|bowsprit}}.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}

{{term|jibe}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|gybe}}.}}}}

{{term|jibe-ho}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|gybe-oh}}.}}}}

{{term|jigger-mast}}
{{defn|The fourth {{nautical term|mast}} on a ship, or the aftmost mast where it is smallest on vessels of less than four masts.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=110}}}}

{{term|joggle}}
{{defn|A slender, triangular recess cut into the [[faying]] surface of a frame or steamed {{nautical term|timber}} to fit over the land of {{gli|clinker-built|clinker}} planking, or cut into the faying edge of a plank or rebate to avoid feather ends on a {{nautical term|strake}} of planking. The feather end is cut off to produce a {{nautical term|nib}}. The joggle and nib in this case is made wide enough to allow a {{gli|caulking}} iron to enter the seam.}}

{{term|jollies}}
{{defn|Traditional Royal Navy nickname for the Royal Marines.}}

{{term|jolly boat}}
{{defn|A type of {{nautical term|ship's boat}} used to ferry crew and stores.}}

{{term|term=Jonah|content=[[Jonah#Cultural influence|Jonah]]}}
{{defn|A person (either a sailor or a passenger) who carries a jinx, one whose presence on board brings bad luck and endangers the ship.}}

{{term|Jonah's lift}}
{{defn|The throwing overboard of a man considered to be a {{gli|Jonah,}} almost always in the dark of night.}}

{{term|junk}}
{{defn|no=1|Old cordage past its useful service life as {{gli|line|lines}} aboard a ship. The strands of old junk were teased apart in a process known as "picking {{nautical term|oakum}}".}}
{{defn|no=2|A sailing ship of classic Chinese design with characteristic full {{gli|batten}} sails that span the masts usually on unstayed rigs.}}

{{term|term=jury rig|content=[[jury rig]]}}
{{defn|Both the act of {{nautical term|rigging}} a temporary {{nautical term|mast}} and/or {{nautical term|sail|sails}} and the name of the resulting rig. A jury rig would be built at sea when the original rig was damaged, and then used to sail to a harbor or other safe place for permanent repairs. Also used as a general term for a temporary repair, hence "jury rudder", "jury tiller", etc.{{sfn|Mayne|2000|p=158}}}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==K==
{{glossary}}
{{term|term=kaep|content=[[kaep]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{nautical term|proa}} native to [[Palau]].<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=q-h6i3GpMQUC&pg=PA263|page=263 |title=The Illustrated Encyclopaedia of Ships and Boats |type=Hardcover |date=November 21, 2002 |first1=Graham |last1=Blackburn |location=London |publisher=I.B.Tauris |isbn=1-86064-839-8 }}</ref>}}

{{term|term=K BO Line|content=[[K BO Line]]}}
{{defn|A line or mark on the aft end of a ship indicating the true centerline of the {{nautical term|transom}}.}}

{{term|term=kedge|content=[[Kedge anchor|kedge]] {{anchor|kedge anchor}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''kedge anchor'''.}}
A type of relatively light {{gli|anchor}}.}}

{{term|term=kedging|content=[[kedge anchor#kedging|kedging]]}}
{{defn|A technique for moving or turning a ship by using a {{gli|kedge}}. The kedge anchor may be dropped while in motion to create a pivot and thus perform a sharp turn. It may also be carried away from the ship in a smaller boat, dropped, and then weighed, pulling the ship forward.}}

{{term|term=keel|content=[[keel]]}}
{{defn|The principal central longitudinal structural member of a {{gli|hull}}, positioned at or close to the lowest point of the hull. Where the keel protrudes below the surface of the hull, it provides hydrodynamic resistance to the lateral forces that give rise to {{gli|leeway}}. A {{gli|ballast}} keel of (typically) lead or cast iron may be fastened underneath the structural keel in sailing vessels to provide stability and usually also additional hydrodynamic lift and lateral resistance effects.<ref name="Read 2014k">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014k |c= K |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/k-16172}}</ref> See also ''{{gli|bilge keel}}''.}}

{{term|keel draft}}
{{term|keel draught|multi=y}}
{{defn|Depth of water occupied by the vessel from the waterline to the underside of the keel.<ref name="PNA" /> Compare with {{nautical term|moulded draught}}. }}

{{term|term=keelhauling|content=[[keelhauling]]}}
{{defn|A type of maritime punishment by which one is dragged under the {{gli|keel}} of a ship.}}

{{term|term=keelson|content=[[keelson]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''kelson'''.}}
A baulk of timber or a steel girder immediately above the {{gli|keel}} that forms the backbone of a wooden ship. A chine keelson of more modest proportions is fitted at the junction of the floors and frames.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|kellet}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|anchor sentinel}}.}}}}

{{term|term=kentledge|content=[[kentledge]]}}
{{defn|Weights, usually [[pig iron]], used as permanent, high-density {{gli|ballast}}.}}

{{term|term=ketch|content=[[ketch]]}}
{{defn|A two-masted {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft-rigged}} sailboat with the aft mast (the {{nautical term|mizzen}}) mounted ({{nautical term|stepped}}) {{gli|afore}} the {{nautical term|rudder}}.}}

{{term|killick}}
{{defn|no=1|A small {{gli|anchor}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{nautical term|seaman}} promoted to the first step of the promotion ladder in the British Royal Navy. A fouled anchor is the substantive badge of non-commissioned officers, signifying that the wearer is an {{gli|able seaman}} skilled to cope with the awkward job of dealing with a fouled killick.}}

{{term|kicking strap}}
{{defn|no=1|A rope, tackle, or hydraulic ram running from the mast at or just above deck level to a point partway along the {{gli|boom}} of a yacht's {{nautical term|mainsail}} or {{nautical term|mizzen}}. Its function is to pull the boom down, flattening the sail in strong winds, reducing twist, and preventing the boom from kicking up when running.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|chain}} rigged from {{nautical term|rudder}} to {{nautical term|quarter}} that is tight at anchor, stopping the rudder from kicking and reducing pressure on its {{gli|gudgeon|gudgeons}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|king plank}}
{{defn|The {{gli|centerline}} plank of a laid deck. Its sides are often recessed, or nibbed, to take the ends of their parallel curved deck planks.}}

{{term|king post}}
{{defn|no=1|On an American wooden-hulled steamboat, a type of {{nautical term|mast}} or {{nautical term|stanchion}} located along the vessel's {{gli|centerline}} from which heavy chains (and later cables) were suspended to support the weight of the {{gli|hull}} and provide stiffening, in much the same manner as the cables on a [[suspension bridge]]; usually used in conjunction with a {{gli|hog frame}}.<ref>{{cite book |last= Harvey |first= Steven |date= 2007 |title= It Started with a Steamboat: An American Saga |location= Bloomington, IN |publisher= AuthorHouse |page= 64 |isbn= 9781425967192 }}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=2|On a cargo ship, a strong vertical post from which a {{gli|derrick}} or {{gli|boom}} is suspended.}}

{{term|term=Kingston valve|content=[[Kingston valve]]}}
{{defn|A type of {{nautical term|seacock}} designed so that the water pressure from the sea keeps it closed under normal operating conditions, but can be opened from the inside of the ship, allowing seawater to enter internal fuel, water, or {{gli|ballast tank|ballast tanks}}. Kingston valves can be opened to {{nautical term|scuttling|scuttle}} a ship.}}

{{term|term=kissing the gunner's daughter|content=[[Cat o' nine tails|kissing the gunner's daughter]]}}
{{defn|Bending over the barrel of a gun for punitive beating with a cane or {{gli|cat o' nine tails}}.}}

{{term|term=kitchen rudder|content=[[kitchen rudder]]}}
{{defn|A hinged cowling around a fixed {{nautical term|propeller}}, allowing the drive to be directed to the side or forwards in order to manoeuvre the vessel.}}

{{term|kite}}
{{defn|A {{nautical term|spinnaker}}.}}

{{term|knee}}
{{defn|no=1|A structural element connecting two parts roughly at right angles, e.g. deck {{gli|beam|beams}} to {{gli|frame|frames}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A vertical rubber fender used on {{nautical term|pushboat|pushboats}} or {{nautical term|pier|piers}}, sometimes shaped like a human leg bent slightly at the knee.}}

{{term|knighthead}}
{{defn|no=1|A mitred backing timber that extends the after line of the {{nautical term|rabbet}} in the {{nautical term|stem}} to give extra support to the ends of the planks and the {{gli|bowsprit}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|bollard}} or {{gli|bitt}}.}}
{{defn|no=3|Either of two timbers rising from the {{gli|keel}} of a sailing ship and supporting the inner end of the bowsprit.}}

{{term|knock}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|header}}.}}}}

{{term|knockdown}}
{{defn|The condition of a sailboat being pushed abruptly over on its side, i.e. to horizontal or "on its {{gli|beam ends}}", with the {{nautical term|mast|masts}} parallel to the water surface.}}

{{term|term=knot|content=[[Knot (unit)|knot]]}}
{{defn|A unit of speed equivalent to {{convert|1|nmi|km mi|sigfig=5}} per hour. Originally the speed of a moving vessel was measured by paying out a line from the {{nautical term|stern}}; the line was tied into a knot every {{convert|47|ft|3|in|m}}, and the number of knots paid out in 30 seconds gave the speed through the water in nautical miles per hour. Sometimes "knots" is mistakenly stated as "knots per hour", but the latter is a measure of acceleration (i.e. "nautical miles per hour per hour") rather than of speed. Both vessel speed and wind speed are commonly reported in knots.}}

{{term|know the ropes}}
{{defn|A sailor who ''knows the ropes'' can identify all the many ropes used in working a sailing vessel. On a square rigged ship, there would typically be more than 130 named ropes in the running rigging which are made fast at deck level – the majority of these are duplicated on both the port and starboard sides, so doubling that count.{{sfn|Underhill|1946|pp=280–288}} In order to know the ropes, a sailor must first ''{{gli|learn the ropes}}''. There were conventions with the positioning of all the many ropes {{gli|belay|belayed}} at deck level on a square-rigged ship, so a newly signed-on hand would quickly know where to find a particular rope on a strange ship.}}
{{glossary end}}
{{Glossary of nautical terms ToC}}

==L==
{{glossary}}
{{term|lace}}
{{defn|To attach a {{nautical term|sail}} to a {{nautical term|spar}} by passing a rope through eyelet holes and around the spar or its {{gli|jackstay}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|ladder}}
{{defn|On board a ship, all "stairs" are called ladders, except for literal staircases aboard passenger ships. Most "stairs" on a ship are extremely narrow and nearly vertical, hence the name.}}

{{term|lagan}}
{{defn|Cargo that has been thrown {{nautical term|overboard}}, sunk to the [[seabed]], and {{gli|buoy}}ed so it can be found later.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} }}

{{term|laid up}}
{{defn|To be placed in {{nautical term|reserve fleet|reserve}} or {{nautical term|mothball|mothballed}}. The latter usage in modern times refers to a specific set of procedures used by the United States Navy to preserve ships in good condition.}}

{{term|term=lake freighter|content=[[lake freighter]]}}
{{defn|A {{gli|bulk carrier}} that operates on the [[Great Lakes]] of [[North America]].}}

{{term|lakeboat}}
{{term|laker|multi=y}}
{{defn|no=1|[[Great Lakes]] slang for a vessel that spends all of her time on any of the five Great Lakes.}}
{{defn|no=2|A {{gli|lake freighter}}.}}

{{term|lakeshoring}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''lakeshoring trade'''.}}
A [[Great Lakes]] term for the general cargo and passenger trade between settlements on the Great Lakes during the 19th and early 20th centuries. Lakeshoring usually was conducted by {{nautical term|schooner|schooners}} of {{convert|50|to|60|ft}} in length, sometimes referred to as ''lakeshoring schooners''.<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=xih_ki9bLyQC&dq=lakeshoring&pg=PA47 Karamanski, Theodore J., ''Schooner Passage: Sailing Ships and the Lake Michigan Frontier''], Detroit: Wayne State University Press, 2000, {{ISBN|0-8143-2911-X}}, pp. 46, 47 Accessed July 8, 2021</ref>}}

{{term|land lubber}}
{{defn|A person unfamiliar with being on the sea or with the workings of a seafaring vessel.}}

{{term|landfall}}
{{defn|no=1|Arrival at a coastline by ship.}}
{{defn|no=2|In now-obsolete usage, the first land discovered after a sea voyage.}}

{{term|landmark}}
{{defn|An object ashore that is visible from sea and sufficiently distinct such that it is marked on nautical charts for the purpose of fixing position while at sea.}}

{{term|term=landsman|content=[[Landsman (rank)|landsman]]}}
{{defn|A military rank for a naval recruit, used in the United Kingdom in the 18th century and the first half of the 19th century and in the United States in the 19th and early 20th centuries.}}

{{term|term=langrage|content=[[langrage]]}}
{{term|langridge|multi=y}}
{{defn|no=1|Another name for {{gli|canister shot}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|Solid shot suitable for damaging rigging.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} }}

{{term|lang's lay}}
{{defn|Rope in which the lay of the strands is on the same hand as the lay of the constituents of the strands.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} }}

{{term|term=lanyard|content=[[lanyard]]}}
{{defn|A light {{nautical term|rope}} that suspends a small item to prevent loss or is used to operate something by pulling on it.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=larboard|content=[[Port (nautical)|larboard]]}}
{{defn|An obsolete term for the left side of a ship.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} Derived from "lay-board", which provided access between a ship and a {{nautical term|quay}} when ships normally docked with the left side to the wharf. Later replaced by "port side" or "{{nautical term|port}}", to avoid confusion with {{nautical term|starboard}}.}}

{{term|large}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|by and large}}.}}}}

{{term|term=lateen sail|content=[[lateen sail]] {{anchor|Latin-rig|Latin rig}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''Latin-rig'''.}}
A triangular, sometimes quadrilateral, {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} {{nautical term|sail}} set on a long {{nautical term|yard}} mounted at an angle to the {{nautical term|mast}}.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|lateral system}}
{{defn|A system of {{gli|aid to navigation|aids to navigation}} in which characteristics of buoys and beacons indicate the sides of the channel or route relative to a conventional direction of buoyage (usually upstream).}}

{{term|term=lattice mast|content=[[lattice mast]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''{{gli|cage mast}}'''.}}
A type of observation mast constructed with a [[hyperboloid structure]] using an array of thin columns at angles, crossing each other in a double-helical spiral configuration. Lattice masts were most common aboard major United States Navy warships in the early 20th century, particularly on {{gli|dreadnought}} {{gli|battleship|battleships}} and {{gli|armored cruiser|armored cruisers}}; they were largely replaced by {{nautical term|tripod mast|tripod masts}} during the 1920s and 1930s.}}

{{term|launch}}
{{defn|no=1|The largest {{nautical term|ship's boat}} carried by a warship – usually an open boat and, in more recent times, fitted with an engine. Historically, fitted both to be rowed or sailed.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=2|In modern usage, a large [[motorboat]]; e.g,. a {{gli|harbourmaster}}'s launch.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=3|An elegant power boat of traditional character with a {{gli|displacement hull}}; e.g., a {{nautical term|slipper launch}}.<ref name="Read 2014l">{{harvc |last= Read |year= 2014 |anchor-year= 2014l |c= L |in= Read |url= https://web.archive.org/web/http://www.pbo.co.uk/nautical-almanac/glossary-of-nautical-terms/l-16170}}</ref> }}
{{defn|no=4|[[Ship naming and launching|To dispatch a newly built ship]] down a {{nautical term|slipway}}, usually with ceremony, prior to {{gli|fitting-out}} and [[ship commissioning|commissioning]].{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}
{{defn|no=5|To put into the water any boat that is stored or temporarily kept out of the water; e.g., "launch the {{gli|lifeboat}}" or "launch a {{gli|dinghy}}".}}

{{term|lay}}
{{defn|no=1|To come and go, used in giving orders to the {{gli|crew}}, e.g., "lay forward" or "lay aloft", respectively indicating that the crew should move to the forward part of the ship or take up positions aloft.}}
{{defn|no=2|To direct the {{gli|course}} of a vessel.}}
{{defn|no=3|(verb) To twist the strands of a {{nautical term|rope}} together. (n) The direction of twist in cordage made from twisted strands }}
{{defn|no=4|To travel in a direction which will reach or pass just upwind of a mark, buoy, or harbor, e.g., "We will lay the mark".}}

{{term|lay day}}
{{defn|An unexpected delay time during a voyage often spent at anchor or in a harbor. It is usually caused by bad weather, equipment failure, or needed maintenance.}}

{{term|lay to}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See also '''{{gli|heave to}}'''.}}
To bring a vessel into the wind and hold her stationary. A vessel doing this is said to be ''laying to''.}}

{{term|laying down}}
{{defn|[[Keel-laying|Laying]] the {{gli|keel}} of a ship in a {{nautical term|shipyard}}, and thereby beginning her construction. The age of a ship is often indicated by giving the date it was ''laid down''.}}

{{term|term=laytime|content=[[laytime]]}}
{{defn|The amount of time stipulated in a {{nautical term|voyage charter}} for a vessel to be loaded or unloaded. If a vessel is loaded or unloaded in less than the laytime, the shipowner may be required to pay {{gli|despatch}} to the charter party. If the loading or unloading takes longer than the laytime, the charter party may be required to pay {{gli|demurrage}} to the shipowner.}}

{{term|term=lazaret|content=lazaret {{anchor|lazarette|lazaretto}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''lazarette''' or '''lazaretto'''.}}
1.&nbsp;A small stowage locker at the {{gli|aft}} end of a boat.}}
{{defn|no=2|A ship or building used for the quarantine of sick patients.}}
{{defn|no=3|An area on some merchant ships where provisions are stored.}}
{{defn|no=4|In modern shipbuilding and on powerboats of all sizes, the location of the steering gear equipment for the vessel.}}

{{term|term=lazy jacks|content=[[lazy jack]]s}}
{{term|lazyjacks|multi=y}}
{{defn|A network of {{gli|cordage}} rigged to a point on the {{nautical term|mast}} and to a series of points on either side of the {{gli|boom}} that cradles and guides the sail onto the boom when the sail is lowered.}}

{{term|lazy line}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''slime line'''.}}
A line used for stern-to mooring attached to a floating pontoon or harbor wall which leads back to a seabed mooring.<ref>{{cite web|website=Windbag the Sailor|url=https://windbagthesailor.com/2014/06/slime-line-slashers/|title=Slime-line slashers|date=June 22, 2014|access-date=April 19, 2022|archive-date=2022-05-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513003605/https://windbagthesailor.com/2014/06/slime-line-slashers/|url-status=dead}}</ref>}}

{{term|LBP|noid=true}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|length between perpendiculars}}.}}}}

{{term|leach|noid=true}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|leech}}.}}}}

{{term|lead}}
{{defn|no=1|1=A plummet or mass of lead attached to a line, used in {{nautical term|sounding}} depth at sea.}}
{{defn|no=2|1=In former usage, to estimate velocity in {{gli|knot|knots}}.{{citation needed|date=June 2022}}}}
{{defn|no=3|1=The path or route of a line (cordage).}}
{{defn|no=4|1=[[Lead (sea ice)]]: Large fracture in sea ice creating a navigable waterway.}}

{{term|term=lead ship|content=[[lead ship]]}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''class leader'''.}}
The first in a series or {{gli|class}} of ships. The lead ship is usually, but not always, the first of her class to be completed and often, but not always, the class as a whole is known by her name. In the latter case, the ''lead ship'' is also the ''{{nautical term|name ship}}'' of the class.}}

{{term|term=leadline|content=leadline {{anchor|sounding line}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''sounding line'''.}}
An instrument used in navigation to {{nautical term|sounding|measure water depth}}; the {{gli|line}} attached to a {{gli|lead}}.}}

{{term|leadsman}}
{{defn|A sailor who takes {{nautical term|sounding|soundings}} with a {{gli|lead}}, measuring the depth of the water.}}

{{term|term=league|content=[[League (unit)|league]]}}
{{defn|A unit of length used to measure distances, normally equal to three {{nautical term|nautical mile|nautical miles}}, but varies by nationality.}}

{{term|learn the ropes}}
{{defn|To be trained in the identification and proper use of the many various {{nautical term|rope|ropes}} used on a sailing ship. An apprentice sailor, especially on a {{nautical term|square rig|square-rigged}} ship, needs to know which rope of the many that are {{gli|belay|belayed}} at deck level does which job. A small square sail will have, at a minimum, two {{nautical term|sheet|sheets}}, two {{gli|clewline|clewlines}}, several {{gli|buntline|buntlines}}, and two {{gli|brace|braces}}, and may also have a {{gli|halyard}}. A single {{nautical term|mast}} may have up to five square sails. To do his job, a sailor must be able to identify each rope from all the many options – and in the dark. {{nautical term|slacking|Slacking}} or {{gli|hauling}} the wrong one may not only be inefficient but also potentially dangerous. Once proficient in these tasks, a sailor is said to "{{gli|know the ropes}}".}}

{{term|term=lee helm|content=[[lee helm]]}}
{{defn|The tendency of a sailboat to turn to {{gli|leeward}} in a strong wind when there is no change in the {{nautical term|rudder}}'s position. This is the opposite of {{nautical term|weather helm}} and is the result of a dynamically unbalanced condition. See also ''{{gli|center of lateral resistance}}''.}}

{{term|lee side}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''leeward side''' or simply '''{{gli|leeward}}'''.}}
The side of a ship that is sheltered from the wind; i.e. the side that is downwind, or in the direction toward which the wind is blowing. Contrast ''{{nautical term|weather side}}'' or ''{{nautical term|windward}}''.}}

{{term|term=lee shore|content=[[lee shore]]}}
{{defn|A shore downwind of a ship. A ship that cannot sail well to {{nautical term|windward}} risks being blown onto a lee shore and {{gli|grounded}}.}}

{{term|term=leeboard|content=[[leeboard]]}}
{{defn|A large fan-shaped wooden board or fin mounted in pairs on the side of a boat. They can be lowered on the {{gli|lee side}} of the ship to reduce {{gli|leeway}} (similarly to a {{gli|centerboard}} on a {{gli|dinghy}}).<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|leeboard irons}}
{{defn|The iron bars that run from the {{nautical term|mainmast}} case to the head of each {{gli|leeboard}}, which they support.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|leeboard pendant}}
{{defn|A wire connecting the fan of the {{gli|leeboard}} to a winch on the barges quarter. They control the fall of the leeboard.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|leech|content=leech {{anchor|leach}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''leach'''.}}
The {{gli|aft}} or trailing edge of a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} sail, the {{gli|leeward}} edge of a {{nautical term|spinnaker}}, or a vertical edge of a square sail. The leech is susceptible to twist, which is controlled by the {{gli|boom vang}}, {{nautical term|mainsheet}}, and, if rigged with one, the {{gli|gaff vang}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=lee-oh|content=lee-oh {{anchor|hard alee|hard-a-lee}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''hard alee'''.}}
A command to {{gli|come about}} ({{nautical term|tack}} through the wind) on a sailing boat. The response by the helmsman to indicate the order has been carried out, is "helm's alee"}}

{{term|term=leeward|content=[[Windward and leeward|leeward]]}}
{{defn|(pronounced {{IPAc-en|ˈ|lj|uː|ər|d}} in nautical use) In the direction toward which the wind is blowing. Contrast ''{{nautical term|windward}}''.}}

{{term|term=leeway|content=[[leeway]]}}
{{defn|The amount that a ship is blown {{gli|leeward}} by the wind. Also the amount of open free sailing space available to the {{gli|lee side}} of a vessel before encountering hazards. See also ''{{nautical term|weatherly}}''.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|leg}}
{{defn|In navigation, a segment of a {{nautical term|voyage}} between two waypoints.}}

{{term|term=length between perpendiculars|content=[[length between perpendiculars]] {{anchor|p/p|p.p.|pp|LPP|LBP|Length BPP}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''p/p''', '''p.p.''', '''pp''', '''LPP''', '''LBP''', or '''Length BPP'''.}}
The length of a vessel along the {{nautical term|waterline}} from the forward surface of the {{nautical term|stem}} or main {{gli|bow}} perpendicular member, to the after surface of the {{nautical term|sternpost}} or main {{nautical term|stern}} perpendicular member. The measure generally allows for a reasonable estimate of the vessel's carrying capacity, as it excludes the small, often unusable volume contained in her overhanging ends.}}

{{term|term=length overall|content=[[length overall]] (LOA){{anchor|LOA}}}}
{{defn|The maximum length of a vessel's {{gli|hull}} measured parallel to the {{nautical term|waterline}}, usually measured on the hull alone, and including overhanging ends that extend beyond the main {{gli|bow}} and main {{nautical term|stern}} perpendicular members. For sailing vessels, this may exclude the {{gli|bowsprit}} and other fittings added to the hull, but sometimes bowsprits are included.}}

{{term|let go and haul}}
{{defn|An order indicating that the ship is now on the desired course relative to the wind and that the sails should be trimmed ({{gli|haul|hauled}}) to suit.}}

{{term|letter of marque|content=[[letter of marque]]}}
{{term|letter of marque and reprisal|multi=y}}
{{defn|An official warrant granted to a {{nautical term|privateer}} condoning specific acts of {{nautical term|piracy}} against a specific target as a redress for grievances.}}

{{term|liberty}}
{{defn|A relatively short period when a sailor is allowed ashore for recreation. See also ''{{nautical term|shore leave}}''.}}

{{term|licensed ship}}
{{defn|A term used by the British [[East India Company]] from the 17th to the 19th centuries for merchant ships not under {{gli|charter}} to it which it nevertheless permitted under a license issued by the company to trade between England (later the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|United Kingdom]]) and ports east of the [[Cape of Good Hope]], a trade over which the company otherwise held a strict monopoly. The company placed strict controls on what ports a licensed ship could visit and what kinds of trade it could engage in. A licensed ship that violated these rules became an {{gli|interloper}} and faced harsh penalties if caught.<ref name=threedecksshiproles/>}}

{{term|lie to}}
{{defn|To arrange a ship's {{nautical term|sail|sails}} so that they counteract each other. A ship in this condition or in the process of achieving this condition is said to be ''lying to''.}}

{{term|term=lifebelt|content=[[Personal flotation device|lifebelt]] {{anchor|lifejacket|life preserver|lifebuoy|personal flotation device|PFD}}}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''lifebuoy''', '''lifejacket''', '''life preserver''', and '''personal flotation device (PFD)'''.}}
A portable or wearable device such as a buoyant ring or inflatable jacket designed to keep a person {{gli|afloat}} in the water.}}

{{term|lifeboat}}
{{defn|no=1|([[Lifeboat (shipboard)|shipboard lifeboat]]) A small boat kept on board a vessel and used to take crew and passengers to safety in the event of the ship being abandoned.}}
{{defn|no=2|([[Lifeboat (rescue)|rescue lifeboat]]) A small boat usually launched from shore and used to rescue people from the water or from vessels in difficulty.}}

{{term|term=liferaft|content=[[Lifeboat (shipboard)|liferaft]]}}
{{defn|An inflatable, sometimes covered raft used in the event of a vessel being abandoned or in the evacuation of an aircraft after a water landing.}}

{{term|lift}}
{{defn|no=1|A rope that supports a spar on a sailing vessel. Examples include the [[topping lift]] on the boom of a fore and aft rigged sail, or the lifts on the yard of a square rigged sail, which can adjust the yard to the horizontal or cock-bill the yard to get it out of the way when unloading cargo or alongside another vessel.{{Sfn|Mayne|2000}}}}
{{defn|no=2|An enabling shift in the direction of the wind that allows a {{gli|close-hauled}} sailing ship to {{nautical term|point up}} from its current {{gli|course}} to a more favorable one. This is the opposite of a {{gli|header}}.}}

{{term|light irons}}
{{defn|Iron bars mounted near the main {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}} that support the navigation lights.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|light screens}}
{{defn|Boards on which the navigation lights are hooked and which shield the direction that the red or green light shows.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=lighter|content=[[Lighter (barge)|lighter]]}}
{{defn|A flat-bottomed {{gli|barge}} used to transfer goods and passengers to and from moored ships, traditionally unpowered and moved and steered using "sweeps" (long {{nautical term|oar|oars}}), with their motive power provided by water currents.}}

{{term|term=lightering|content=[[lightering]]}}
{{defn|The process of transferring cargo from one vessel to another in order to reduce the {{gli|draft}} of the first vessel, typically done to allow a vessel to enter a {{nautical term|port}} with limited depth or to help free a {{gli|grounded}} vessel.}}

{{term|lightship}}
{{term|term=lightvessel|content=[[lightvessel]]|multi=y}}
{{defn|A permanently anchored vessel performing the functions of a {{gli|lighthouse}}, typically in a location where construction of the latter is impractical. These have largely been replaced by {{gli|buoy|buoys}} or, as construction techniques have improved, actual lighthouses.}}

{{term|limber board}}
{{defn|A part of the {{gli|ceiling}} alongside the {{gli|keelson}}, easily removable for cleaning out the {{gli|limber hole|limber holes}}.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|limber hole}}
{{defn|A channel cut in the underside of a {{gli|frame}}, close to the {{gli|keel}}, to allow {{gli|bilge}} water to drain away to the pump well, rather than being trapped between each set of frames.{{sfn|Steffy|2013}}}}

{{term|limber strake}}
{{defn|In traditional timber construction, the lowest permanently fastened {{nautical term|strake}} of {{gli|ceiling}}, positioned close to the {{gli|keelson}}. It performs a structural role, usually binding together each pair of {{gli|floor}} and first {{gli|futtock}}.{{r|Adams 2013|at=glossary}}}}

{{term|line}}
{{defn|The correct nautical term for the majority of the {{gli|cordage}} or "ropes" used on a vessel. An individual line will always have a more specific name (e.g,. the {{nautical term|mizzen}} {{nautical term|topsail}} {{gli|halyard}}) that specifies its use.}}

{{term|lines}}
{{term|hull lines|multi=y}}
{{term|lines drawing|multi=y}}
{{defn|no=1|The depiction of the shape of a {{gli|hull}} with three views: sheer plan, body plan and half breadth plan. The lines on these drawings denote the shape of the hull similarly to the contours of a map.{{sfn|Steffy|1994|p=15-19, 274}}}}
{{defn|no=2|A general term for the shape of a hull.}}
{{defn|no=3|''See {{Nautical term|fine lines}}''.}}

{{term|line astern}}
{{defn|In naval warfare, a {{gli|line of battle}} formed behind a {{gli|flagship}}.}}

{{term|liner}}
{{defn|no=1|During the [[Age of Sail]], a {{nautical term|ship-of-the-line}}, or a major {{nautical term|warship}} capable of taking its place in the main {{gli|line of battle|battle line}} of fighting ships.}}
{{defn|no=2|Any cargo or passenger ship running scheduled service along a specific route with published {{nautical term|port of call|ports of call}}, excluding [[Ferryboat|ferries]] and other vessels engaged in short-sea trading. When referring to {{gli|cargo ship|cargo ships}}, "liner" contrasts with "{{nautical term|tramp}}", which refers to a ship engaged in spot-market trade that does not follow a regular schedule or make regular calls at specific ports. When referring to passenger ships, "{{nautical term|ocean liner}}" refers to ships providing scheduled transportation between regular ports of call, but excludes {{gli|cruise ship|cruise ships}}, which voyage for recreational purposes and not primarily as a form of transportation between ports.}}

{{term|term=list|content=[[Angle of list|list]]}}
{{defn|
[[File:Ivory Tirupati with heavy list 3.jpg|thumb|right|A ship with severe '''{{gli|list}}''']]
The degree or angle to which a vessel leans or tilts to one side, on the {{nautical term|roll}} axis, at equilibrium, i.e. with no external forces acting upon it. The term typically refers to a lean caused by flooding or improperly loaded or shifted cargo, as opposed to {{gli|heeling}}, which is a consequence of external forces. A vessel with such a lean is said to be ''listing''.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}} Compare ''{{gli|loll}}''.}}

{{term|lizard}}
{{defn|A short length of {{nautical term|rope}} with an eye, used to hold another rope in position.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|LOA|noid=y}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|length overall}}.}}}}

{{term|loaded to the gunwales}}
{{defn|Literally, having cargo loaded as high as the ship's rail. The term is also used as an idiom meaning "extremely drunk".}}

{{term|term=lofting|content=[[lofting]]}}
{{defn|In boat construction, a drafting technique used to convert a scaled drawing to full size.}}

{{term|loggerhead}}
{{defn|no=1|A bollard mounted in the {{nautical term|sternsheets}} of a [[whaleboat]] for snubbing the whale line as a harpooned whale swam away from the boat.<ref name="Ansel 78">{{cite book |last1=Ansel |first1=Willits Dyer |title=The Whaleboat : a study of design, construction, and use from 1850 to 1970 |date=1978 |publisher=Mystic Seaport Museum |location=[Mystic, Conn.] |isbn=0-913372-39-0}}</ref>{{rp|144}}}}
{{defn|no=2|An iron ball attached to a long handle, used for driving caulking into seams and (occasionally) in a fight; hence the expression "at loggerheads".{{citation needed|date=June 2022}} }}

{{term|1=loll}}
{{term|1=[[Angle of loll|lolling]]|multi=y}}
{{defn|1=A {{gli|list}} to either side caused by inadequate transverse stability in the upright condition.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|term=Long Forties|content=[[Long Forties]]}}
{{defn|An area of the northern [[North Sea]] which is fairly consistently 40 {{gli|fathom|fathoms}} (240 feet; 73 metres) deep. On nautical charts with depths indicated in fathoms, it appears as a long area with many "40" notations.}}

{{term|long stay}}
{{defn|The relative slackness of an {{gli|anchor chain}}; this term means taut and extended.}}

{{term|term=longboat|content=[[longboat]]}}
{{defn|no=1|In the [[Age of Sail]], a double-banked open boat carried by a sailing ship, rowed by eight or ten oarsmen, two per {{nautical term|thwart}}, although designed also to be rigged for sailing; more seaworthy than a {{gli|cutter}} or {{gli|dinghy}} and with a {{gli|beam}} greater than that of a {{gli|gig}}. Eventually supplanted by the [[whaleboat]].}}
{{defn|no=2|The largest, and thus the most capable, of boats carried on a ship.}}
{{defn|no=3|[[Great Lakes]] slang for a vessel that spends all of her time on any of the five [[Great Lakes]], referring to the slender appearance of such vessels.}}

{{term|longliner}}
{{defn|A fishing vessel rigged for [[longline fishing]] ("longlining").}}

{{term|term=longship|content=[[longship]]}}
{{defn|A type of ship invented and used by the Vikings for trade, commerce, exploration, and warfare, evolving over several centuries and appearing in its complete form between the 9th and 13th centuries.}}

{{term|lookout}}
{{defn|A member of the {{gli|crew}} specifically assigned to watch surrounding waters for other vessels, land, objects in the water, hazards, threats, etc. Lookouts usually have duty stations high on a vessel's superstructure, in a specially designed {{nautical term|top}} or {{gli|crow's nest}}, or in her {{nautical term|rigging}}, in order to enhance their field of view.}}

{{term|loose cannon}}
{{defn|An irresponsible and reckless individual whose behavior (either intentionally or unintentionally) endangers the group he or she belongs to. The term refers to a hypothetical literal loose cannon which, weighing thousands of pounds, would crush anything and anyone in its path, and possibly even break a hole in the hull, thus endangering the seaworthiness of the whole ship.}}

{{term|loose-footed}}
{{defn|A fore-and-aft{{nautical term|mainsail}} that is not connected to a {{gli|boom}} along its foot.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|term=lorcha|content=[[Lorcha (boat)|lorcha]]}}
{{defn|A sailing vessel of 30 to 150 [[Builder's Old Measurement|tons burthen]] developed around 1550 that has a {{gli|junk}} {{nautical term|rigging|rig}} with Cantonese or other Chinese-style {{gli|batten}} {{nautical term|sail}}s on a Portuguese or other European-style {{gli|hull}}. The design combines the ease of handling of a junk rig with the greater speed, cargo capacity, and ease of repair of the European-style hull.}}

{{term|lower deck}}
{{defn|no=1|The {{gli|deck}} of a ship immediately above the {{gli|hold}}.}}
{{defn|no=2|In British usage, those members of a {{nautical term|ship's company}} who are not officers, often used in the plural (e.g., "the lower decks").}}

{{term|lowers}}
{{defn|The lower {{gli|brail|brails}} on the {{nautical term|mainsail}}.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|lubber's hole}}
{{defn|A port cut into the bottom of a {{nautical term|masthead}} or {{nautical term|top}} ({{gli|crow's-nest}}) allowing easy entry and exit. It was considered "un-seamanlike" to use this method rather than going over the side from the {{nautical term|shroud|shrouds}}, and few sailors would risk the scorn of their shipmates by doing so (at least if there were witnesses). In practice, it is often actually quicker and easier for a fit sailor to climb outside the masthead than through the lubber's hole.{{sfn|Underhill|1955|p=111}}}}

{{term|lubber's line}}
{{defn|A line or mark inside or on a compass case or binnacle indicating the direction of the ship's head.{{sfn|Palmer|1975}}}}

{{term|lucky bag}}
{{defn|no=1|A locker or compartment for storage of unclaimed articles.<ref>{{cite web|website=www.merriam-webster.com|title=Merriam-Webster: "Lucky Bag"|url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/lucky%20bag|access-date=March 22, 2022}}</ref>}}
{{defn|no=2|[[United States Naval Academy|US Naval Academy]] yearbook.}}

{{term|term=luff|content=[[Luff (sail)|luff]]}}
{{defn|no=1|The {{gli|forward}} edge of a sail.<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}
{{defn|no=2|The fullest or roundest part of a ship's {{gli|bow}}.{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=60}}<ref name="smyth" />}}
{{defn|no=3|To point a sailing vessel closer to the wind.{{sfnp|Hamersly|1884|p= [https://archive.org/details/navalencyclopedia00hamerich/page/451/ 451]}}<ref name="Thames Sailing Barge glossary"/>}}

{{term|luff and touch her}}
{{defn|To bring a vessel so close to the wind that the sails shake.{{sfnp|Hamersly|1884|p= [https://archive.org/details/navalencyclopedia00hamerich/page/451/ 451]|loc= "Luff"}} }}

{{term|luff barge}}
{{defn|{{ghat|Also '''paddy boat'''.}}
An 18th-century term for a sailing barge with a rounded {{gli|bow}} and not a {{nautical term|swim-head}}.{{sfn|Carr|1951|p=59}}}}

{{term|luff perpendicular (LP)}}
{{defn|The shortest distance between the {{gli|clew}} and the {{gli|luff}}, which is a perpendicular line from the luff to the clew. Commonly given as a percentage of the "J" measurement.<ref name="Uksailmakers">{{cite web |title=Encyclopedia – 4.2 -Genoas and Other Jibs |url=http://www.uksailmakers.com/encyclopedia/4-2-genoas-and-other-jibs/ |website=Uksailmakers |publisher=UK Sailmakers International |access-date=11 August 2018}}</ref>}}

{{term|luff up}}
{{defn|To steer a sailing vessel more towards the direction of the wind until the pressure is eased on the {{nautical term|sheet}}.}}

{{term|term=luffing|content=[[luffing]]}}
{{defn|no=1|(of a sailing vessel) Being steered far enough to {{nautical term|windward}} that the sail is no longer completely filled with wind; in such a state, the {{gli|luff}} of a {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} sail begins to flap first.}}
{{defn|no=2|Loosening a {{nautical term|sheet}} so far past optimal trim that the sail no longer completely fills with wind.}}
{{defn|no=3|The flapping of a sail from having no wind at all.{{citation needed|date=July 2022}} }}

{{term|term=lumber hooker|content=[[lumber hooker]]}}
{{defn|A [[Great Lakes]] ship designed to simultaneously carry her own deck load of lumber and to tow one or two {{gli|barge|barges}}. The barges were big old {{nautical term|schooner|schooners}} stripped of their {{nautical term|mast|masts}} and running gear to carry large cargoes of lumber.}}

{{term|term=lugger|content=[[lugger]]}}
{{defn|A sailing vessel with {{gli|lug sail|lug sails}} set on one, two, or more {{nautical term|mast|masts}} and perhaps lug {{nautical term|topsail|topsails}}, widely used as traditional fishing boats, particularly off the coasts of France, England, and Scotland; also used as privateers and smugglers.}}

{{term|term=lug sail|content=[[lug sail]]}}
{{defn|A four-sided {{gli|fore-and-aft rig|fore-and-aft}} {{nautical term|sail}} supported by a {{nautical term|spar}} along the top that is fixed to the {{nautical term|mast}} at a point some distance from the center of the spar. A dipping lug had to be moved to the other side of the mast when {{nautical term|tacking}} (in larger vessels, by partially lowering the sail and hauling down either the peak or the throat to move the {{nautical term|yard}} across). A standing lug can be used on either tack in the same position. It was common for British fishing luggers to have a dipping lug on the {{gli|foremast}} and a standing lug on the {{nautical term|mizzen}}.{{sfn|March|1972|p=''passim''}}}}

{{term|term=lying ahull|content=[[lying ahull]]}}
{{defn|Waiting out a storm by {{gli|dousing}} all {{nautical term|sail|sails}} and simply letting the boat drift.}}

{{term|lying to}}
{{defn|{{ghat|See {{gli|lie to}}.}}}}
{{glossary end}}
